window.POP_REGISTER_CONTENT("1timothy", {"intro":{"type":"masterIntroduction","book":"1 Timothy","chapters":6,"bigPicture":{"subtitle":"How to Behave in the Household of the Living God","paragraphs":["Picture a young pastor left in charge of a large, troubled church in one of the greatest cities of the Roman world. Ephesus had a famous temple to the goddess Artemis, a theatre that held twenty-five thousand people, and a flourishing trade in magic and religion. Paul had spent nearly three years there, and the gospel had spread through the whole province of Asia. But Paul had also warned the Ephesian elders that after he left, “vicious wolves will enter in among you” (Acts 20:29) and that “Men will arise from among your own selves, speaking perverse things, to draw away the disciples after them” (Acts 20:30). Now it had happened. And the young man Paul left behind to deal with it was Timothy.","Timothy was no stranger to this letter's readers. He had grown up in Lystra with a believing Jewish mother and grandmother and a Greek father. He had travelled with Paul for years, helped write both letters to the Thessalonians, and been sent on difficult missions to Thessalonica, Corinth and Philippi. Paul trusted him more than anyone: “For I have no one else like-minded, who will truly care about you” (Philippians 2:20). Yet Timothy seems to have been young for his role, perhaps timid, and often unwell. Paul writes to strengthen him: “Let no man despise your youth; but be an example to those who believe” (4:12).","1 Timothy is the first of the three ‘pastoral letters’ (with 2 Timothy and Titus). Its purpose is stated in 3:14–15: “These things I write to you, hoping to come to you shortly; but if I wait long, that you may know how men ought to behave themselves in God’s house, which is the assembly of the living God, the pillar and ground of the truth.” The church is God's household, and households need order: prayer, leaders of proven character, care for widows, honour for elders, right attitudes to money, and above all faithful teaching.","But 1 Timothy is not a dry church manual. At its heart is the gospel, stated with burning clarity: “Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners; of whom I am chief” (1:15). Paul never forgot that he had been a blasphemer and persecutor who received mercy. And the letter rises again and again into worship — “Now to the King eternal, immortal, invisible” (1:17), “the mystery of godliness is great” (3:16). Order in God's house flows from the grace and glory of God's Son.","The key word of the letter is εὐσέβεια, ‘godliness’ — real devotion to God expressed in a life that reflects Him. The false teachers turned religion into speculation, controversy and a way to make money. Paul calls Timothy back to godliness rooted in the truth: “But godliness with contentment is great gain” (6:6). That is the call of 1 Timothy to you: guard the truth, live in godliness, love with a pure heart, and fight the good fight of faith."]},"bridge":{"fromBook":"2 Thessalonians","intro":"The Holy Spirit placed 1 Timothy directly after 2 Thessalonians. 2 Thessalonians called a shaken church to hold fast to the teaching it had received. 1 Timothy shows how the household of God is ordered to guard that teaching — and it is written to Timothy, who helped write both Thessalonian letters.","points":[{"title":"From Letters to Churches to Letters to Pastors","body":"2 Thessalonians was the last of Paul's letters to churches in our New Testament order. 1 Timothy begins the letters to individual church leaders. The concerns overlap closely — false teaching, holding to apostolic tradition, discipline, work and community care — but now Paul speaks directly to the person responsible. Timothy, who stood beside Paul when he wrote “stand firm, and hold the traditions which you were taught by us” (2 Thessalonians 2:15), is now the one who must make sure a church does so."},{"title":"From Deception About the End to Deception Now","body":"In 2 Thessalonians the danger was a false claim about the day of the Lord, and Paul warned of a coming rebellion. In 1 Timothy the danger is false teaching already active inside the church: myths, endless genealogies, speculative law-teaching, and later ascetic rules forbidding marriage and foods. Paul says plainly, “the Spirit says expressly that in later times some will fall away from the faith” (4:1). The ‘mystery of lawlessness’ that was ‘already at work’ (2 Thessalonians 2:7) shows its face in the local church."},{"title":"From Idle Hands to Godly Contentment","body":"2 Thessalonians confronted believers who would not work: “If anyone will not work, don’t let him eat” (2 Thessalonians 3:10). 1 Timothy addresses the other side of the money problem: teachers who see godliness as a way to get rich, and believers tempted by the love of money. The answer in both letters is a quiet, faithful, generous life. Here it is summed up: “But godliness with contentment is great gain” (6:6)."},{"title":"From Love Directed to Love as the Goal","body":"2 Thessalonians prayed, “May the Lord direct your hearts into God’s love” (2 Thessalonians 3:5). 1 Timothy makes that love the very purpose of all Christian teaching: “but the goal of this command is love, out of a pure heart and a good conscience and sincere faith” (1:5). Sound doctrine is never an end in itself. It exists to produce love."}]},"coreTheme":{"title":"The Household of God: Pillar and Ground of the Truth","anchorRef":"1 Timothy 3:15–16","paragraphs":["The centre of 1 Timothy is 3:15–16. Paul writes so “that you may know how men ought to behave themselves in God’s house, which is the assembly of the living God, the pillar and ground of the truth” (3:15). Three pictures in one sentence: the church is a household (God's family, with order and responsibilities), an assembly (the gathered people of the living God), and a pillar and foundation (holding up and displaying the truth before the world).","Then Paul tells us what that truth is: “Without controversy, the mystery of godliness is great: God was revealed in the flesh, justified in the spirit, seen by angels, preached among the nations, believed on in the world, and received up in glory” (3:16). This is probably an early Christian hymn. The truth the church holds up is not a set of rules but a Person: Jesus Christ, God in the flesh, vindicated, proclaimed, believed and glorified.","Everything else in the letter flows from this. Because the church holds the truth, it must silence false teachers (chapter 1) and pray for all people, since God wants all to be saved (chapter 2). Because it is a household, it needs leaders of tested character (chapter 3), a pastor who is an example (chapter 4), and wise care for widows, elders and slaves (chapter 5). Because it lives for godliness, not gain, it must resist the love of money (chapter 6).","For you, this means that how you live in the church is never a private matter. The local church is God's house. Your prayer, your character, your speech, your generosity and your faithfulness are part of how the truth of Christ is held up before a watching world."]},"truths":{"intro":"As we journey through these 6 chapters, the Holy Spirit wants to anchor these truths in your spirit:","items":[{"title":"The Gospel Is for the Worst of Sinners","body":"Paul never moved past the wonder of his own salvation: “The saying is faithful and worthy of all acceptance, that Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners; of whom I am chief” (1:15). He had been “a blasphemer, a persecutor, and insolent” (1:13), yet he received mercy as an example of Christ's patience. If Christ could save Paul, He can save anyone. Sound doctrine begins with this grace."},{"title":"There Is One God and One Mediator","body":"In a city full of gods and go-betweens, Paul declares: “For there is one God, and one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus” (2:5). No priest, saint, angel or spiritual technique stands between you and God. Jesus alone is the bridge, and He “gave himself as a ransom for all” (2:6). That is why the church prays for all people."},{"title":"Godliness Is Great Gain","body":"False teachers treated religion as a path to profit. Paul turns that upside down: “But godliness with contentment is great gain” (6:6). Real wealth is a life devoted to God, satisfied with what He provides, and generous to others. The love of money leads many to wander from the faith; contentment and generosity lay up treasure that lasts."}]},"preparation":{"intro":"Before we step into Chapter 1, prepare your spirit with these reflections:","items":[{"title":"What Is Your Teaching Producing?","body":"Paul says the goal of all instruction is “love, out of a pure heart and a good conscience and sincere faith” (1:5). Think about the teaching you listen to and the way you share your faith. Is it producing love, or arguments, pride and speculation? Ask God to make His truth bear the fruit of love in you."},{"title":"Your Place in God's House","body":"1 Timothy describes the church as God's household. Where is your place in it? Are you praying, serving, supporting leaders, caring for the vulnerable, and living a life that holds up the truth? Or are you a spectator? Ask the Spirit to show you your part in the household of the living God."}]},"prayerOfActivation":"“Father, thank You that Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners, and that He saved me. Like Paul, I have received mercy I did not deserve. As I study this letter, teach me how to behave in Your house, the assembly of the living God, the pillar and foundation of the truth. Guard me from myths, speculation and teaching that produces arguments instead of love. Let the goal of everything I learn be love from a pure heart, a good conscience and sincere faith. Make me a person of prayer for all people, because You want all to be saved and come to the knowledge of the truth. Thank You for the one Mediator, the man Christ Jesus. Make me godly and content, free from the love of money, and generous. Whatever my age or weakness, make me an example to others in word, conduct, love, faith and purity. Help me to fight the good fight of faith and take hold of eternal life. To the King eternal, immortal, invisible, the only God, be honour and glory for ever and ever. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”"},"chapters":[{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Timothy","chapter":1,"chapterTitle":"Love From a Pure Heart — Sound Teaching and the Mercy Shown to the Chief of Sinners","themeTitle":"“Christ Jesus Came Into the World to Save Sinners; of Whom I Am Chief”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ according to the commandment of God our Savior and the Lord Jesus Christ our hope;","understanding":"Paul, ἀπόστολος, an apostle, κατ᾽ ἐπιταγήν, “according to the commandment” — a royal decree, an order from the throne — of God our σωτήρ, “Savior.” Calling God the Father ‘Savior’ is distinctive of the pastoral letters (six times). And Christ Jesus is ἡ ἐλπὶς ἡμῶν, “our hope.” Paul's authority does not come from his own ambition but from God's command; Timothy can lean on it when false teachers challenge him.","words":[{"strongs":"G652","hebrew":"ἀπόστολος","translit":"apóstolos","english":"apostle"},{"strongs":"G2003","hebrew":"ἐπιταγή","translit":"epitagḗ","english":"commandment"},{"strongs":"G4990","hebrew":"σωτήρ","translit":"sōtḗr","english":"Savior"},{"strongs":"G1680","hebrew":"ἐλπίς","translit":"elpís","english":"hope"}]},{"v":2,"text":"to Timothy, my true child in faith: Grace, mercy, and peace, from God our Father and Christ Jesus our Lord.","understanding":"To Timothy, γνησίῳ τέκνῳ ἐν πίστει, “my true child in faith” — γνήσιος means legitimate, genuine-born. Paul probably led Timothy to Christ on his first visit to Lystra (Acts 14), and Timothy proved to be a true son in character. Then a greeting found only in the letters to Timothy (and 2 John): χάρις, ἔλεος, εἰρήνη, “Grace, mercy, and peace.” Mercy is added for a young man facing a hard task: God's compassion for the one who feels weak.","words":[{"strongs":"G1103","hebrew":"γνήσιος","translit":"gnḗsios","english":"true"},{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"child"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"Grace"},{"strongs":"G1656","hebrew":"ἔλεος","translit":"éleos","english":"mercy"},{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"}]},{"v":3,"text":"As I urged you when I was going into Macedonia, stay at Ephesus that you might command certain men not to teach a different doctrine,","understanding":"Paul παρεκάλεσα, “urged” Timothy προσμεῖναι, “to stay,” at Ἔφεσος, Ephesus, while Paul went on to Μακεδονία, Macedonia. His task: παραγγείλῃς, “command” — the military word for passing on orders — certain men μὴ ἑτεροδιδασκαλεῖν, “not to teach a different doctrine.” The word contains ἕτερος from the ‘another’ family: another of a different kind. Their teaching was not just a variation in style; it was different in substance from the apostolic gospel (compare Galatians 1:6, ‘a different good news’).","words":[{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"urged"},{"strongs":"G4357","hebrew":"προσμένω","translit":"prosménō","english":"stay"},{"strongs":"G2181","hebrew":"Ἔφεσος","translit":"Éphesos","english":"Ephesus"},{"strongs":"G3109","hebrew":"Μακεδονία","translit":"Makedonía","english":"Macedonia"},{"strongs":"G3853","hebrew":"παραγγέλλω","translit":"parangéllō","english":"command"},{"strongs":"G2085","hebrew":"ἑτεροδιδασκαλέω","translit":"heterodidaskaléō","english":"teach a different doctrine"}]},{"v":4,"text":"and not to pay attention to myths and endless genealogies, which cause disputes, rather than God’s stewardship, which is in faith—","understanding":"What were they teaching? μύθοις, “myths” — fables, speculative stories — and γενεαλογίαις ἀπεράντοις, “endless genealogies” — probably fanciful interpretations built on the Old Testament family lists. Such things παρέχουσιν ζητήσεις, “cause disputes,” questions and arguments, “rather than God’s stewardship (οἰκονομίαν), which is in faith.” οἰκονομία is household management: God's plan of salvation, administered by faith. Speculation feeds arguments; God's plan feeds faith.","words":[{"strongs":"G4337","hebrew":"προσέχω","translit":"proséchō","english":"pay attention"},{"strongs":"G3454","hebrew":"μῦθος","translit":"mŷthos","english":"myths"},{"strongs":"G1076","hebrew":"γενεαλογία","translit":"genealogía","english":"genealogies"},{"strongs":"G562","hebrew":"ἀπέραντος","translit":"apérantos","english":"endless"},{"strongs":"G2214","hebrew":"ζήτησις","translit":"zḗtēsis","english":"disputes"},{"strongs":"G3622","hebrew":"οἰκονομία","translit":"oikonomía","english":"stewardship"}]},{"v":5,"text":"but the goal of this command is love, out of a pure heart and a good conscience and sincere faith;","understanding":"The purpose statement of the whole letter: τὸ δὲ τέλος τῆς παραγγελίας ἐστὶν ἀγάπη, “but the goal of this command is love.” τέλος is the target, the finish line. Love — the love family's ἀγάπη, self-giving love — has three sources: ἐκ καθαρᾶς καρδίας, “out of a pure heart” (clean motives), συνειδήσεως ἀγαθῆς, “a good conscience” (ἀγαθός from the good family: a conscience that does what is right), and πίστεως ἀνυποκρίτου, “sincere faith” — literally ‘unhypocritical’, faith without a mask. Teaching that does not produce love has missed its target.","words":[{"strongs":"G5056","hebrew":"τέλος","translit":"télos","english":"goal"},{"strongs":"G3852","hebrew":"παραγγελία","translit":"parangelía","english":"command"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G2513","hebrew":"καθαρός","translit":"katharós","english":"pure"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"heart"},{"strongs":"G18","hebrew":"ἀγαθός","translit":"agathós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G4893","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","english":"conscience"},{"strongs":"G505","hebrew":"ἀνυπόκριτος","translit":"anypókritos","english":"sincere"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"}]},{"v":6,"text":"from which things some, having missed the mark, have turned away to vain talking;","understanding":"Some ἀστοχήσαντες, “having missed the mark” — like an archer whose arrow flies wide of the target (compare ἁμαρτία, sin, which also pictures missing the mark) — ἐξετράπησαν, “have turned away,” swerved off course, εἰς ματαιολογίαν, “to vain talking” — empty chatter, words without substance. Teaching that misses love ends up as noise.","words":[{"strongs":"G795","hebrew":"ἀστοχέω","translit":"astochéō","english":"missed the mark"},{"strongs":"G1624","hebrew":"ἐκτρέπω","translit":"ektrépō","english":"turned away"},{"strongs":"G3150","hebrew":"ματαιολογία","translit":"mataiología","english":"vain talking"}]},{"v":7,"text":"desiring to be teachers of the law, though they understand neither what they say, nor about what they strongly affirm.","understanding":"They wanted to be νομοδιδάσκαλοι, “teachers of the law” — experts in the Law of Moses — “though they understand (νοοῦντες) neither what they say, nor about what they strongly affirm (διαβεβαιοῦνται).” Confident speech is not the same as understanding. They spoke with great assurance about things they did not grasp.","words":[{"strongs":"G3547","hebrew":"νομοδιδάσκαλος","translit":"nomodidáskalos","english":"teachers of the law"},{"strongs":"G3539","hebrew":"νοιέω","translit":"noiéō","english":"understand"},{"strongs":"G1226","hebrew":"διαβεβαιόομαι","translit":"diabebaióomai","english":"strongly affirm"}]},{"v":8,"text":"But we know that the law is good, if a person uses it lawfully,","understanding":"“But we know (οἴδαμεν) that the law (νόμος) is good (καλός)” — καλός from the good family: noble, excellent, beautiful in its design. Paul is not against the Law; he is against its misuse. It is good ἐάν τις αὐτῷ νομίμως χρῆται, “if a person uses it lawfully” — a wordplay: use the law ‘law-fully’, according to its real purpose.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G3545","hebrew":"νομίμως","translit":"nomímōs","english":"lawfully"}]},{"v":9,"text":"as knowing this, that law is not made for a righteous person, but for the lawless and insubordinate, for the ungodly and sinners, for the unholy and profane, for murderers of fathers and murderers of mothers, for manslayers,","understanding":"The law's purpose: it is not laid down for a δικαίῳ, “righteous person,” but for those who break it. The list follows the order of the Ten Commandments. First, sins against God: ἀνόμοις, “the lawless” (from the sin family: living as if there were no law), ἀνυποτάκτοις, “insubordinate,” ἀσεβέσι, “ungodly” (the opposite of εὐσέβεια, the key word of the letter), ἁμαρτωλοῖς, “sinners,” ἀνοσίοις, “unholy,” βεβήλοις, “profane.” Then sins against parents and life: πατρολῴαις, “murderers of fathers,” μητρολῴαις, “murderers of mothers,” ἀνδροφόνοις, “manslayers.”","words":[{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"righteous"},{"strongs":"G459","hebrew":"ἄνομος","translit":"ánomos","english":"lawless"},{"strongs":"G506","hebrew":"ἀνυπότακτος","translit":"anypótaktos","english":"insubordinate"},{"strongs":"G765","hebrew":"ἀσεβής","translit":"asebḗs","english":"ungodly"},{"strongs":"G268","hebrew":"ἁμαρτωλός","translit":"hamartōlós","english":"sinners"},{"strongs":"G462","hebrew":"ἀνόσιος","translit":"anósios","english":"unholy"},{"strongs":"G952","hebrew":"βέβηλος","translit":"bébēlos","english":"profane"},{"strongs":"G3964","hebrew":"πατραλῴας","translit":"patralṓias","english":"murderers of fathers"},{"strongs":"G3389","hebrew":"μητραλῴας","translit":"mētralṓias","english":"murderers of mothers"},{"strongs":"G409","hebrew":"ἀνδροφόνος","translit":"androphónos","english":"manslayers"}]},{"v":10,"text":"for the sexually immoral, for homosexuals, for slave-traders, for liars, for perjurers, and for any other thing contrary to the sound doctrine;","understanding":"The list continues with the seventh, eighth and ninth commandments. πόρνοις, “the sexually immoral.” ἀρσενοκοίταις, “homosexuals” — a word formed from ἄρσην (male) and κοίτη (bed), the two words used together in the Greek translation of Leviticus 18:22 and 20:13; it refers to men who have sex with men, and the text should be read as saying so. It stands in a list that also names ἀνδραποδισταῖς, “slave-traders” (kidnappers who sell people), ψεύσταις, “liars,” and ἐπιόρκοις, “perjurers” — sins that are equally condemned, and equally forgiven, as verse 15 shows. All are contrary to τῇ ὑγιαινούσῃ διδασκαλίᾳ, “the sound doctrine” — ὑγιαίνω means to be healthy: teaching that produces spiritual health.","words":[{"strongs":"G4205","hebrew":"πόρνος","translit":"pórnos","english":"sexually immoral"},{"strongs":"G733","hebrew":"ἀρσενοκοίτης","translit":"arsenokoítēs","english":"homosexuals"},{"strongs":"G405","hebrew":"ἀνδραποδιστής","translit":"andrapodistḗs","english":"slave-traders"},{"strongs":"G5583","hebrew":"ψεύστης","translit":"pseústēs","english":"liars"},{"strongs":"G1965","hebrew":"ἐπίορκος","translit":"epíorkos","english":"perjurers"},{"strongs":"G5198","hebrew":"ὑγιαίνω","translit":"hygiaínō","english":"sound"},{"strongs":"G1319","hebrew":"διδασκαλία","translit":"didaskalía","english":"doctrine"}]},{"v":11,"text":"according to the Good News of the glory of the blessed God, which was committed to my trust.","understanding":"This sound teaching is “according to the Good News (εὐαγγέλιον) of the glory (δόξης) of the blessed (μακαρίου) God.” μακάριος means supremely happy, blessed in Himself. The gospel reveals the glory of a God who is complete joy. And this gospel ἐπιστεύθην ἐγώ, “was committed to my trust” — entrusted like a valuable deposit. The law exposes sin; the gospel reveals glory and grace.","words":[{"strongs":"G2098","hebrew":"εὐαγγέλιον","translit":"euangélion","english":"Good News"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G3107","hebrew":"μακάριος","translit":"makários","english":"blessed"},{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"committed to my trust"}]},{"v":12,"text":"And I thank him who enabled me, Christ Jesus our Lord, because he counted me faithful, appointing me to service;","understanding":"Paul cannot speak of the gospel without breaking into thanks: “And I thank him who enabled me (ἐνδυναμώσαντι), Christ Jesus our Lord.” ἐνδυναμόω, a power-family word: to put δύναμις into someone, to empower from within — as in Philippians 4:13, “I can do all things through Christ, who strengthens me.” Christ ‘counted me faithful (πιστόν)’, θέμενος εἰς διακονίαν, “appointing me to service.” Ministry is not a career Paul chose; it is a trust Christ placed in him.","words":[{"strongs":"G1743","hebrew":"ἐνδυναμόω","translit":"endynamóō","english":"enabled"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G2233","hebrew":"ἡγέομαι","translit":"hēgéomai","english":"counted"},{"strongs":"G5087","hebrew":"τίθημι","translit":"títhēmi","english":"appointing"},{"strongs":"G1248","hebrew":"διακονία","translit":"diakonía","english":"service"}]},{"v":13,"text":"although I was before a blasphemer, a persecutor, and insolent. However, I obtained mercy, because I did it ignorantly in unbelief.","understanding":"Three words describe who Paul had been: βλάσφημον, “a blasphemer” — speaking against Christ; διώκτην, “a persecutor” — hunting down believers; ὑβριστήν, “insolent” — a violent, arrogant abuser. “However, I obtained mercy (ἠλεήθην), because I did it ignorantly (ἀγνοῶν) in unbelief (ἀπιστίᾳ).” Paul is not excusing himself; ignorance did not make him innocent. But it placed him among those who sinned without knowing, for whom Jesus prayed, ‘Father, forgive them’.","words":[{"strongs":"G989","hebrew":"βλάσφημος","translit":"blásphēmos","english":"blasphemer"},{"strongs":"G1376","hebrew":"διώκτης","translit":"diṓktēs","english":"persecutor"},{"strongs":"G5197","hebrew":"ὑβριστής","translit":"hybristḗs","english":"insolent"},{"strongs":"G1653","hebrew":"ἐλεέω","translit":"eleéō","english":"obtained mercy"},{"strongs":"G50","hebrew":"ἀγνοέω","translit":"agnoéō","english":"ignorantly"},{"strongs":"G570","hebrew":"ἀπιστία","translit":"apistía","english":"unbelief"}]},{"v":14,"text":"The grace of our Lord abounded exceedingly with faith and love which is in Christ Jesus.","understanding":"ὑπερεπλεόνασεν δὲ ἡ χάρις, “The grace of our Lord abounded exceedingly” — a ‘super-abounded’ word: grace overflowed beyond all measure, more than enough to cover a blasphemer's sin. And it came μετὰ πίστεως καὶ ἀγάπης, “with faith and love which is in Christ Jesus” — replacing his unbelief (v. 13) with faith, and his violence with love.","words":[{"strongs":"G5250","hebrew":"ὑπερπλεονάζω","translit":"hyperpleonázō","english":"abounded exceedingly"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"}]},{"v":15,"text":"The saying is faithful and worthy of all acceptance, that Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners; of whom I am chief.","understanding":"πιστὸς ὁ λόγος, “The saying is faithful” — the first of five ‘faithful sayings’ in the pastoral letters, probably memorable statements used in the churches — καὶ πάσης ἀποδοχῆς ἄξιος, “and worthy of all acceptance”: deserving to be welcomed wholeheartedly. Here is the gospel in one sentence: “Christ Jesus came into the world (κόσμον) to save (σῶσαι) sinners (ἁμαρτωλούς); of whom I am chief (πρῶτος).” Present tense: not ‘I was’ but ‘I am’ the foremost. Paul never stopped seeing himself as a forgiven sinner.","words":[{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"saying"},{"strongs":"G594","hebrew":"ἀποδοχή","translit":"apodochḗ","english":"acceptance"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"save"},{"strongs":"G268","hebrew":"ἁμαρτωλός","translit":"hamartōlós","english":"sinners"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"chief"}]},{"v":16,"text":"However, for this cause I obtained mercy, that in me first, Jesus Christ might display all his patience, for an example of those who were going to believe in him for eternal life.","understanding":"Why did Paul receive mercy? So that in him πρώτῳ, “first” — as the foremost sinner — Jesus Christ might ἐνδείξηται, “display,” τὴν ἅπασαν μακροθυμίαν, “all his patience” — His long-tempered, enduring patience — πρὸς ὑποτύπωσιν, “for an example,” a pattern, a sketch, of all who would later believe for ζωὴν αἰώνιον, “eternal life.” If Christ could save Paul, no one is beyond His reach.","words":[{"strongs":"G1653","hebrew":"ἐλεέω","translit":"eleéō","english":"obtained mercy"},{"strongs":"G1731","hebrew":"ἐνδείκνυμι","translit":"endeíknymi","english":"display"},{"strongs":"G3115","hebrew":"μακροθυμία","translit":"makrothymía","english":"patience"},{"strongs":"G5296","hebrew":"ὑποτύπωσις","translit":"hypotýpōsis","english":"example"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"}]},{"v":17,"text":"Now to the King eternal, immortal, invisible, to God who alone is wise, be honor and glory forever and ever. Amen.","understanding":"Paul bursts into worship. τῷ δὲ βασιλεῖ τῶν αἰώνων, “Now to the King eternal” — literally ‘the King of the ages’, the time family's αἰών: He rules over every age of history. ἀφθάρτῳ, “immortal” — incorruptible, not subject to decay; ἀοράτῳ, “invisible”; μόνῳ σοφῷ θεῷ, “to God who alone is wise” (the WEB follows manuscripts that include ‘wise’). To Him be τιμὴ καὶ δόξα, “honor and glory forever and ever.” Doctrine that does not end in worship has not been understood.","words":[{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"King"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"eternal"},{"strongs":"G862","hebrew":"ἄφθαρτος","translit":"áphthartos","english":"immortal"},{"strongs":"G517","hebrew":"ἀόρατος","translit":"aóratos","english":"invisible"},{"strongs":"G4680","hebrew":"σοφός","translit":"sophós","english":"wise"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"}]},{"v":18,"text":"This instruction I commit to you, my child Timothy, according to the prophecies which were given to you before, that by them you may wage the good warfare;","understanding":"“This instruction (παραγγελίαν) I commit (παρατίθεμαι) to you, my child Timothy” — παρατίθημι means to deposit something in trust. It is given ‘according to the prophecies (προφητείας) which were given to you before’ — probably words spoken over Timothy when he was set apart for ministry (4:14). By them he is to στρατεύῃ τὴν καλὴν στρατείαν, “wage the good warfare” — fight the noble campaign. Ministry is a military campaign, and καλός, the good-family word, marks it as honourable.","words":[{"strongs":"G3852","hebrew":"παραγγελία","translit":"parangelía","english":"instruction"},{"strongs":"G3908","hebrew":"παρατίθημι","translit":"paratíthēmi","english":"commit"},{"strongs":"G4394","hebrew":"προφητεία","translit":"prophēteía","english":"prophecies"},{"strongs":"G4754","hebrew":"στρατεύομαι","translit":"strateúomai","english":"wage"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G4752","hebrew":"στρατεία","translit":"strateía","english":"warfare"}]},{"v":19,"text":"holding faith and a good conscience; which some having thrust away made a shipwreck concerning the faith;","understanding":"Two weapons: πίστιν, “faith,” and ἀγαθὴν συνείδησιν, “a good conscience.” Some ἀπωσάμενοι, “having thrust away” — deliberately pushed aside — a good conscience, ναυάγησαν, “made a shipwreck,” concerning the faith. The picture is a ship broken on rocks. A conscience ignored is like a ship's rudder thrown overboard: shipwreck of the faith follows.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G18","hebrew":"ἀγαθός","translit":"agathós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G4893","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","english":"conscience"},{"strongs":"G683","hebrew":"ἀπωθέομαι","translit":"apōthéomai","english":"thrust away"},{"strongs":"G3489","hebrew":"ναυαγέω","translit":"nauagéō","english":"made a shipwreck"}]},{"v":20,"text":"of whom are Hymenaeus and Alexander; whom I delivered to Satan, that they might be taught not to blaspheme.","understanding":"Two examples: Ὑμέναιος, Hymenaeus (who later taught that the resurrection had already happened, 2 Timothy 2:17–18), and Ἀλέξανδρος, Alexander (possibly the coppersmith of 2 Timothy 4:14). Paul παρέδωκα τῷ Σατανᾷ, “delivered to Satan” — put them out of the church's fellowship into the realm of Satan (compare 1 Corinthians 5:5). The purpose is restorative: ἵνα παιδευθῶσιν, “that they might be taught” — disciplined like a child being trained — μὴ βλασφημεῖν, “not to blaspheme.” Even this severe discipline aims at repentance.","words":[{"strongs":"G5211","hebrew":"Ὑμεναῖος","translit":"Hymenaîos","english":"Hymenaeus"},{"strongs":"G223","hebrew":"Ἀλέξανδρος","translit":"Aléxandros","english":"Alexander"},{"strongs":"G3860","hebrew":"παραδίδωμι","translit":"paradídōmi","english":"delivered"},{"strongs":"G4567","hebrew":"Σατανᾶς","translit":"Satanâs","english":"Satan"},{"strongs":"G3811","hebrew":"παιδεύω","translit":"paideúō","english":"taught"},{"strongs":"G987","hebrew":"βλασφημέω","translit":"blasphēméō","english":"blaspheme"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Archer Who Missed the Target","verseRef":"v. 5-7","image":"Picture an archery range. At the far end stands a target with a bright centre marked ‘LOVE’. One archer draws, aims carefully and hits it. Others are busy arguing about the history of their bows, the genealogy of the man who made the arrows, the exact theory of flight. They shoot confidently, but their arrows fly wide and land in the dust behind the target. They keep talking loudly about archery, yet none of them has hit the mark.","meaning":"Paul writes, “but the goal of this command is love, out of a pure heart and a good conscience and sincere faith; from which things some, having missed the mark, have turned away to vain talking” (vv. 5–6). The word for ‘goal’ is τέλος, the target, and ‘missed the mark’ is the archer's word. All Christian teaching aims at love. Teaching that produces only arguments, speculation and pride has missed the target, however confident it sounds. Ask yourself: is what I learn making me more loving?"},{"title":"The Chief of Sinners","verseRef":"v. 12-16","image":"Picture a road outside Damascus at midday. A young rabbi rides at the head of armed men, carrying arrest warrants for followers of Jesus. He has already watched Stephen stoned to death and approved. He has dragged men and women from their homes to prison. His heart is full of threats. Then a light brighter than the sun knocks him to the ground, and a voice says his name: ‘Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me?’ Years later the same man, now an old apostle, writes to his spiritual son: of all sinners, I am the worst — and look what Christ's patience did with me.","meaning":"Paul remembers that he was “a blasphemer, a persecutor, and insolent” (v. 13). Yet “The grace of our Lord abounded exceedingly” (v. 14). Then the heart of the gospel: “The saying is faithful and worthy of all acceptance, that Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners; of whom I am chief” (v. 15). Christ saved Paul so “that in me first, Jesus Christ might display all his patience, for an example of those who were going to believe in him for eternal life” (v. 16). There is no one you know — including you — who is beyond the reach of this patience."},{"title":"The Shipwreck","verseRef":"v. 18-20","image":"Picture a merchant ship leaving harbour, sails full, cargo loaded. The captain has a good compass and a steady rudder. But during the voyage he grows tired of the compass's warnings and throws it overboard, and then lashes the rudder so that he does not have to steer. For a while the ship sails on. Then the night grows dark, the rocks appear, and with a terrible crash the hull splits open. The cargo is lost, and the ship lies broken on the shore.","meaning":"Paul charges Timothy to wage the good warfare “holding faith and a good conscience; which some having thrust away made a shipwreck concerning the faith” (v. 19). The conscience is a gift from God, warning us when we drift. When believers deliberately push it aside to keep doing what they want, they may sail on for a while, but shipwreck follows. Hymenaeus and Alexander were real examples. Keep your faith and your conscience together; they are your compass and your rudder."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Greeting (v. 1-2)","body":"Paul, an apostle by command of God our Saviour and Christ Jesus our hope, greets Timothy, his true child in the faith, with grace, mercy and peace."},{"label":"Silence the False Teachers (v. 3-7)","body":"Timothy is to remain in Ephesus and command certain people not to teach a different doctrine or devote themselves to myths and endless genealogies, which produce speculation; the goal of the command is love from a pure heart, a good conscience and sincere faith, which some have abandoned for empty talk."},{"label":"The Right Use of the Law (v. 8-11)","body":"The law is good when used lawfully: it is not for the righteous but for the lawless and every sin contrary to sound teaching, according to the glorious gospel entrusted to Paul."},{"label":"Mercy for the Chief of Sinners (v. 12-17)","body":"Paul thanks Christ for strengthening and appointing him though he had been a blasphemer, persecutor and violent man; Christ came into the world to save sinners, of whom Paul is the foremost, so that His perfect patience might be displayed as an example; to the eternal King be honour and glory."},{"label":"Wage the Good Warfare (v. 18-20)","body":"Paul entrusts this charge to Timothy, to fight the good fight holding faith and a good conscience, which some have rejected and shipwrecked their faith, such as Hymenaeus and Alexander, whom Paul handed over to Satan to learn not to blaspheme."}],"sections":[{"heading":"The Goal Is Love","paragraphs":["1 Timothy opens with an urgent assignment. Paul had left Timothy in Ephesus “that you might command certain men not to teach a different doctrine, and not to pay attention to myths and endless genealogies, which cause disputes, rather than God’s stewardship, which is in faith” (vv. 3–4). We do not know every detail of the false teaching, but its features are clear: speculative stories, fanciful use of Old Testament genealogies, confident claims about the Law, and an appetite for controversy. It produced arguments, not godliness. Paul had warned the Ephesian elders that this would happen: “Men will arise from among your own selves, speaking perverse things, to draw away the disciples after them” (Acts 20:30).","Against all this Paul sets the purpose of true teaching: “but the goal of this command is love, out of a pure heart and a good conscience and sincere faith” (v. 5). This is one of the most important sentences in the pastoral letters. Sound doctrine is not an end in itself. Its target is love — and love that flows from a clean heart, an honest conscience and faith without pretence. Some had “missed the mark” and “turned away to vain talking” (v. 6). They wanted to be teachers of the Law, but they understood “neither what they say, nor about what they strongly affirm” (v. 7).","This is a warning for every generation. It is possible to be very confident, very busy with Bible teaching and very interested in hidden details — and still miss the point entirely. The test of teaching is not how clever it is but whether it produces love. A church full of arguments about speculative questions and empty of love has missed the target, however orthodox its vocabulary."]},{"heading":"The Law Used Lawfully","paragraphs":["Paul does not reject the Law. “But we know that the law is good, if a person uses it lawfully” (v. 8). The false teachers misused it for speculation. Its real purpose is moral: it exposes sin. “law is not made for a righteous person, but for the lawless and insubordinate, for the ungodly and sinners” (v. 9). The list that follows (vv. 9–10) runs roughly through the Ten Commandments: sins against God, against parents, against life, sexual sin, kidnapping, and lies and false oaths.","One word in the list needs to be stated clearly. The WEB's ‘homosexuals’ translates ἀρσενοκοίτης, a word formed from the two words used in the Greek Old Testament's prohibitions in Leviticus 18:22 and 20:13; it refers to men who have sex with men. Paul names it alongside sexual immorality in general, slave-trading, lying and perjury. The Bible does not single out one sin as uniquely unforgivable; it names all these as contrary to “the sound doctrine” (v. 10), and then, five verses later, declares that “Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners” (v. 15). The same chapter that names sin plainly offers mercy freely.","The standard for judging all this is “the sound doctrine; according to the Good News of the glory of the blessed God, which was committed to my trust” (vv. 10–11). ‘Sound’ means healthy. Healthy teaching produces healthy lives. The Law shows the disease; the gospel provides the cure. As Paul wrote to the Galatians, “the law has become our tutor to bring us to Christ, that we might be justified by faith” (Galatians 3:24)."]},{"heading":"Mercy That Makes an Example","paragraphs":["The mention of the gospel sends Paul into thanksgiving and testimony: “And I thank him who enabled me, Christ Jesus our Lord, because he counted me faithful, appointing me to service; although I was before a blasphemer, a persecutor, and insolent. However, I obtained mercy” (vv. 12–13). Paul never forgot who he had been. He told the Galatians that “beyond measure I persecuted the assembly of God, and ravaged it” (Galatians 1:13), and the Corinthians, “I am the least of the apostles … because I persecuted the assembly of God” (1 Corinthians 15:9).","Then comes the first ‘faithful saying’: “The saying is faithful and worthy of all acceptance, that Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners; of whom I am chief” (v. 15). Notice the present tense: ‘I am’. Paul did not see himself as a former sinner who had graduated to a better class; he saw himself as the foremost example of how far grace can reach. And that was God's purpose: “that in me first, Jesus Christ might display all his patience, for an example of those who were going to believe in him for eternal life” (v. 16). Paul's life is a sketch, a pattern, showing every future believer what Christ's patience can do.","The testimony ends in worship: “Now to the King eternal, immortal, invisible, to God who alone is wise, be honor and glory forever and ever. Amen” (v. 17). Then Paul turns back to Timothy: “This instruction I commit to you, my child Timothy, according to the prophecies which were given to you before, that by them you may wage the good warfare; holding faith and a good conscience” (vv. 18–19). Ministry is warfare, and its two essential weapons are faith and a good conscience. Some, like Hymenaeus and Alexander, threw away their conscience and “made a shipwreck concerning the faith” (v. 19)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Paul's Conversion and Timothy's Calling","paragraphs":["Acts tells the story Paul summarises in verses 12–16. On the road to Damascus, Saul the persecutor “fell on the earth, and heard a voice saying to him, ‘Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me?’” (Acts 9:4). Later he told King Agrippa, “I myself most certainly thought that I ought to do many things contrary to the name of Jesus of Nazareth” (Acts 26:9). His ignorance did not make him innocent, but it made him a recipient of mercy — and his conversion became the great example of Christ's patience.","Timothy's story also begins in Acts: “He came to Derbe and Lystra: and behold, a certain disciple was there, named Timothy, the son of a Jewess who believed; but his father was a Greek” (Acts 16:1), and “The brothers who were at Lystra and Iconium gave a good testimony about him” (Acts 16:2). Paul later reminded him of “the sincere faith that is in you; which lived first in your grandmother Lois, and your mother Eunice” (2 Timothy 1:5). The ‘true child in faith’ of 1 Timothy 1:2 had a godly heritage and a proven character.","The fate of Hymenaeus is noted again in Paul's last letter: “and those words will consume like gangrene, of whom is Hymenaeus and Philetus” (2 Timothy 2:17). And Paul says of an Alexander, “Alexander, the coppersmith, did much evil to me. The Lord will repay him according to his deeds” (2 Timothy 4:14). Rejecting a good conscience is never a private matter; it spreads harm. That is why Paul calls Timothy to hold on to both faith and a good conscience."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Teach a different doctrine","hebrew":"ἑτεροδιδασκαλέω","translit":"heterodidaskaléō","strongs":"G2085","verses":"v. 3","insight":"Strong's: “to instruct differently.” The word combines ἕτερος, another of a different kind, and διδάσκαλος, teacher. It appears only in 1 Timothy (here and 6:3). The problem in Ephesus was not new emphases or styles but teaching different in kind from the apostolic gospel. Galatians 1:6 uses ἕτερος for a ‘different good news’ that is no gospel at all. Variety in style is healthy; difference in substance is dangerous."},{"english":"Goal / end / target","hebrew":"τέλος","translit":"télos","strongs":"G5056","verses":"v. 5","insight":"Strong's: “properly, the point aimed at as a limit, i.e. (by implication) the conclusion of an act or state.” It is the finish line, the aim, the purpose for which something exists. Paul uses it in Romans 10:4: Christ is the τέλος of the law. Here the τέλος of all apostolic instruction is love. If you want to know whether teaching is sound, look at where it is aimed and what it produces. True doctrine always aims at love from a pure heart."},{"english":"Be sound / healthy","hebrew":"ὑγιαίνω","translit":"hygiaínō","strongs":"G5198","verses":"v. 10","insight":"Strong's: “to have sound health, i.e. be well (in body); figuratively, to be uncorrupt (true in doctrine).” It gives us the word ‘hygiene’. The pastoral letters use it eight times for ‘sound doctrine’ and ‘sound words’. Teaching is not only true or false; it is healthy or diseased. Healthy teaching produces spiritually healthy people: humble, loving, godly. False teaching, Paul warns, ‘spreads like gangrene’ (2 Timothy 2:17)."},{"english":"Empower / strengthen","hebrew":"ἐνδυναμόω","translit":"endynamóō","strongs":"G1743","verses":"v. 12","insight":"Strong's: “to empower.” From ἐν, in, and δύναμις, power: to put power into someone. In the power family it describes Christ's strength working inside His servants. Paul uses it in Philippians 4:13 (‘Christ, who strengthens me’) and 2 Timothy 4:17 (‘the Lord stood by me and strengthened me’). Paul did not enter ministry because he was strong; Christ put His strength into a former persecutor."},{"english":"Patience / long-suffering","hebrew":"μακροθυμία","translit":"makrothymía","strongs":"G3115","verses":"v. 16","insight":"Strong's: “longanimity, i.e. (objectively) forbearance or (subjectively) fortitude.” Literally ‘long temper’ — the opposite of being short-tempered. It describes God's slowness to anger with sinners. Paul's conversion displayed ‘all’ of Christ's patience: years of blasphemy and persecution met not with immediate judgment but with mercy. Peter says the Lord's patience means salvation. If Christ was that patient with Paul, He is patient with you."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: what your knowledge produces, how you see your own past, and the state of your conscience.","items":[{"title":"Hitting the Target","body":"The goal of all instruction is love (v. 5). The Spirit wants to ask: what is your Bible knowledge producing? More love for God and people, or more arguments, pride and speculation? Perhaps you have spent energy on controversies, theories or debates that leave you colder toward people. Ask Him to aim your learning at its true target — love from a pure heart, a good conscience and sincere faith."},{"title":"You Are Not Beyond Mercy","body":"Maybe you carry a past that still shames you — things you did against God or against people. Paul called himself the chief of sinners, yet he received mercy as an example. The Spirit wants you to hear it: “Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners” (v. 15). That includes you. Do not let your past keep you away from His patience; let it become a testimony of how far His grace reaches."},{"title":"Keep Your Compass","body":"Some made shipwreck of their faith by pushing aside a good conscience (v. 19). Is there something your conscience keeps warning you about, but you keep pushing the warning away? The Spirit is not condemning you; He is trying to keep you off the rocks. Listen to Him today, confess what needs confessing, and make the change. A good conscience is precious."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Target Check","body":"List the last three spiritual topics you have studied, argued about or watched videos on. Next to each, write whether it has produced more love in you (v. 5). If not, choose one way to redirect your learning toward love."},{"title":"The Testimony Check","body":"Write a short paragraph: who you were before Christ, and what His mercy has done (vv. 12–16). Read it back. Thank God, and ask Him for one person you could share it with."},{"title":"The Conscience Check","body":"Is there any area where you have been ignoring your conscience (v. 19)? Write it down privately, bring it to God in confession, and decide on one step to set it right."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Learn the Faithful Saying","body":"Memorise verse 15 this week: “The saying is faithful and worthy of all acceptance, that Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners; of whom I am chief.” Say it each morning. Each day, thank God for one specific way His patience has shown itself in your life. At the end of the week, share the verse and your story with someone who thinks they are too bad for God, or too good to need Him."},{"title":"Fight the Good Fight Together","body":"Paul entrusted Timothy with a charge and reminded him of the prophetic words spoken over him (v. 18). Find a mature believer and ask them to pray with you about the calling God has given you. Write down any encouragement or Scripture they share. Each week for a month, read it again and ask: am I waging the good warfare, holding faith and a good conscience? Report back to them at the end of the month."}]},"prayerReflection":"“God my Saviour and Christ Jesus my hope, thank You for grace, mercy and peace. Guard me from different doctrines, myths and endless speculation that produce arguments instead of faith. Let the goal of everything I learn be love, flowing from a pure heart, a good conscience and sincere faith. Keep me from missing the mark and turning aside to empty talk. Thank You that Your law is good, and that it shows me my need. Thank You most of all that Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners — and I am one of them. Your grace has overflowed toward me with faith and love. Strengthen me as You strengthened Paul, and count me faithful in the service You give me. Display Your patience through my life, so that others may believe in You for eternal life. Help me fight the good fight, holding faith and a good conscience, so that I never make shipwreck of my faith. Now to the King eternal, immortal, invisible, the only wise God, be honour and glory for ever and ever. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Saul on the Damascus Road","ref":"Acts 9:1-19","relatedVerses":"v. 12-16","summary":"Saul, still breathing threats and murder against the Lord's disciples, obtained letters from the high priest to arrest followers of the Way in Damascus. As he approached the city, a light from heaven flashed around him. “He fell on the earth, and heard a voice saying to him, ‘Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me?’” (Acts 9:4). The voice was Jesus. Blind for three days, Saul was led into Damascus, where the Lord sent a frightened disciple named Ananias to lay hands on him. His sight returned, he was filled with the Spirit and baptized, and immediately he began to preach that Jesus is the Son of God. This is the ‘mercy’ of 1 Timothy 1:13 and the ‘example’ of 1:16."},{"title":"Paul's Farewell to the Ephesian Elders","ref":"Acts 20:17-38","relatedVerses":"v. 3-7","summary":"On his way to Jerusalem, Paul called the elders of the Ephesian church to meet him at Miletus. He reminded them how he had served among them with tears, taught them publicly and from house to house, and declared the whole counsel of God. Then he warned them: “For I know that after my departure, vicious wolves will enter in among you, not sparing the flock” (Acts 20:29), and “Men will arise from among your own selves, speaking perverse things, to draw away the disciples after them” (Acts 20:30). They wept and embraced him, knowing they would not see his face again. 1 Timothy shows that the warning came true — and that Paul sent Timothy to deal with it."},{"title":"The Riot at Ephesus","ref":"Acts 19:23-41","relatedVerses":"v. 3, 20","summary":"During Paul's ministry in Ephesus, so many people turned to Christ that the trade in silver shrines of the goddess Artemis began to suffer. A silversmith named Demetrius gathered the craftsmen and stirred them up, and the whole city filled with confusion. The crowd rushed into the theatre shouting, ‘Great is Artemis of the Ephesians!’ for about two hours. “They brought Alexander out of the multitude, the Jews putting him forward. Alexander beckoned with his hand, and would have made a defense to the people” (Acts 19:33), but the crowd would not listen. Finally the town clerk calmed them. This is the city where Timothy now served: proud, religious, commercial and spiritually dark — a place where the church had to be ‘the pillar and ground of the truth’."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Timothy","chapter":2,"chapterTitle":"One God, One Mediator — Prayer for All People and Order in Worship","themeTitle":"“There Is One God, and One Mediator Between God and Men, the Man Christ Jesus”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"I exhort therefore, first of all, that petitions, prayers, intercessions, and givings of thanks, be made for all men:","understanding":"παρακαλῶ οὖν πρῶτον πάντων, “I exhort therefore, first of all” — before anything else in church life, prayer. Four words from the pray family appear in one breath: δεήσεις, “petitions” — specific requests out of need; προσευχάς, “prayers” — the general word for prayer as worship directed to God; ἐντεύξεις, “intercessions” — approaching a king to plead on behalf of others; and εὐχαριστίας, “givings of thanks.” And all of it ὑπὲρ πάντων ἀνθρώπων, “for all men” — no one is outside the church's prayers.","words":[{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"exhort"},{"strongs":"G1162","hebrew":"δέησις","translit":"déēsis","english":"petitions"},{"strongs":"G4335","hebrew":"προσευχή","translit":"proseuchḗ","english":"prayers"},{"strongs":"G1783","hebrew":"ἔντευξις","translit":"énteuxis","english":"intercessions"},{"strongs":"G2169","hebrew":"εὐχαριστία","translit":"eucharistía","english":"givings of thanks"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"}]},{"v":2,"text":"for kings and all who are in high places; that we may lead a tranquil and quiet life in all godliness and reverence.","understanding":"Specifically ὑπὲρ βασιλέων, “for kings” — in Paul's day this meant the emperor Nero — καὶ πάντων τῶν ἐν ὑπεροχῇ ὄντων, “and all who are in high places.” Christians pray for rulers, even hostile ones. The purpose: ἵνα ἤρεμον καὶ ἡσύχιον βίον διάγωμεν, “that we may lead a tranquil and quiet life” — βίος from the life family: the course of daily earthly life. ἐν πάσῃ εὐσεβείᾳ, “in all godliness” — the first appearance of the key word of the letter — καὶ σεμνότητι, “and reverence,” dignity, seriousness.","words":[{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"kings"},{"strongs":"G5247","hebrew":"ὑπεροχή","translit":"hyperochḗ","english":"high places"},{"strongs":"G2263","hebrew":"ἤρεμος","translit":"ḗremos","english":"tranquil"},{"strongs":"G2272","hebrew":"ἡσύχιος","translit":"hēsýchios","english":"quiet"},{"strongs":"G979","hebrew":"βίος","translit":"bíos","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G2150","hebrew":"εὐσέβεια","translit":"eusébeia","english":"godliness"},{"strongs":"G4587","hebrew":"σεμνότης","translit":"semnótēs","english":"reverence"}]},{"v":3,"text":"For this is good and acceptable in the sight of God our Savior;","understanding":"τοῦτο καλὸν καὶ ἀπόδεκτον, “For this is good and acceptable” — καλός from the good family: noble, beautiful — ἐνώπιον τοῦ σωτῆρος ἡμῶν θεοῦ, “in the sight of God our Savior.” Praying for all people pleases the God whose very title in this letter is ‘Savior’.","words":[{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G587","hebrew":"ἀπόδεκτος","translit":"apódektos","english":"acceptable"},{"strongs":"G1799","hebrew":"ἐνώπιον","translit":"enṓpion","english":"in the sight"},{"strongs":"G4990","hebrew":"σωτήρ","translit":"sōtḗr","english":"Savior"}]},{"v":4,"text":"who desires all people to be saved and come to full knowledge of the truth.","understanding":"Why pray for everyone? Because God πάντας ἀνθρώπους θέλει σωθῆναι, “desires all people to be saved,” καὶ εἰς ἐπίγνωσιν ἀληθείας ἐλθεῖν, “and come to full knowledge of the truth.” ἐπίγνωσις from the know family: full, precise knowledge. God's heart is for the salvation of every kind of person — Jew and Gentile, ruler and slave. Compare 2 Peter 3:9, ‘not wishing that any should perish’.","words":[{"strongs":"G2309","hebrew":"θέλω","translit":"thélō","english":"desires"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"saved"},{"strongs":"G1922","hebrew":"ἐπίγνωσις","translit":"epígnōsis","english":"full knowledge"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"}]},{"v":5,"text":"For there is one God, and one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus,","understanding":"A confession, probably from early Christian worship. εἷς γὰρ θεός, “For there is one God” — against every god in Ephesus, including Artemis. εἷς καὶ μεσίτης θεοῦ καὶ ἀνθρώπων, “and one mediator between God and men” — μεσίτης is a go-between, someone who stands in the middle to bring two parties together. ἄνθρωπος Χριστὸς Ἰησοῦς, “the man Christ Jesus.” Paul stresses His humanity: to represent humans before God, the Mediator had to be truly human — and to bring God to us, truly God.","words":[{"strongs":"G1520","hebrew":"εἷς","translit":"heîs","english":"one"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3316","hebrew":"μεσίτης","translit":"mesítēs","english":"mediator"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"man"}]},{"v":6,"text":"who gave himself as a ransom for all; the testimony in its own times;","understanding":"ὁ δοὺς ἑαυτὸν ἀντίλυτρον ὑπὲρ πάντων, “who gave himself as a ransom for all.” ἀντίλυτρον is a ransom paid in exchange — ἀντί, in place of — echoing Jesus' words in Mark 10:45, ‘to give his life as a ransom for many’. Christ is not only a go-between; He paid the price Himself. τὸ μαρτύριον καιροῖς ἰδίοις, “the testimony in its own times” — the time family's καιρός: at the right, appointed moment in history God gave this testimony.","words":[{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"gave"},{"strongs":"G487","hebrew":"ἀντίλυτρον","translit":"antílytron","english":"ransom"},{"strongs":"G3142","hebrew":"μαρτύριον","translit":"martýrion","english":"testimony"},{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"times"}]},{"v":7,"text":"to which I was appointed a preacher and an apostle (I am telling the truth in Christ, not lying), a teacher of the Gentiles in faith and truth.","understanding":"For this message Paul ἐτέθην, “was appointed,” κῆρυξ, “a preacher” — a herald announcing a king's proclamation — καὶ ἀπόστολος, “and an apostle.” He adds an oath: ἀλήθειαν λέγω, οὐ ψεύδομαι, “I am telling the truth … not lying.” He is διδάσκαλος ἐθνῶν, “a teacher of the Gentiles,” in faith and truth. The universal gospel needs a universal mission.","words":[{"strongs":"G5087","hebrew":"τίθημι","translit":"títhēmi","english":"appointed"},{"strongs":"G2783","hebrew":"κῆρυξ","translit":"kēryx","english":"preacher"},{"strongs":"G652","hebrew":"ἀπόστολος","translit":"apóstolos","english":"apostle"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"},{"strongs":"G5574","hebrew":"ψεύδομαι","translit":"pseúdomai","english":"lying"},{"strongs":"G1320","hebrew":"διδάσκαλος","translit":"didáskalos","english":"teacher"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"Gentiles"}]},{"v":8,"text":"I desire therefore that the men in every place pray, lifting up holy hands without anger and doubting.","understanding":"βούλομαι οὖν, “I desire therefore,” that τοὺς ἄνδρας, “the men” — ἀνήρ, males specifically — ἐν παντὶ τόπῳ, “in every place” (every gathering), προσεύχεσθαι, “pray,” ἐπαίροντας ὁσίους χεῖρας, “lifting up holy hands” — the common posture of prayer, palms raised toward heaven — and those hands must be ὅσιος, holy, devout. And χωρὶς ὀργῆς καὶ διαλογισμοῦ, “without anger and doubting” — διαλογισμός can mean doubting or quarrelling. The false teaching in Ephesus produced disputes (1:4); men who pray must not be angry arguers.","words":[{"strongs":"G1014","hebrew":"βούλομαι","translit":"boúlomai","english":"desire"},{"strongs":"G435","hebrew":"ἀνήρ","translit":"anḗr","english":"men"},{"strongs":"G4336","hebrew":"προσεύχομαι","translit":"proseúchomai","english":"pray"},{"strongs":"G1869","hebrew":"ἐπαίρω","translit":"epaírō","english":"lifting up"},{"strongs":"G3741","hebrew":"ὅσιος","translit":"hósios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hands"},{"strongs":"G3709","hebrew":"ὀργή","translit":"orgḗ","english":"anger"},{"strongs":"G1261","hebrew":"διαλογισμός","translit":"dialogismós","english":"doubting"}]},{"v":9,"text":"In the same way, that women also adorn themselves in decent clothing, with modesty and propriety; not just with braided hair, gold, pearls, or expensive clothing;","understanding":"ὡσαύτως γυναῖκας, “In the same way, that women also” — as men are to pray without anger, women are to κοσμεῖν ἑαυτάς, “adorn themselves,” ἐν καταστολῇ κοσμίῳ, “in decent clothing” — orderly, appropriate — μετὰ αἰδοῦς καὶ σωφροσύνης, “with modesty and propriety” — αἰδώς is a sense of shame or reserve; σωφροσύνη is sound-mindedness, self-control. Not with πλέγμασιν, “braided hair,” χρυσῷ, “gold,” μαργαρίταις, “pearls,” or ἱματισμῷ πολυτελεῖ, “expensive clothing.” In Ephesus, elaborate hairstyles woven with gold and pearls displayed wealth and could signal sexual availability. Paul's concern is not beauty but display.","words":[{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"women"},{"strongs":"G2885","hebrew":"κοσμέω","translit":"kosméō","english":"adorn"},{"strongs":"G2887","hebrew":"κόσμιος","translit":"kósmios","english":"decent"},{"strongs":"G2689","hebrew":"καταστολή","translit":"katastolḗ","english":"clothing"},{"strongs":"G127","hebrew":"αἰδώς","translit":"aidṓs","english":"modesty"},{"strongs":"G4997","hebrew":"σωφροσύνη","translit":"sōphrosýnē","english":"propriety"},{"strongs":"G4117","hebrew":"πλέγμα","translit":"plégma","english":"braided hair"},{"strongs":"G5557","hebrew":"χρυσός","translit":"chrysós","english":"gold"},{"strongs":"G3135","hebrew":"μαργαρίτης","translit":"margarítēs","english":"pearls"},{"strongs":"G4185","hebrew":"πολυτελής","translit":"polytelḗs","english":"expensive"}]},{"v":10,"text":"but (which becomes women professing godliness) with good works.","understanding":"Instead, ὃ πρέπει γυναιξὶν ἐπαγγελλομέναις θεοσέβειαν, “which becomes women professing godliness” — θεοσέβεια, from the godliness family, used only here in the New Testament: reverence for God. Their adornment is to be δι᾽ ἔργων ἀγαθῶν, “with good works” — ἀγαθός from the good family: good in effect, beneficial. Compare 1 Peter 3:3–4: true beauty is ‘the hidden person of the heart’. The best ornament of a godly woman is a life of good deeds.","words":[{"strongs":"G4241","hebrew":"πρέπω","translit":"prépō","english":"becomes"},{"strongs":"G1861","hebrew":"ἐπαγγέλλω","translit":"epangéllō","english":"professing"},{"strongs":"G2317","hebrew":"θεοσέβεια","translit":"theosébeia","english":"godliness"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G18","hebrew":"ἀγαθός","translit":"agathós","english":"good"}]},{"v":11,"text":"Let a woman learn in quietness with full submission.","understanding":"γυνὴ ἐν ἡσυχίᾳ μανθανέτω, “Let a woman learn in quietness.” The command is first of all positive and, in its world, remarkable: μανθάνω, learn — women were to be taught the Scriptures alongside men, which many teachers of the day refused. ἐν ἡσυχίᾳ, ‘in quietness’ — the same word used of the ‘quiet life’ in verse 2 and of quiet work in 2 Thessalonians 3:12: a settled, peaceful, teachable spirit, not necessarily total silence. ἐν πάσῃ ὑποταγῇ, “with full submission” — an orderly, receptive posture toward the teaching and those who give it.","words":[{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"woman"},{"strongs":"G2271","hebrew":"ἡσυχία","translit":"hēsychía","english":"quietness"},{"strongs":"G3129","hebrew":"μανθάνω","translit":"manthánō","english":"learn"},{"strongs":"G5292","hebrew":"ὑποταγή","translit":"hypotagḗ","english":"submission"}]},{"v":12,"text":"But I don’t permit a woman to teach, nor to exercise authority over a man, but to be in quietness.","understanding":"διδάσκειν δὲ γυναικὶ οὐκ ἐπιτρέπω, “But I don’t permit a woman to teach” — in context, the authoritative teaching of the church gathering — οὐδὲ αὐθεντεῖν ἀνδρός, “nor to exercise authority over a man.” αὐθεντέω appears only here in the New Testament; Strong's gives ‘to act of oneself … dominate’, and its exact shade (exercising authority, or seizing it) is debated. Christians reach different conclusions on how this verse applies today; the reasons Paul gives in verses 13–14 come from creation and the fall, which is why many read it as a lasting pattern for the church's teaching office rather than only a local rule. The same Scriptures show women praying and prophesying in worship (1 Corinthians 11:5) and Priscilla teaching Apollos (Acts 18:26).","words":[{"strongs":"G1321","hebrew":"διδάσκω","translit":"didáskō","english":"teach"},{"strongs":"G2010","hebrew":"ἐπιτρέπω","translit":"epitrépō","english":"permit"},{"strongs":"G831","hebrew":"αὐθεντέω","translit":"authentéō","english":"exercise authority"},{"strongs":"G435","hebrew":"ἀνήρ","translit":"anḗr","english":"man"},{"strongs":"G2271","hebrew":"ἡσυχία","translit":"hēsychía","english":"quietness"}]},{"v":13,"text":"For Adam was first formed, then Eve.","understanding":"Paul's first reason is creation: Ἀδὰμ γὰρ πρῶτος ἐπλάσθη, εἶτα Εὕα, “For Adam was first formed, then Eve.” πλάσσω is the potter's word, used in the Greek Genesis for God shaping the man from the dust. Paul grounds the order of verse 12 not in Ephesian culture but in Genesis 2. Compare 1 Corinthians 11:8: “For man is not from woman, but woman from man.” Order in creation is not inequality of worth; both bear God's image.","words":[{"strongs":"G76","hebrew":"Ἀδάμ","translit":"Adám","english":"Adam"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G4111","hebrew":"πλάσσω","translit":"plássō","english":"formed"},{"strongs":"G2096","hebrew":"Εὖα","translit":"Eûa","english":"Eve"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Adam wasn’t deceived, but the woman, being deceived, has fallen into disobedience;","understanding":"The second reason is the fall: “Adam wasn’t deceived (ἠπατήθη), but the woman, being deceived (ἐξαπατηθεῖσα), has fallen into disobedience (παραβάσει).” παράβασις from the sin family: stepping over a line. Paul is not saying women are more gullible than men — Adam's sin was deliberate and in Romans 5:12 the blame rests on him. He is recalling how the order of creation was reversed in the fall, when the serpent addressed the woman and the man followed. Compare 2 Corinthians 11:3.","words":[{"strongs":"G76","hebrew":"Ἀδάμ","translit":"Adám","english":"Adam"},{"strongs":"G538","hebrew":"ἀπατάω","translit":"apatáō","english":"deceived"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"woman"},{"strongs":"G1818","hebrew":"ἐξαπατάω","translit":"exapatáō","english":"being deceived"},{"strongs":"G3847","hebrew":"παράβασις","translit":"parábasis","english":"disobedience"}]},{"v":15,"text":"but she will be saved through her childbearing, if they continue in faith, love, and sanctification with sobriety.","understanding":"σωθήσεται δὲ διὰ τῆς τεκνογονίας, “but she will be saved through her childbearing” — literally ‘through the childbearing’. This is one of the hardest sentences in Paul. It cannot mean that women earn salvation by having children; salvation is by grace through faith, and the verse itself adds ‘if they continue in faith’. The main readings: women will be kept safe through childbirth; women's salvation is worked out in godly embrace of their God-given callings, against the false teachers who forbade marriage (4:3); or ‘the childbearing’ points to the birth of the Messiah, the promised seed of the woman (Genesis 3:15). Then the condition: ἐὰν μείνωσιν, “if they continue,” ἐν πίστει καὶ ἀγάπῃ καὶ ἁγιασμῷ μετὰ σωφροσύνης, “in faith, love, and sanctification with sobriety.”","words":[{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"saved"},{"strongs":"G5042","hebrew":"τεκνογονία","translit":"teknogonía","english":"childbearing"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"continue"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G38","hebrew":"ἁγιασμός","translit":"hagiasmós","english":"sanctification"},{"strongs":"G4997","hebrew":"σωφροσύνη","translit":"sōphrosýnē","english":"sobriety"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Holy Hands Raised for the Emperor","verseRef":"v. 1-4","image":"Picture a small house church in Ephesus. Outside, the city celebrates the emperor Nero with banners and sacrifices. Inside, a handful of believers kneel and stand with their hands lifted. They pray for the city officials who have threatened them, for the governor, for the emperor himself, for the silversmiths who started a riot against them, and for their unbelieving neighbours by name. They give thanks. Their prayers rise from a cramped room and reach the throne of the King above every king.","meaning":"Paul exhorts “that petitions, prayers, intercessions, and givings of thanks, be made for all men: for kings and all who are in high places” (vv. 1–2). Four prayer words show the full range of prayer: specific requests, worshipful prayer, intercession on behalf of others, and thanks. The reason is God's heart: He “desires all people to be saved and come to full knowledge of the truth” (v. 4). If God wants everyone saved, His people pray for everyone — including the powerful and the hostile."},{"title":"The Bridge Across the Chasm","verseRef":"v. 5-6","image":"Picture a vast chasm. On one side stands a holy God; on the other, humanity, unable to cross. People try to build bridges with good works, rituals, priests, saints, angels, philosophies and gifts to the gods, but every bridge falls into the gap. Then one Man walks out from God's side and stands in the middle. He takes hold of God with one hand and of humanity with the other, and He lays down His own life as the bridge. Every other path to God collapses; this one holds.","meaning":"“For there is one God, and one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus, who gave himself as a ransom for all” (vv. 5–6). The word μεσίτης means a go-between who brings two parties together. Jesus is the only one, because He alone is both fully God and fully human, and He alone paid the ransom. You do not need another mediator — no priest, saint or spiritual technique. You can come directly to God through Christ."},{"title":"The Ornament That Lasts","verseRef":"v. 9-10","image":"Picture a wealthy woman in Ephesus preparing for a religious festival. Her hair is piled high and woven with gold threads and pearls, her robe is dyed with expensive purple, and everyone who sees her knows how rich she is. Now picture another woman, plainly dressed, who spent the morning bringing food to a sick widow, teaching a slave child to read the Scriptures and praying with a frightened neighbour. In the city's eyes the first woman is splendid. In heaven's eyes the second is adorned.","meaning":"Paul writes that women should adorn themselves “in decent clothing, with modesty and propriety; not just with braided hair, gold, pearls, or expensive clothing; but (which becomes women professing godliness) with good works” (vv. 9–10). The concern is not that beauty is wrong, but that display, wealth and seduction should not dominate the gathering of God's people. The same principle applies to men and to every age: what we put on to be seen matters far less than the good works that show our reverence for God."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Pray for All People (v. 1-4)","body":"First of all, every kind of prayer is to be made for all people, including kings and those in authority, so that believers may live peaceful, godly lives; this pleases God our Saviour, who wants all people to be saved and come to know the truth."},{"label":"One God, One Mediator (v. 5-7)","body":"There is one God and one mediator between God and humanity, the man Christ Jesus, who gave Himself as a ransom for all; Paul was appointed a herald, apostle and teacher of the Gentiles for this testimony."},{"label":"Men Praying Without Anger (v. 8)","body":"Men everywhere are to pray, lifting holy hands, without anger or disputing."},{"label":"Women Adorned With Good Works (v. 9-10)","body":"Women are to dress modestly and sensibly, not with ostentatious hairstyles, gold, pearls or costly clothes, but with good works fitting for those who profess godliness."},{"label":"Learning, Teaching and Authority (v. 11-15)","body":"A woman should learn quietly and with submission; Paul does not permit a woman to teach or exercise authority over a man, grounding this in creation (Adam formed first) and the fall (Eve deceived); she will be saved through childbearing if they continue in faith, love and holiness with self-control."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Prayer for All, Because God Wants All","paragraphs":["After dealing with the false teachers, Paul turns to the life of the church, and his first instruction is prayer: “I exhort therefore, first of all, that petitions, prayers, intercessions, and givings of thanks, be made for all men” (v. 1). The four words from the pray family are not four separate types of prayer so much as a full description of praying: asking for specific needs, worshipping God, pleading on behalf of others, and thanking Him. A church that wants to hold up the truth before the world begins on its knees.","The scope is ‘all men’, and Paul names the hardest group first: “for kings and all who are in high places; that we may lead a tranquil and quiet life in all godliness and reverence” (v. 2). The emperor at the time was Nero. Paul tells believers to pray for him, not against him. The goal is not political privilege but a peaceful setting in which godliness can flourish and the gospel can spread. Paul teaches the same in Romans 13:1: “Let every soul be in subjection to the higher authorities, for there is no authority except from God.”","The deepest reason is God's heart: “For this is good and acceptable in the sight of God our Savior; who desires all people to be saved and come to full knowledge of the truth” (vv. 3–4). The false teachers seem to have made the faith exclusive and complicated. Paul insists that the gospel is for everyone. As Jesus said, “For God didn’t send his Son into the world to judge the world, but that the world should be saved through him” (John 3:17). If God wants all to be saved, the church prays for all."]},{"heading":"The Only Mediator","paragraphs":["Paul grounds the universal mission in a creed: “For there is one God, and one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus, who gave himself as a ransom for all” (vv. 5–6). In Ephesus there were many gods and many go-betweens: priests of Artemis, magicians, spirits, angels. Paul cuts through all of them. One God; one Mediator. And that Mediator is ‘the man Christ Jesus’ — truly human, able to represent us, and truly divine, able to bring God to us.","He is not only a go-between but a Redeemer: He ‘gave himself as a ransom for all’. The word ἀντίλυτρον echoes Jesus' own words: “For the Son of Man also came not to be served, but to serve, and to give his life as a ransom for many” (Mark 10:45). A mediator usually negotiates; Jesus paid the price Himself. Hebrews calls Him “the mediator of a better covenant” (Hebrews 8:6).","Verse 8 returns to prayer, addressing the men: “I desire therefore that the men in every place pray, lifting up holy hands without anger and doubting.” In a church troubled by disputes, angry men arguing over speculative questions could not pray with holy hands. Holiness of life and peace with others are the conditions of effective public prayer."]},{"heading":"Order in God's House","paragraphs":["Verses 9–15 address women in the church, and they must be read carefully and honestly. First, adornment: women are to adorn themselves “in decent clothing, with modesty and propriety; not just with braided hair, gold, pearls, or expensive clothing; but (which becomes women professing godliness) with good works” (vv. 9–10). In wealthy Ephesus, extravagant display was a mark of status and sometimes of seduction. Peter gives the same counsel: let your beauty be “in the hidden person of the heart, in the incorruptible adornment of a gentle and quiet spirit” (1 Peter 3:4).","Then learning and teaching: “Let a woman learn in quietness with full submission. But I don’t permit a woman to teach, nor to exercise authority over a man, but to be in quietness” (vv. 11–12). The command to learn was itself a radical affirmation in a world where women were often denied religious instruction. The restriction concerns the authoritative teaching and leadership of the church gathering. Paul's reasons are drawn from Genesis: “For Adam was first formed, then Eve” (v. 13), and “Adam wasn’t deceived, but the woman, being deceived, has fallen into disobedience” (v. 14). Because he appeals to creation and the fall rather than local custom, the most natural reading is that this is a lasting pattern for the church's teaching office; believers who read it as aimed at a particular Ephesian problem should be heard with respect, but the text's own reasons point beyond Ephesus.","This is not a statement about worth. The same apostle wrote that “there is neither male nor female; for you are all one in Christ Jesus” (Galatians 3:28), commended Phoebe as a servant of the church (Romans 16:1), and called Prisca and Aquila “my fellow workers in Christ Jesus” (Romans 16:3). Women prayed and prophesied in worship (1 Corinthians 11:5). The chapter ends with hope: “but she will be saved through her childbearing, if they continue in faith, love, and sanctification with sobriety” (v. 15). Whatever the precise meaning of ‘childbearing’, the condition is clear: salvation is found in continuing in faith, love and holiness — the same path for every believer."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Prayer, the Mediator and Adornment Across the New Testament","paragraphs":["Paul's instruction to pray in every form echoes Philippians: “In nothing be anxious, but in everything, by prayer and petition with thanksgiving, let your requests be made known to God” (Philippians 4:6). And our praying is supported by the Spirit's own intercession: “the Spirit himself makes intercession for us with groanings which can’t be uttered” (Romans 8:26). God's desire for all to be saved appears again in Peter: “The Lord is not slow concerning his promise, as some count slowness; but is patient with us, not wishing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance” (2 Peter 3:9).","The one Mediator who gave Himself as a ransom is the Son of Man who “came not to be served, but to serve, and to give his life as a ransom for many” (Mark 10:45). Hebrews explains His mediation: He “has obtained a more excellent ministry, by so much as he is also the mediator of a better covenant” (Hebrews 8:6). There is no other.","On adornment, Peter's parallel is close: “Let your beauty be not just the outward adorning of braiding the hair, and of wearing jewels of gold, or of putting on fine clothing” (1 Peter 3:3). On the fall, Paul writes elsewhere that “sin entered into the world through one man” (Romans 5:12), placing responsibility on Adam, and he warns the whole church: “as the serpent deceived Eve in his craftiness, so your minds might be corrupted from the simplicity that is in Christ” (2 Corinthians 11:3). Deception is a danger for every believer, man or woman."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Intercession","hebrew":"ἔντευξις","translit":"énteuxis","strongs":"G1783","verses":"v. 1","insight":"Strong's: “an interview, i.e. (specially) supplication.” From ἐντυγχάνω, to meet with or approach someone, especially a king, to make a request — the word used of Christ and the Spirit interceding for believers (Romans 8:26–27; Hebrews 7:25). ἔντευξις pictures entering the throne room to plead on behalf of others. Paul wants the church to intercede for all people, even rulers. When you pray for others, you are standing before the King on their behalf."},{"english":"Godliness","hebrew":"εὐσέβεια","translit":"eusébeia","strongs":"G2150","verses":"v. 2","insight":"Strong's: “piety; specially, the gospel scheme.” It combines εὖ, well, and σέβομαι, to reverence: well-directed reverence for God that shapes the whole of life. It appears ten times in 1 Timothy and is the letter's key word. It is not mere religious feeling or outward ritual; it is devotion to God lived out in everyday conduct. Paul prays for a peaceful life so that believers can live ‘in all godliness’. The goal of public order is private and corporate devotion to God."},{"english":"Mediator / go-between","hebrew":"μεσίτης","translit":"mesítēs","strongs":"G3316","verses":"v. 5","insight":"Strong's: “a go-between, i.e. (simply) an internunciator, or (by implication) a reconciler (intercessor).” From μέσος, middle: one who stands in the middle to bring two parties together. Moses was called a mediator of the old covenant (Galatians 3:19–20); Christ is ‘the mediator of a better covenant’ (Hebrews 8:6). Because He is both fully God and fully human, He alone can represent each side to the other. There is one mediator, not many."},{"english":"Ransom (in exchange)","hebrew":"ἀντίλυτρον","translit":"antílytron","strongs":"G487","verses":"v. 6","insight":"Strong's: “a redemption-price.” It is used only here in the New Testament. It combines ἀντί, in place of, and λύτρον, the price paid to free a slave or prisoner. The prefix stresses substitution: Christ gave Himself in place of others. Jesus used λύτρον in Mark 10:45. The ransom is ‘for all’ — offered to every kind of person, which is why the church prays for all."},{"english":"Exercise authority / dominate","hebrew":"αὐθεντέω","translit":"authentéō","strongs":"G831","verses":"v. 12","insight":"Strong's: “to act of oneself, i.e. (figuratively) dominate.” This verb appears only here in the New Testament, which makes its exact shade difficult; outside the Bible it can mean to have authority over, and in some contexts to domineer. Paul pairs it with teaching: the issue is authoritative teaching and leadership in the church gathering. Honest readers should hold its precise meaning with care, while recognising that Paul's reasons in verses 13–14 come from creation and the fall."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: the breadth of your prayers, your confidence in the one Mediator, and what you are adorning yourself with.","items":[{"title":"Pray for the People You Would Rather Ignore","body":"Paul tells believers to pray for all people, especially rulers — including hostile ones. The Spirit may be pointing to someone you have stopped praying for: a political leader you dislike, a boss who treats you badly, a family member who opposes your faith. God desires them to be saved. Ask the Spirit to give you God's heart for them, and lift holy hands in prayer for them this week, without anger."},{"title":"You Need No Other Go-Between","body":"There is one Mediator, Christ Jesus. Some believers live as though they need extra go-betweens — a special leader, a ritual, a saint, a spiritual technique — before God will hear them. The Spirit wants you to come boldly through Christ alone. He gave Himself as your ransom. There is nothing standing between you and the Father except the Mediator who opened the way."},{"title":"What Are You Wearing?","body":"The call to adorn ourselves with good works applies to every believer. The Spirit may be showing you how much energy you put into appearance, status or display — and how little into good works that reflect reverence for God. Ask Him to make your life beautiful with acts of kindness, service and generosity, the adornment that never fades."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Prayer-Range Check","body":"List the people you pray for regularly. Now add the leaders of your nation and city, and one person who opposes you. Pray for each this week using the four prayer words of verse 1: petition, prayer, intercession and thanksgiving."},{"title":"The Mediator Check","body":"Is there anything you rely on, besides Christ, to feel accepted by God or heard by Him (v. 5)? Write it down and thank God that Christ alone is enough."},{"title":"The Adornment Check","body":"Compare the time and money you spend on appearance with the time and money you give to good works (vv. 9–10). Choose one way to rebalance this month."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"A Week of Praying for Leaders","body":"Each day this week, pray for one leader: your head of state, a local official, your employer, your pastor, a school principal, a police officer and a leader you strongly disagree with. Use verse 2: pray that they will govern justly so that people can live peaceful and godly lives, and pray for their salvation (v. 4). Notice how praying for them changes your attitude toward them."},{"title":"Learn and Share the Creed","body":"Memorise verses 5–6: “For there is one God, and one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus, who gave himself as a ransom for all.” Write it on a card. This week, look for an opportunity to share it with someone who believes they must earn their way to God, or who trusts in other mediators. Explain simply that Jesus is the bridge, and that He paid the price."}]},"prayerReflection":"“God our Saviour, You desire all people to be saved and to come to the full knowledge of the truth. Teach me to pray as Paul urged: with petitions, prayers, intercessions and thanksgiving for everyone — for those in authority, for those who oppose me, and for those who do not yet know You. Let us live peaceful and quiet lives in all godliness and reverence. Thank You that there is one God and one Mediator between You and us, the man Christ Jesus, who gave Himself as a ransom for all. I come to You through Him alone. Make my hands holy and my heart free from anger and quarrelling when I pray. Let me be adorned not with display but with good works that show my reverence for You. Give me a teachable spirit, a willingness to learn, and a heart that honours the order of Your house. Keep me continuing in faith, love and holiness with self-control. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Priscilla and Aquila Teach Apollos","ref":"Acts 18:24-28","relatedVerses":"v. 11-12","summary":"A gifted Jewish teacher named Apollos arrived in Ephesus. He was eloquent, mighty in the Scriptures and fervent in spirit, but he knew only the baptism of John. “He began to speak boldly in the synagogue. But when Priscilla and Aquila heard him, they took him aside, and explained to him the way of God more accurately” (Acts 18:26). Apollos went on to strengthen the churches in Achaia, powerfully refuting opponents from the Scriptures. Priscilla, a woman, together with her husband Aquila, helped instruct one of the great teachers of the early church — in Ephesus, the very city where Timothy later served. The New Testament shows women learning deeply and serving significantly in the spread of the gospel."},{"title":"The Fall in the Garden","ref":"Genesis 3:1-13","relatedVerses":"v. 13-15","summary":"In Eden the serpent approached the woman and questioned God's word: had God really said they could not eat from any tree? He then contradicted God outright, promising that eating the forbidden fruit would make them like God. The woman saw that the tree was good for food and desirable, took some and ate, and gave some to her husband, who was with her, and he ate. Their eyes were opened, they hid from God, and when He called, the man blamed the woman and the woman blamed the serpent. Yet in the curse on the serpent, God promised that the seed of the woman would crush the serpent's head. Paul draws on this account in 1 Timothy 2:13–15: the order of creation, the deception, and perhaps the promised Child through whom salvation would come."},{"title":"Paul Prays for King Agrippa","ref":"Acts 26:24-29","relatedVerses":"v. 1-4","summary":"Standing in chains before King Agrippa and the governor Festus, Paul told the story of his conversion and preached Christ's death and resurrection. Festus shouted that Paul was out of his mind. Paul calmly replied that he spoke words of truth and reason, and turned to the king, asking if he believed the prophets. Agrippa said that Paul was almost persuading him to become a Christian. Paul answered that he prayed to God that not only Agrippa but all who heard him that day would become as he was, except for his chains. Here is 1 Timothy 2 lived out: a prisoner praying for the salvation of kings and those in high places, because God desires all people to be saved."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Timothy","chapter":3,"chapterTitle":"Leaders of Proven Character — and the Great Mystery of Godliness","themeTitle":"“The Assembly of the Living God, the Pillar and Ground of the Truth”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"This is a faithful saying: if a man seeks the office of an overseer, he desires a good work.","understanding":"The second ‘faithful saying’ (πιστὸς ὁ λόγος): “if a man seeks (ὀρέγεται) the office of an overseer (ἐπισκοπῆς), he desires (ἐπιθυμεῖ) a good (καλοῦ) work (ἔργου).” ὀρέγομαι means to stretch out for, reach toward. ἐπισκοπή is oversight, from ἐπί, over, and σκοπέω, to watch: the elder watches over the flock. And it is a καλὸν ἔργον — from the good family, a noble, beautiful task, and from the work family, a task, not a title. Wanting to serve God's people as an overseer is good, if it is the work that is desired and not the status.","words":[{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"saying"},{"strongs":"G3713","hebrew":"ὀρέγομαι","translit":"orégomai","english":"seeks"},{"strongs":"G1984","hebrew":"ἐπισκοπή","translit":"episkopḗ","english":"office of an overseer"},{"strongs":"G1937","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμέω","translit":"epithyméō","english":"desires"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"work"}]},{"v":2,"text":"The overseer therefore must be without reproach, the husband of one wife, temperate, sensible, modest, hospitable, good at teaching;","understanding":"The ἐπίσκοπος, “overseer,” must be ἀνεπίλημπτον, “without reproach” — literally ‘not able to be taken hold of’: no handle for an accuser. Then: μιᾶς γυναικὸς ἄνδρα, “the husband of one wife” — literally ‘a one-woman man’. The most natural reading is a man faithful in marriage, sexually devoted to one wife; others have taken it to exclude polygamy, remarriage after divorce, or even unmarried men (though Paul himself was unmarried). Then νηφάλιον, “temperate” — clear-headed; σώφρονα, “sensible” — self-controlled; κόσμιον, “modest” — orderly, respectable; φιλόξενον, “hospitable” — literally ‘a lover of strangers’; διδακτικόν, “good at teaching” — the one qualification that is a skill rather than a character trait.","words":[{"strongs":"G1985","hebrew":"ἐπίσκοπος","translit":"epískopos","english":"overseer"},{"strongs":"G423","hebrew":"ἀνεπίληπτος","translit":"anepílēptos","english":"without reproach"},{"strongs":"G1520","hebrew":"εἷς","translit":"heîs","english":"one"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"wife"},{"strongs":"G435","hebrew":"ἀνήρ","translit":"anḗr","english":"husband"},{"strongs":"G3524","hebrew":"νηφάλεος","translit":"nēpháleos","english":"temperate"},{"strongs":"G4998","hebrew":"σώφρων","translit":"sṓphrōn","english":"sensible"},{"strongs":"G2887","hebrew":"κόσμιος","translit":"kósmios","english":"modest"},{"strongs":"G5382","hebrew":"φιλόξενος","translit":"philóxenos","english":"hospitable"},{"strongs":"G1317","hebrew":"διδακτικός","translit":"didaktikós","english":"good at teaching"}]},{"v":3,"text":"not a drinker, not violent, not greedy for money, but gentle, not quarrelsome, not covetous;","understanding":"Six more marks, mostly negative: μὴ πάροινον, “not a drinker” — literally ‘not sitting long beside wine’; μὴ πλήκτην, “not violent” — not a striker; μὴ αἰσχροκερδῆ, “not greedy for money” — not eager for shameful profit; ἀλλὰ ἐπιεικῆ, “but gentle” — reasonable, yielding, not insisting on his rights; ἄμαχον, “not quarrelsome” — not a fighter; ἀφιλάργυρον, “not covetous” — literally ‘not a lover of silver’. The false teachers were quarrelsome and money-loving (6:4–5); church leaders must be the opposite.","words":[{"strongs":"G3943","hebrew":"πάροινος","translit":"pároinos","english":"drinker"},{"strongs":"G4131","hebrew":"πλήκτης","translit":"plḗktēs","english":"violent"},{"strongs":"G146","hebrew":"αἰσχροκερδής","translit":"aischrokerdḗs","english":"greedy for money"},{"strongs":"G1933","hebrew":"ἐπιεικής","translit":"epieikḗs","english":"gentle"},{"strongs":"G269","hebrew":"ἄμαχος","translit":"ámachos","english":"not quarrelsome"},{"strongs":"G866","hebrew":"ἀφιλάργυρος","translit":"aphilárgyros","english":"not covetous"}]},{"v":4,"text":"one who rules his own house well, having children in subjection with all reverence;","understanding":"Leadership starts at home. He must be τοῦ ἰδίου οἴκου καλῶς προϊστάμενον, “one who rules his own house well” — προΐστημι means to stand before, to lead and care for — with τέκνα, “children,” ἐν ὑποταγῇ μετὰ πάσης σεμνότητος, “in subjection with all reverence.” The home is the training ground and the proving ground for church leadership.","words":[{"strongs":"G3624","hebrew":"οἶκος","translit":"oîkos","english":"house"},{"strongs":"G2573","hebrew":"καλῶς","translit":"kalōs","english":"well"},{"strongs":"G4291","hebrew":"προΐστημι","translit":"proḯstēmi","english":"rules"},{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"children"},{"strongs":"G5292","hebrew":"ὑποταγή","translit":"hypotagḗ","english":"subjection"},{"strongs":"G4587","hebrew":"σεμνότης","translit":"semnótēs","english":"reverence"}]},{"v":5,"text":"(but if a man doesn’t know how to rule his own house, how will he take care of the assembly of God?)","understanding":"Paul's reasoning in a parenthesis: “if a man doesn’t know (οἶδεν) how to rule his own house, how will he take care (ἐπιμελήσεται) of the assembly (ἐκκλησίας) of God?” ἐπιμελέομαι means to care for — the word used of the Good Samaritan caring for the wounded man (Luke 10:34). The church is a family, and leading it is caring for it.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G4291","hebrew":"προΐστημι","translit":"proḯstēmi","english":"rule"},{"strongs":"G3624","hebrew":"οἶκος","translit":"oîkos","english":"house"},{"strongs":"G1959","hebrew":"ἐπιμελέομαι","translit":"epimeléomai","english":"take care"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assembly"}]},{"v":6,"text":"not a new convert, lest being puffed up he fall into the same condemnation as the devil.","understanding":"μὴ νεόφυτον, “not a new convert” — literally ‘newly planted’, the origin of ‘neophyte’. The reason: ἵνα μὴ τυφωθείς, “lest being puffed up” — τυφόω means to wrap in smoke, to make conceited and foggy-headed — he fall εἰς κρίμα τοῦ διαβόλου, “into the same condemnation as the devil” — the judgment that fell on the devil for pride. Promoting new believers too fast feeds pride.","words":[{"strongs":"G3504","hebrew":"νεόφυτος","translit":"neóphytos","english":"new convert"},{"strongs":"G5187","hebrew":"τυφόω","translit":"typhóō","english":"puffed up"},{"strongs":"G2917","hebrew":"κρίμα","translit":"kríma","english":"condemnation"},{"strongs":"G1228","hebrew":"διάβολος","translit":"diábolos","english":"devil"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Moreover he must have good testimony from those who are outside, to avoid falling into reproach and the snare of the devil.","understanding":"μαρτυρίαν καλὴν ἔχειν ἀπὸ τῶν ἔξωθεν, “he must have good testimony from those who are outside” — a good reputation among unbelievers. καλός from the good family: honourable, admirable. Otherwise he falls εἰς ὀνειδισμόν, “into reproach,” and παγίδα τοῦ διαβόλου, “the snare of the devil” — a trap laid to bring public disgrace on the church through its leaders.","words":[{"strongs":"G3141","hebrew":"μαρτυρία","translit":"martyría","english":"testimony"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G1855","hebrew":"ἔξωθεν","translit":"éxōthen","english":"outside"},{"strongs":"G3680","hebrew":"ὀνειδισμός","translit":"oneidismós","english":"reproach"},{"strongs":"G3803","hebrew":"παγίς","translit":"pagís","english":"snare"},{"strongs":"G1228","hebrew":"διάβολος","translit":"diábolos","english":"devil"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Servants, in the same way, must be reverent, not double-tongued, not addicted to much wine, not greedy for money;","understanding":"διακόνους, “Servants” — the servant family's διάκονος, the word that gives ‘deacon’: a servant in action, someone who serves others practically. ὡσαύτως, “in the same way,” they must be σεμνούς, “reverent” — dignified, worthy of respect; μὴ διλόγους, “not double-tongued” — not saying one thing to one person and another to someone else; μὴ οἴνῳ πολλῷ προσέχοντας, “not addicted to much wine”; μὴ αἰσχροκερδεῖς, “not greedy for money” — deacons often handled the church's money and its gifts to the poor.","words":[{"strongs":"G1249","hebrew":"διάκονος","translit":"diákonos","english":"Servants"},{"strongs":"G4586","hebrew":"σεμνός","translit":"semnós","english":"reverent"},{"strongs":"G1351","hebrew":"δίλογος","translit":"dílogos","english":"double-tongued"},{"strongs":"G3631","hebrew":"οἶνος","translit":"oînos","english":"wine"},{"strongs":"G4337","hebrew":"προσέχω","translit":"proséchō","english":"addicted"},{"strongs":"G146","hebrew":"αἰσχροκερδής","translit":"aischrokerdḗs","english":"greedy for money"}]},{"v":9,"text":"holding the mystery of the faith in a pure conscience.","understanding":"“holding (ἔχοντας) the mystery (μυστήριον) of the faith in a pure (καθαρᾷ) conscience (συνειδήσει).” The ‘mystery of the faith’ is the gospel once hidden and now revealed. Deacons are not only practical helpers; they must hold the truth firmly, and with a clean conscience — what they believe and how they live must match.","words":[{"strongs":"G3466","hebrew":"μυστήριον","translit":"mystḗrion","english":"mystery"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G2513","hebrew":"καθαρός","translit":"katharós","english":"pure"},{"strongs":"G4893","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","english":"conscience"}]},{"v":10,"text":"Let them also first be tested; then let them serve if they are blameless.","understanding":"δοκιμαζέσθωσαν πρῶτον, “Let them also first be tested” — δοκιμάζω, the word for testing metals, proving them genuine (1 Thessalonians 2:4). Character is observed before responsibility is given. Then διακονείτωσαν, “let them serve,” ἀνέγκλητοι ὄντες, “if they are blameless” — not accused of anything that would discredit them.","words":[{"strongs":"G1381","hebrew":"δοκιμάζω","translit":"dokimázō","english":"tested"},{"strongs":"G1247","hebrew":"διακονέω","translit":"diakonéō","english":"serve"},{"strongs":"G410","hebrew":"ἀνέγκλητος","translit":"anénklētos","english":"blameless"}]},{"v":11,"text":"Their wives in the same way must be reverent, not slanderers, temperate, faithful in all things.","understanding":"γυναῖκας ὡσαύτως, “Their wives in the same way” — the Greek says simply ‘women in the same way’. It may mean the wives of deacons (as the WEB takes it) or women who themselves served as deacons, like Phoebe, called a διάκονος of the church at Cenchreae (Romans 16:1); the text allows both readings. They must be σεμνάς, “reverent,” μὴ διαβόλους, “not slanderers” — the same word as ‘devil’ (vv. 6–7), whose name means ‘slanderer’: to slander is to do the devil's work — νηφαλίους, “temperate,” πιστὰς ἐν πᾶσιν, “faithful in all things.”","words":[{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"wives"},{"strongs":"G4586","hebrew":"σεμνός","translit":"semnós","english":"reverent"},{"strongs":"G1228","hebrew":"διάβολος","translit":"diábolos","english":"slanderers"},{"strongs":"G3524","hebrew":"νηφάλεος","translit":"nēpháleos","english":"temperate"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Let servants be husbands of one wife, ruling their children and their own houses well.","understanding":"Deacons too must be μιᾶς γυναικὸς ἄνδρες, “husbands of one wife” — faithful in marriage — τέκνων καλῶς προϊστάμενοι καὶ τῶν ἰδίων οἴκων, “ruling their children and their own houses well.” The same home test as for overseers (v. 4).","words":[{"strongs":"G1249","hebrew":"διάκονος","translit":"diákonos","english":"servants"},{"strongs":"G435","hebrew":"ἀνήρ","translit":"anḗr","english":"husbands"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"wife"},{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"children"},{"strongs":"G2573","hebrew":"καλῶς","translit":"kalōs","english":"well"},{"strongs":"G4291","hebrew":"προΐστημι","translit":"proḯstēmi","english":"ruling"},{"strongs":"G3624","hebrew":"οἶκος","translit":"oîkos","english":"houses"}]},{"v":13,"text":"For those who have served well gain for themselves a good standing, and great boldness in the faith which is in Christ Jesus.","understanding":"The reward of faithful service: οἱ γὰρ καλῶς διακονήσαντες, “For those who have served well,” βαθμὸν ἑαυτοῖς καλὸν περιποιοῦνται, “gain for themselves a good standing” — βαθμός is a step, a rank, a standing — καὶ πολλὴν παρρησίαν, “and great boldness,” confident freedom of speech, ἐν πίστει τῇ ἐν Χριστῷ Ἰησοῦ, “in the faith which is in Christ Jesus.” Humble service does not diminish; it builds respect and confidence.","words":[{"strongs":"G2573","hebrew":"καλῶς","translit":"kalōs","english":"well"},{"strongs":"G1247","hebrew":"διακονέω","translit":"diakonéō","english":"served"},{"strongs":"G898","hebrew":"βαθμός","translit":"bathmós","english":"standing"},{"strongs":"G4046","hebrew":"περιποιέομαι","translit":"peripoiéomai","english":"gain"},{"strongs":"G3954","hebrew":"παῤῥησία","translit":"parrhēsía","english":"boldness"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"}]},{"v":14,"text":"These things I write to you, hoping to come to you shortly;","understanding":"“These things I write (γράφω) to you, hoping (ἐλπίζων) to come to you shortly (τάχιον).” Paul hoped to visit soon, but he wrote in case he was delayed. The letter would serve as his presence and his authority.","words":[{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"write"},{"strongs":"G1679","hebrew":"ἐλπίζω","translit":"elpízō","english":"hoping"},{"strongs":"G5032","hebrew":"τάχιον","translit":"táchion","english":"shortly"}]},{"v":15,"text":"but if I wait long, that you may know how men ought to behave themselves in God’s house, which is the assembly of the living God, the pillar and ground of the truth.","understanding":"The purpose statement of the letter: ἐὰν δὲ βραδύνω, “but if I wait long” (am delayed), ἵνα εἰδῇς πῶς δεῖ ἐν οἴκῳ θεοῦ ἀναστρέφεσθαι, “that you may know how men ought to behave themselves in God’s house.” Three pictures of the church: God's οἶκος, house or household; ἐκκλησία θεοῦ ζῶντος, “the assembly of the living God” — unlike the dead goddess Artemis; and στῦλος καὶ ἑδραίωμα τῆς ἀληθείας, “the pillar and ground of the truth” — a column that holds the truth up high for all to see, and a foundation that holds it firm.","words":[{"strongs":"G1019","hebrew":"βραδύνω","translit":"bradýnō","english":"wait long"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G3624","hebrew":"οἶκος","translit":"oîkos","english":"house"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assembly"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"living"},{"strongs":"G4769","hebrew":"στῦλος","translit":"stŷlos","english":"pillar"},{"strongs":"G1477","hebrew":"ἑδραίωμα","translit":"hedraíōma","english":"ground"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"}]},{"v":16,"text":"Without controversy, the mystery of godliness is great: God was revealed in the flesh, justified in the spirit, seen by angels, preached among the nations, believed on in the world, and received up in glory.","understanding":"What truth does the church hold up? ὁμολογουμένως μέγα, “Without controversy … is great” — confessedly, by common confession — τὸ τῆς εὐσεβείας μυστήριον, “the mystery of godliness.” Then probably an early Christian hymn in six lines. A textual note: the WEB, with most manuscripts, reads θεός, “God was revealed in the flesh”; several of the oldest read ὅς, ‘He who was revealed’ — either way the subject is Christ. ἐφανερώθη ἐν σαρκί, “revealed (φανερόω) in the flesh (σαρκί)”; ἐδικαιώθη ἐν πνεύματι, “justified (vindicated) in the spirit”; ὤφθη ἀγγέλοις, “seen (ὁράω, from the see family) by angels”; ἐκηρύχθη ἐν ἔθνεσιν, “preached among the nations”; ἐπιστεύθη ἐν κόσμῳ, “believed on in the world”; ἀνελήμφθη ἐν δόξῃ, “received up (ἀναλαμβάνω, the ascension word) in glory.” The secret of godliness is a Person.","words":[{"strongs":"G3672","hebrew":"ὁμολογουμένως","translit":"homologouménōs","english":"Without controversy"},{"strongs":"G3466","hebrew":"μυστήριον","translit":"mystḗrion","english":"mystery"},{"strongs":"G2150","hebrew":"εὐσέβεια","translit":"eusébeia","english":"godliness"},{"strongs":"G5319","hebrew":"φανερόω","translit":"phaneróō","english":"revealed"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G1344","hebrew":"δικαιόω","translit":"dikaióō","english":"justified"},{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"seen"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G2784","hebrew":"κηρύσσω","translit":"kērýssō","english":"preached"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nations"},{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believed on"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G353","hebrew":"ἀναλαμβάνω","translit":"analambánō","english":"received up"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Shepherd's Own Household","verseRef":"v. 1-7","image":"Picture a man who wants to become a shepherd over a large flock. Before giving him the flock, the owner visits his home. He watches how the man treats his wife, whether his children respect and love him, whether strangers are welcomed at his table, how he handles money, whether he loses his temper, whether the neighbours trust him. Only then does the owner say: ‘You have cared for your own household well. Now care for mine.’","meaning":"Paul writes that an overseer must be “one who rules his own house well, having children in subjection with all reverence; (but if a man doesn’t know how to rule his own house, how will he take care of the assembly of God?)” (vv. 4–5). The list of qualifications is almost entirely about character, not talent: faithful in marriage, self-controlled, hospitable, gentle, not quarrelsome, not money-loving, respected by outsiders. The church does not need impressive personalities as much as tested, godly lives. And these qualities are the goal for every believer, not only leaders."},{"title":"Pillar and Foundation","verseRef":"v. 15","image":"Picture the great temple of Artemis in Ephesus, one of the wonders of the ancient world, with over a hundred marble columns, each sixty feet high, rising above the city. People came from far away to admire its pillars. Now picture a small group of believers meeting in a house nearby. No marble, no gold, no columns. Yet God calls this little household of believers ‘the pillar and ground of the truth’ — the true structure on which His truth stands and is lifted high for the world to see.","meaning":"Paul writes, “that you may know how men ought to behave themselves in God’s house, which is the assembly of the living God, the pillar and ground of the truth” (v. 15). The church does not create the truth; it holds it up and holds it firm. A pillar lifts something so that all can see it; a foundation keeps it from shifting. When believers live in godliness and teach soundly, the truth of Christ is displayed and protected in a world full of false gods."},{"title":"The Hymn of the Mystery","verseRef":"v. 16","image":"Picture a house church in Ephesus at dawn. Slaves, merchants, widows and young people stand together and begin to sing a simple hymn in six short lines. Revealed in flesh — a baby in Bethlehem. Vindicated by the Spirit — the empty tomb. Seen by angels — at His birth, His temptation, His resurrection. Preached among the nations — and here we are, Gentiles in Ephesus. Believed in the world — in our own hearts. Taken up in glory — reigning now. As they sing, the whole story of Christ passes before them.","meaning":"“Without controversy, the mystery of godliness is great: God was revealed in the flesh, justified in the spirit, seen by angels, preached among the nations, believed on in the world, and received up in glory” (v. 16). The secret of true godliness is not a technique or a rule but a Person: Jesus Christ. All the church's order, leadership and behaviour exist to hold up this truth. Godliness grows as we behold Him."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Qualifications for Overseers (v. 1-7)","body":"Desiring the office of overseer is a good work; an overseer must be above reproach, faithful in marriage, self-controlled, respectable, hospitable, able to teach, not a drunkard, violent, quarrelsome or money-loving, managing his household well, not a recent convert, and well thought of by outsiders."},{"label":"Qualifications for Deacons (v. 8-13)","body":"Deacons must likewise be dignified, honest in speech, not given to much wine or greedy for gain, holding the faith with a clear conscience, tested before serving; the women must be dignified, not slanderers, sober and faithful; deacons must be faithful husbands managing their households well; those who serve well gain good standing and great confidence in the faith."},{"label":"The Purpose of the Letter (v. 14-15)","body":"Paul writes so that, if he is delayed, Timothy will know how people ought to conduct themselves in God's household, the church of the living God, the pillar and foundation of the truth."},{"label":"The Mystery of Godliness (v. 16)","body":"Great indeed is the mystery of godliness: Christ revealed in the flesh, vindicated by the Spirit, seen by angels, proclaimed among the nations, believed on in the world, taken up in glory."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Character Before Charisma","paragraphs":["Chapter 3 begins with the second ‘faithful saying’: “This is a faithful saying: if a man seeks the office of an overseer, he desires a good work” (v. 1). In a church where false teachers were seeking influence for the wrong reasons, Paul affirms that leadership is good — but it is a work, not a prize. The word ‘overseer’ (ἐπίσκοπος) is used interchangeably with ‘elder’ (πρεσβύτερος) in the New Testament; Paul told the Ephesian elders, “the Holy Spirit has made you overseers, to shepherd the assembly of the Lord and God which he purchased with his own blood” (Acts 20:28).","What is striking about the list in verses 2–7 is how ordinary it is. Only one item is a skill: “good at teaching” (v. 2). Everything else is character: faithful in marriage, self-controlled, respectable, hospitable, gentle, peaceable, free from the love of money, a good manager of his family, mature in faith, and respected by outsiders. These are qualities God wants in every believer. The leader must simply be a clear, tested example of them. Peter gives the same vision: elders are to shepherd “not for dishonest gain, but willingly; neither as lording it over those entrusted to you, but making yourselves examples to the flock” (1 Peter 5:2–3).","‘The husband of one wife’ is literally ‘a one-woman man’. The strongest reading is a man who is faithful in marriage — sexually and emotionally devoted to one wife — in a culture where infidelity was common and often accepted. Some read it more narrowly (no remarriage after divorce, or only married men), but Paul himself was unmarried, and the phrase describes character rather than marital status. The same pattern appears in Titus: “if anyone is blameless, the husband of one wife, having children who believe” (Titus 1:6)."]},{"heading":"Servants Who Are Tested","paragraphs":["The second group is διάκονοι, ‘servants’ or deacons. Acts 6 may show their origin, when the apostles asked the church to “select from among you, brothers, seven men of good report, full of the Holy Spirit and of wisdom, whom we may appoint over this business” (Acts 6:3) — the daily distribution of food. Deacons handled practical service and often money, so Paul stresses honesty and freedom from greed: “reverent, not double-tongued, not addicted to much wine, not greedy for money” (v. 8). And they must hold “the mystery of the faith in a pure conscience” (v. 9).","Two principles stand out. First, testing: “Let them also first be tested; then let them serve if they are blameless” (v. 10). Responsibility follows proven faithfulness. Second, verse 11 adds instructions for ‘women’, which the WEB renders “Their wives”; the Greek allows either deacons' wives or women serving as deacons, and the New Testament names Phoebe as “a servant of the assembly that is at Cenchreae” (Romans 16:1). Either way, they must be “reverent, not slanderers, temperate, faithful in all things” (v. 11). The word for ‘slanderers’ is διάβολοι — the same word as ‘devil’. To slander is to take the devil's part.","The reward of humble service is surprising: “For those who have served well gain for themselves a good standing, and great boldness in the faith which is in Christ Jesus” (v. 13). Jesus taught the same reversal: “whoever wants to become great among you shall be your servant” (Mark 10:43). Faithful service in small, practical things builds confidence in God and respect among His people."]},{"heading":"The Household and the Hymn","paragraphs":["Verses 14–15 give the purpose of the whole letter: “that you may know how men ought to behave themselves in God’s house, which is the assembly of the living God, the pillar and ground of the truth.” The church is God's household — a family with a Father and order and responsibilities. It is the assembly of the living God, in contrast to the lifeless idols of Ephesus. And it is the pillar and foundation of the truth: it holds the truth up for the world to see and keeps it firm. Hebrews says the same: “Christ is faithful as a Son over his house; whose house we are” (Hebrews 3:6).","What truth does the church hold? Paul answers with what appears to be an early hymn: “Without controversy, the mystery of godliness is great: God was revealed in the flesh, justified in the spirit, seen by angels, preached among the nations, believed on in the world, and received up in glory” (v. 16). The six lines trace Christ's whole story: incarnation (“The Word became flesh”, John 1:14), resurrection vindication (He “was declared to be the Son of God with power … by the resurrection from the dead”, Romans 1:4), angelic witness, worldwide proclamation, faith, and ascension.","There is a textual question in the first line. Most manuscripts, followed by the WEB, read ‘God was revealed in the flesh’; several of the oldest read ‘He who was revealed in the flesh’. The meaning is the same: the one revealed in the flesh is Christ, and the whole hymn confesses His divine glory. The ‘mystery of godliness’ — the secret source of a godly life — is not a method but a Person. Leadership qualifications and church order (vv. 1–13) exist to protect and display this Christ."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Elders, Deacons and the House of God","paragraphs":["The parallel list in Titus confirms the emphasis on character: “For the overseer must be blameless, as God’s steward; not self-pleasing, not easily angered, not given to wine, not violent, not greedy for dishonest gain” (Titus 1:7), and he must be “holding to the faithful word which is according to the teaching, that he may be able to exhort in the sound doctrine, and to convict those who contradict him” (Titus 1:9). Philippians shows both offices in a local church: “To all the saints in Christ Jesus who are at Philippi, with the overseers and servants” (Philippians 1:1).","Paul's charge to the Ephesian elders, years before this letter, shows the weight of the role: “Take heed, therefore, to yourselves, and to all the flock, in which the Holy Spirit has made you overseers, to shepherd the assembly of the Lord and God which he purchased with his own blood” (Acts 20:28). Peter adds the heart: “Shepherd the flock of God which is among you, exercising the oversight, not under compulsion, but voluntarily” (1 Peter 5:2).","The picture of the church as God's house runs through the New Testament: believers are “built on the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Christ Jesus himself being the chief cornerstone” (Ephesians 2:20), and “as living stones, are built up as a spiritual house, to be a holy priesthood” (1 Peter 2:5). The hymn of verse 16 echoes the Gospels and Acts: the Word became flesh (John 1:14), and at the ascension “he was taken up, and a cloud received him out of their sight” (Acts 1:9)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Overseer / bishop","hebrew":"ἐπίσκοπος","translit":"epískopos","strongs":"G1985","verses":"v. 2","insight":"Strong's: “a superintendent, i.e. Christian officer in genitive case charge of a (or the) church (literally or figuratively).” From ἐπί, over, and σκοπός, a watcher: one who watches over. It gives English ‘episcopal’ and ‘bishop’. In the New Testament it is the same office as elder (Acts 20:17, 28; Titus 1:5, 7). The overseer's job is not to rule like a lord but to watch over the flock like a shepherd. And the qualifications are about character: what he is matters more than what he can do."},{"english":"Hospitable / lover of strangers","hebrew":"φιλόξενος","translit":"philóxenos","strongs":"G5382","verses":"v. 2","insight":"Strong's: “fond of guests, i.e. hospitable.” It combines φίλος, friend, lover, and ξένος, stranger. In the first century, travelling believers depended on Christian homes, and churches met in homes. A leader whose door was closed could not shepherd. Hospitality is not entertaining; it is opening your life and home to people, especially strangers. It is required of leaders and commanded for all believers (Romans 12:13)."},{"english":"Not a lover of money","hebrew":"ἀφιλάργυρος","translit":"aphilárgyros","strongs":"G866","verses":"v. 3","insight":"Strong's: “unavaricious.” Literally ‘not a lover of silver’ (ἀ-, not; φίλος, lover; ἄργυρος, silver). The opposite word, φιλαργυρία, appears in 6:10: ‘the love of money is a root of all kinds of evil’. The false teachers in Ephesus saw godliness as a way to get rich (6:5). Church leaders must be visibly free from that love. A leader who loves money will eventually use the flock instead of feeding it."},{"english":"Devil / slanderer","hebrew":"διάβολος","translit":"diábolos","strongs":"G1228","verses":"v. 6, 7, 11","insight":"Strong's: “a traducer; specially, Satan.” From διαβάλλω, to throw across, to accuse falsely. As a title it means ‘the slanderer’, the accuser of God's people. In verses 6–7 it names the devil; in verse 11 the same word, used as an adjective, describes ‘slanderers’. The connection is deliberate: when believers spread malicious gossip and false accusations, they do the devil's work. Slander is not a small sin of the tongue; it is the devil's own trade."},{"english":"Ground / foundation / support","hebrew":"ἑδραίωμα","translit":"hedraíōma","strongs":"G1477","verses":"v. 15","insight":"Strong's: “a support, i.e. (figuratively) basis.” It appears only here in the New Testament. Related to ἑδραῖος, firm, steadfast (1 Corinthians 15:58). With στῦλος, pillar, it describes the church's role toward the truth: the pillar lifts the truth up for all to see; the foundation holds it steady so that it does not shift. The church does not invent truth; it supports and displays the truth God has revealed in Christ."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: your character at home, your words about others, and your gaze on Christ.","items":[{"title":"Home Is the Test","body":"Paul asks how a man can care for the church if he cannot manage his own household. The Spirit may be pointing you back home. How do you treat your spouse, your children, your parents, your housemates? Are you faithful, gentle, self-controlled and hospitable there, where no one applauds? Ministry that looks impressive in public but is careless at home is not the ministry God wants. Ask Him to make your home the first place your godliness is seen."},{"title":"Not a Slanderer","body":"The word for ‘slanderers’ in verse 11 is the same as ‘devil’. The Spirit may be convicting you about how you speak of others: passing on gossip, painting people in a bad light, sharing half-truths. Ask Him to show you any conversation where you have done the accuser's work. Confess it, and where possible, put it right. Let your words build up rather than tear down."},{"title":"Behold the Mystery","body":"The secret of godliness is Christ: revealed in the flesh, vindicated, seen by angels, proclaimed, believed, taken up in glory (v. 16). The Spirit wants to lift your eyes from rules and performance to the Person. Godliness grows not by trying harder but by beholding Him. Spend time this week simply worshipping Christ through the six lines of this hymn."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Character Check","body":"Read verses 2–7 as a mirror rather than a job description. Mark each quality as strong, growing or weak in your life. Choose one weak area and ask God for help to grow in it this month."},{"title":"The Tongue Check","body":"Think back over the past week. Did you say anything about another person that was unkind, untrue or unnecessary (v. 11)? Write it down, confess it, and decide whether you need to apologise to someone."},{"title":"The Testing Check","body":"Are you serving in your church in some way (v. 10)? If not, ask God where He wants you to start serving faithfully in a small, practical way. If you are, ask whether you are serving well (v. 13)."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Open Your Table","body":"Practise hospitality (v. 2) this week. Invite someone into your home for a meal or a drink — ideally someone new to your church, lonely, or different from you. Keep it simple. Listen to their story, share something of how God has worked in your life, and pray for them before they leave. Afterwards, thank God for the opportunity and ask Him who to invite next."},{"title":"Sing the Mystery","body":"Memorise verse 16. Each day this week, take one line of the hymn and spend five minutes thanking Christ for that part of His work: His incarnation, resurrection, the angels' worship, the gospel reaching the nations, your own faith, and His ascension. On the seventh day, read the whole hymn aloud as a declaration of what the church holds up before the world."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord, thank You for the good work of those who watch over Your people. Raise up leaders in Your church who are above reproach, faithful in marriage, self-controlled, sensible, respectable, hospitable and able to teach; not given to wine, not violent, not quarrelsome, not lovers of money; who manage their homes well and are respected by outsiders. Make me that kind of person, whatever my role. Keep me from pride and from the snare of the devil. Make my words honest and free from slander. Test me and make me faithful in small things, so that I may serve You well and grow in confidence in the faith. Thank You for making Your church the household of the living God, the pillar and foundation of the truth. Help me behave in Your house in a way that holds the truth up high. Great is the mystery of godliness: You were revealed in the flesh, vindicated by the Spirit, seen by angels, proclaimed among the nations, believed on in the world, and taken up in glory. I worship You. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Seven Chosen to Serve","ref":"Acts 6:1-7","relatedVerses":"v. 8-13","summary":"As the Jerusalem church grew, the Greek-speaking widows were being overlooked in the daily distribution of food. The apostles gathered the disciples and said it was not right for them to neglect the word of God to serve tables. They told the church, “Therefore select from among you, brothers, seven men of good report, full of the Holy Spirit and of wisdom, whom we may appoint over this business” (Acts 6:3). The church chose seven men, including Stephen and Philip, and the apostles prayed and laid hands on them. The word of God increased and the number of disciples multiplied. This is the pattern behind 1 Timothy 3:8–13: practical servants of proven character, so that the work of the gospel can flourish."},{"title":"Paul's Charge to the Elders","ref":"Acts 20:17-35","relatedVerses":"v. 1-7","summary":"At Miletus, Paul spoke to the elders of the Ephesian church — the very church Timothy later led. He reminded them of his own example: humble service, tears, teaching publicly and from house to house, and working with his own hands so as not to burden anyone. Then he charged them: “Take heed, therefore, to yourselves, and to all the flock, in which the Holy Spirit has made you overseers, to shepherd the assembly of the Lord and God which he purchased with his own blood” (Acts 20:28). He warned of wolves and of false teachers from within. 1 Timothy 3 gives the qualifications for such shepherds; Acts 20 shows their heart."},{"title":"Eli's Sons and Samuel","ref":"1 Samuel 2:12-36","relatedVerses":"v. 4-5","summary":"Eli was the priest at Shiloh, but his sons Hophni and Phinehas were worthless men who did not know the LORD. They took the best of the sacrifices by force and slept with the women who served at the tent of meeting. Eli rebuked them mildly but did not restrain them, and God sent a prophet to declare judgment on his house. Meanwhile the boy Samuel grew up in the LORD's presence, faithful and trustworthy. The story is a sober illustration of 1 Timothy 3:4–5: a leader who does not manage his own household well brings dishonour on the house of God. Faithfulness at home and faithfulness in God's house belong together."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Timothy","chapter":4,"chapterTitle":"Train Yourself for Godliness — False Asceticism, Good Creation and a Young Leader's Example","themeTitle":"“Exercise Yourself Toward Godliness”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"But the Spirit says expressly that in later times some will fall away from the faith, paying attention to seducing spirits and doctrines of demons,","understanding":"τὸ δὲ πνεῦμα ῥητῶς λέγει, “But the Spirit says expressly” — plainly, unmistakably — that ἐν ὑστέροις καιροῖς, “in later times” (the time family's καιρός: decisive seasons), some ἀποστήσονται τῆς πίστεως, “will fall away from the faith” — the verb behind ‘apostasy’. They will pay attention πνεύμασιν πλάνοις, “to seducing spirits” — spirits that lead astray — καὶ διδασκαλίαις δαιμονίων, “and doctrines of demons.” False teaching is not just human error; behind it are deceiving spiritual powers.","words":[{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G4490","hebrew":"ῥητῶς","translit":"rhētōs","english":"expressly"},{"strongs":"G5305","hebrew":"ὕστερον","translit":"hýsteron","english":"later"},{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"times"},{"strongs":"G868","hebrew":"ἀφίστημι","translit":"aphístēmi","english":"fall away"},{"strongs":"G4108","hebrew":"πλάνος","translit":"plános","english":"seducing"},{"strongs":"G1319","hebrew":"διδασκαλία","translit":"didaskalía","english":"doctrines"},{"strongs":"G1140","hebrew":"δαιμόνιον","translit":"daimónion","english":"demons"}]},{"v":2,"text":"through the hypocrisy of men who speak lies, branded in their own conscience as with a hot iron;","understanding":"The demonic teaching comes ἐν ὑποκρίσει ψευδολόγων, “through the hypocrisy of men who speak lies” — ὑπόκρισις is the actor's mask, playing a part; ψευδολόγοι are liars by profession. Their κεκαυστηριασμένων τὴν ἰδίαν συνείδησιν, “branded in their own conscience as with a hot iron” — καυστηριάζω gives ‘cauterise’: a conscience seared until it no longer feels, or branded like a slave with the mark of their master. In 1:19 some ‘thrust away’ a good conscience; here the conscience is burned dead.","words":[{"strongs":"G5272","hebrew":"ὑπόκρισις","translit":"hypókrisis","english":"hypocrisy"},{"strongs":"G5573","hebrew":"ψευδολόγος","translit":"pseudológos","english":"speak lies"},{"strongs":"G2743","hebrew":"καυτηριάζω","translit":"kautēriázō","english":"branded"},{"strongs":"G4893","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","english":"conscience"}]},{"v":3,"text":"forbidding marriage and commanding to abstain from foods which God created to be received with thanksgiving by those who believe and know the truth.","understanding":"Their teaching: κωλυόντων γαμεῖν, “forbidding marriage,” and ἀπέχεσθαι βρωμάτων, “commanding to abstain from foods.” This was a false spirituality that treated the body and the physical world as unspiritual. But these foods ὁ θεὸς ἔκτισεν, “God created,” εἰς μετάλημψιν μετὰ εὐχαριστίας, “to be received with thanksgiving” — εὐχαριστία from the pray family — by those who are faithful καὶ ἐπεγνωκόσι τὴν ἀλήθειαν, “and know the truth” — ἐπιγινώσκω, the verb of the know family's ἐπίγνωσις: full, recognising knowledge.","words":[{"strongs":"G2967","hebrew":"κωλύω","translit":"kōlýō","english":"forbidding"},{"strongs":"G1060","hebrew":"γαμέω","translit":"gaméō","english":"marriage"},{"strongs":"G567","hebrew":"ἀπέχομαι","translit":"apéchomai","english":"abstain"},{"strongs":"G1033","hebrew":"βρῶμα","translit":"brōma","english":"foods"},{"strongs":"G2936","hebrew":"κτίζω","translit":"ktízō","english":"created"},{"strongs":"G3336","hebrew":"μετάλημψις","translit":"metálēmpsis","english":"received"},{"strongs":"G2169","hebrew":"εὐχαριστία","translit":"eucharistía","english":"thanksgiving"},{"strongs":"G1921","hebrew":"ἐπιγινώσκω","translit":"epiginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"}]},{"v":4,"text":"For every creature of God is good, and nothing is to be rejected, if it is received with thanksgiving.","understanding":"The principle: ὅτι πᾶν κτίσμα θεοῦ καλόν, “For every creature of God is good” — καλός from the good family: beautiful, excellent, as God declared in Genesis 1. καὶ οὐδὲν ἀπόβλητον, “and nothing is to be rejected” — thrown away as unclean — μετὰ εὐχαριστίας λαμβανόμενον, “if it is received with thanksgiving.” Creation is not the enemy; misuse of it is. Gratitude turns ordinary things into acts of worship.","words":[{"strongs":"G2938","hebrew":"κτίσμα","translit":"ktísma","english":"creature"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G579","hebrew":"ἀπόβλητος","translit":"apóblētos","english":"rejected"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"received"},{"strongs":"G2169","hebrew":"εὐχαριστία","translit":"eucharistía","english":"thanksgiving"}]},{"v":5,"text":"For it is sanctified through the word of God and prayer.","understanding":"ἁγιάζεται γὰρ διὰ λόγου θεοῦ καὶ ἐντεύξεως, “For it is sanctified through the word of God and prayer.” ἁγιάζω, to set apart for God. ‘Prayer’ here is ἔντευξις from the pray family — the same word as ‘intercessions’ in 2:1: approaching God with requests. Food is set apart by God's word (which declares it good) and by prayer (giving thanks for it). Every meal can be holy.","words":[{"strongs":"G37","hebrew":"ἁγιάζω","translit":"hagiázō","english":"sanctified"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G1783","hebrew":"ἔντευξις","translit":"énteuxis","english":"prayer"}]},{"v":6,"text":"If you instruct the brothers of these things, you will be a good servant of Christ Jesus, nourished in the words of the faith, and of the good doctrine which you have followed.","understanding":"ταῦτα ὑποτιθέμενος, “If you instruct … these things” — literally ‘laying these things under’, as a foundation — Timothy will be καλὸς διάκονος Χριστοῦ Ἰησοῦ, “a good servant of Christ Jesus” — the servant family's διάκονος and the good family's καλός: an excellent servant. ἐντρεφόμενος, “nourished,” continually fed, in the words of the faith and the καλῆς διδασκαλίας, “good doctrine,” ᾗ παρηκολούθηκας, “which you have followed” — closely followed, like a student following a teacher. You can only feed others what you are being fed.","words":[{"strongs":"G5294","hebrew":"ὑποτίθημι","translit":"hypotíthēmi","english":"instruct"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G1249","hebrew":"διάκονος","translit":"diákonos","english":"servant"},{"strongs":"G1789","hebrew":"ἐντρέφω","translit":"entréphō","english":"nourished"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"words"},{"strongs":"G1319","hebrew":"διδασκαλία","translit":"didaskalía","english":"doctrine"},{"strongs":"G3877","hebrew":"παρακολουθέω","translit":"parakolouthéō","english":"followed"}]},{"v":7,"text":"But refuse profane and old wives’ fables. Exercise yourself toward godliness.","understanding":"τοὺς δὲ βεβήλους καὶ γραώδεις μύθους παραιτοῦ, “But refuse profane and old wives’ fables” — βέβηλος is common, unholy; γραώδης means ‘old-womanish’, a contemptuous Greek term for silly tales — μῦθοι, myths (1:4). Instead: γύμναζε δὲ σεαυτὸν πρὸς εὐσέβειαν, “Exercise yourself toward godliness.” γυμνάζω is the word for training in the gymnasium, where athletes trained naked, stripped of everything that hindered. Godliness requires disciplined training.","words":[{"strongs":"G952","hebrew":"βέβηλος","translit":"bébēlos","english":"profane"},{"strongs":"G1126","hebrew":"γραώδης","translit":"graṓdēs","english":"old wives’"},{"strongs":"G3454","hebrew":"μῦθος","translit":"mŷthos","english":"fables"},{"strongs":"G3868","hebrew":"παραιτέομαι","translit":"paraitéomai","english":"refuse"},{"strongs":"G1128","hebrew":"γυμνάζω","translit":"gymnázō","english":"exercise"},{"strongs":"G2150","hebrew":"εὐσέβεια","translit":"eusébeia","english":"godliness"}]},{"v":8,"text":"For bodily exercise has some value, but godliness has value in all things, having the promise of the life which is now, and of that which is to come.","understanding":"ἡ γὰρ σωματικὴ γυμνασία, “For bodily exercise” — physical training — πρὸς ὀλίγον ἐστὶν ὠφέλιμος, “has some value,” is useful for a little (for a short time or to a small extent). ἡ δὲ εὐσέβεια πρὸς πάντα ὠφέλιμός ἐστιν, “but godliness has value in all things,” ἐπαγγελίαν ἔχουσα ζωῆς τῆς νῦν καὶ τῆς μελλούσης, “having the promise of the life (ζωή, from the life family: life itself) which is now, and of that which is to come.” Physical training helps this life; godliness helps this life and the next.","words":[{"strongs":"G4984","hebrew":"σωματικός","translit":"sōmatikós","english":"bodily"},{"strongs":"G1129","hebrew":"γυμνασία","translit":"gymnasía","english":"exercise"},{"strongs":"G5624","hebrew":"ὠφέλιμος","translit":"ōphélimos","english":"value"},{"strongs":"G2150","hebrew":"εὐσέβεια","translit":"eusébeia","english":"godliness"},{"strongs":"G1860","hebrew":"ἐπαγγελία","translit":"epangelía","english":"promise"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G3568","hebrew":"νῦν","translit":"nŷn","english":"now"},{"strongs":"G3195","hebrew":"μέλλω","translit":"méllō","english":"to come"}]},{"v":9,"text":"This saying is faithful and worthy of all acceptance.","understanding":"The third ‘faithful saying’: πιστὸς ὁ λόγος καὶ πάσης ἀποδοχῆς ἄξιος, “This saying is faithful and worthy of all acceptance” — the same formula as 1:15. Scholars debate whether it points back to verse 8 (godliness has value for both lives) or forward to verse 10; either way it marks a truth worth building life on.","words":[{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"saying"},{"strongs":"G594","hebrew":"ἀποδοχή","translit":"apodochḗ","english":"acceptance"}]},{"v":10,"text":"For to this end we both labor and suffer reproach, because we have set our trust in the living God, who is the Savior of all men, especially of those who believe.","understanding":"εἰς τοῦτο γὰρ κοπιῶμεν, “For to this end we both labor” — κοπιάω from the work family: toil to weariness — καὶ ὀνειδιζόμεθα, “and suffer reproach” (the WEB follows manuscripts with this word; others read ἀγωνιζόμεθα, ‘strive’). Why? ὅτι ἠλπίκαμεν ἐπὶ θεῷ ζῶντι, “because we have set our trust (hope) in the living God,” ὅς ἐστιν σωτὴρ πάντων ἀνθρώπων, μάλιστα πιστῶν, “who is the Savior of all men, especially of those who believe.” God is Saviour of all in that He preserves all life and offers salvation to all (2:4); He is Saviour ‘especially’ of believers, who receive it.","words":[{"strongs":"G2872","hebrew":"κοπιάω","translit":"kopiáō","english":"labor"},{"strongs":"G3679","hebrew":"ὀνειδίζω","translit":"oneidízō","english":"suffer reproach"},{"strongs":"G1679","hebrew":"ἐλπίζω","translit":"elpízō","english":"set our trust"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"living"},{"strongs":"G4990","hebrew":"σωτήρ","translit":"sōtḗr","english":"Savior"},{"strongs":"G3122","hebrew":"μάλιστα","translit":"málista","english":"especially"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"believe"}]},{"v":11,"text":"Command and teach these things.","understanding":"παράγγελλε ταῦτα καὶ δίδασκε, “Command and teach these things.” Two present imperatives: keep commanding, keep teaching. παραγγέλλω, the military word for passing on orders (1:3), and διδάσκω, to teach. Timothy is not to suggest; he is to instruct with authority.","words":[{"strongs":"G3853","hebrew":"παραγγέλλω","translit":"parangéllō","english":"Command"},{"strongs":"G1321","hebrew":"διδάσκω","translit":"didáskō","english":"teach"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Let no man despise your youth; but be an example to those who believe, in word, in your way of life, in love, in spirit, in faith, and in purity.","understanding":"μηδείς σου τῆς νεότητος καταφρονείτω, “Let no man despise your youth” — καταφρονέω means to look down on. Timothy was probably in his thirties — young for leadership in that culture, where elders were respected for age. The answer is not to demand respect but to earn it: τύπος γίνου τῶν πιστῶν, “be an example (τύπος, a stamp, a pattern) to those who believe.” Five areas: ἐν λόγῳ, “in word” (λόγος, speech); ἐν ἀναστροφῇ, “in your way of life” (conduct); ἐν ἀγάπῃ, “in love”; ἐν πνεύματι, “in spirit” (in some manuscripts); ἐν πίστει, “in faith”; ἐν ἁγνείᾳ, “in purity” — moral and sexual purity.","words":[{"strongs":"G3503","hebrew":"νεότης","translit":"neótēs","english":"youth"},{"strongs":"G2706","hebrew":"καταφρονέω","translit":"kataphronéō","english":"despise"},{"strongs":"G5179","hebrew":"τύπος","translit":"týpos","english":"example"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G391","hebrew":"ἀναστροφή","translit":"anastrophḗ","english":"way of life"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G47","hebrew":"ἁγνεία","translit":"hagneía","english":"purity"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Until I come, pay attention to reading, to exhortation, and to teaching.","understanding":"ἕως ἔρχομαι, “Until I come,” πρόσεχε, “pay attention,” devote yourself, τῇ ἀναγνώσει, “to reading” — the public reading of Scripture in the gathering, as in the synagogue (Acts 13:15); τῇ παρακλήσει, “to exhortation” — applying the Scripture, urging and encouraging; τῇ διδασκαλίᾳ, “to teaching” — explaining its truth. Read, exhort, teach: the pattern of a Bible-centred church.","words":[{"strongs":"G4337","hebrew":"προσέχω","translit":"proséchō","english":"pay attention"},{"strongs":"G320","hebrew":"ἀνάγνωσις","translit":"anágnōsis","english":"reading"},{"strongs":"G3874","hebrew":"παράκλησις","translit":"paráklēsis","english":"exhortation"},{"strongs":"G1319","hebrew":"διδασκαλία","translit":"didaskalía","english":"teaching"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Don’t neglect the gift that is in you, which was given to you by prophecy, with the laying on of the hands of the elders.","understanding":"μὴ ἀμέλει τοῦ ἐν σοὶ χαρίσματος, “Don’t neglect the gift that is in you” — ἀμελέω means to be careless about. χάρισμα is a grace-gift (from χάρις). It was given διὰ προφητείας, “by prophecy,” μετὰ ἐπιθέσεως τῶν χειρῶν τοῦ πρεσβυτερίου, “with the laying on of the hands of the elders” (v. 14) — the body of elders. 2 Timothy 1:6 mentions Paul's hands too. God gave the gift; Timothy must not let it lie unused.","words":[{"strongs":"G272","hebrew":"ἀμελέω","translit":"ameléō","english":"neglect"},{"strongs":"G5486","hebrew":"χάρισμα","translit":"chárisma","english":"gift"},{"strongs":"G4394","hebrew":"προφητεία","translit":"prophēteía","english":"prophecy"},{"strongs":"G1936","hebrew":"ἐπίθεσις","translit":"epíthesis","english":"laying on"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hands"},{"strongs":"G4244","hebrew":"πρεσβυτέριον","translit":"presbytérion","english":"elders"}]},{"v":15,"text":"Be diligent in these things. Give yourself wholly to them, that your progress may be revealed to all.","understanding":"ταῦτα μελέτα, “Be diligent in these things” — μελετάω means to give careful thought and practice to, to work at. ἐν τούτοις ἴσθι, “Give yourself wholly to them” — literally ‘be in them’, be absorbed. The goal: ἵνα σου ἡ προκοπὴ φανερὰ ᾖ πᾶσιν, “that your progress may be revealed to all.” προκοπή was used of an army cutting its way forward. People will not despise a young leader whose growth they can see.","words":[{"strongs":"G3191","hebrew":"μελετάω","translit":"meletáō","english":"Be diligent"},{"strongs":"G4297","hebrew":"προκοπή","translit":"prokopḗ","english":"progress"},{"strongs":"G5318","hebrew":"φανερός","translit":"phanerós","english":"revealed"}]},{"v":16,"text":"Pay attention to yourself, and to your teaching. Continue in these things, for in doing this you will save both yourself and those who hear you.","understanding":"ἔπεχε σεαυτῷ καὶ τῇ διδασκαλίᾳ, “Pay attention to yourself, and to your teaching” — life and doctrine, in that order. ἐπίμενε αὐτοῖς, “Continue in these things,” persevere. “For in doing this you will save (σώσεις) both yourself and those who hear (ἀκούοντάς) you.” Not that Timothy earns salvation, but that persevering in sound life and teaching is how God keeps him and his hearers in the faith. A leader who watches his life and his teaching becomes God's instrument of salvation for others.","words":[{"strongs":"G1907","hebrew":"ἐπέχω","translit":"epéchō","english":"Pay attention"},{"strongs":"G1319","hebrew":"διδασκαλία","translit":"didaskalía","english":"teaching"},{"strongs":"G1961","hebrew":"ἐπιμένω","translit":"epiménō","english":"Continue"},{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"save"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"hear"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Branded Conscience","verseRef":"v. 1-2","image":"Picture a blacksmith heating an iron rod until it glows white. He presses it onto a man's skin, and the flesh sizzles and scars. Months later the scar is hard and numb; you could press a needle into it and the man would feel nothing. Now picture a teacher who once felt the sting of conscience when he twisted Scripture for his own ends. He pushed the feeling away again and again. Now he can stand in front of a crowd and tell polished lies about God without a flicker of discomfort.","meaning":"Paul warns of those who teach “through the hypocrisy of men who speak lies, branded in their own conscience as with a hot iron” (v. 2). A conscience can be seared until it no longer warns. It does not happen in one day; it happens through repeated choices to ignore its voice. This is why Paul keeps urging a ‘good conscience’ (1:5, 19; 3:9). Guard your sensitivity to the Spirit's conviction; a tender conscience is a gift."},{"title":"Grace at the Table","verseRef":"v. 3-5","image":"Picture two tables in Ephesus. At the first, a group of ‘spiritual’ teachers refuse meat and wine and look down on anyone who marries, as if the body and the physical world were dirty. Their faces are stern, their food is plain, and their spirits are proud. At the second table, a Christian family gathers for an ordinary meal. The father takes the bread and says, ‘Thank You, Father, for this good gift.’ The children laugh, the food is shared with a poor neighbour, and the meal becomes worship.","meaning":"The false teachers were “forbidding marriage and commanding to abstain from foods which God created to be received with thanksgiving” (v. 3). Paul answers: “For every creature of God is good, and nothing is to be rejected, if it is received with thanksgiving. For it is sanctified through the word of God and prayer” (vv. 4–5). True spirituality does not despise God's creation; it receives it gratefully and uses it for His glory. Marriage, food and the body are gifts. Gratitude turns them into worship."},{"title":"The Spiritual Gymnasium","verseRef":"v. 7-8","image":"Picture an ancient gymnasium at dawn. Young men are running laps, wrestling, lifting stones and stretching. Their trainers shout instructions. Their bodies grow stronger month by month. Now picture another kind of gymnasium: a believer rising early to read Scripture and pray, choosing to forgive a colleague, fasting from a habit, serving someone in secret, guarding the tongue in a heated moment. No one cheers. But day by day, a different kind of strength is growing — one that will last for ever.","meaning":"“Exercise yourself toward godliness. For bodily exercise has some value, but godliness has value in all things, having the promise of the life which is now, and of that which is to come” (vv. 7–8). The word γυμνάζω gives us ‘gymnasium’. Godliness does not grow by accident; it grows by training, by repeated, disciplined practice empowered by grace. Physical fitness is good, but it is temporary. Spiritual fitness benefits this life and the next."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Doctrines of Demons (v. 1-5)","body":"The Spirit clearly says that in later times some will abandon the faith, following deceiving spirits and teachings of demons through liars with seared consciences, who forbid marriage and require abstinence from foods that God created to be received with thanksgiving; everything God created is good and is made holy by the word of God and prayer."},{"label":"A Good Servant Trains in Godliness (v. 6-10)","body":"By teaching these things Timothy will be a good servant of Christ, nourished on the words of faith; he must reject profane myths and train himself for godliness, which is valuable in every way, holding promise for this life and the next; for this believers labour and suffer reproach, trusting the living God, the Saviour of all, especially of believers."},{"label":"An Example Despite Youth (v. 11-16)","body":"Timothy must command and teach these things, let no one despise his youth but set an example in speech, conduct, love, faith and purity; devote himself to public reading of Scripture, exhortation and teaching; not neglect his gift; give himself wholly to these things so all may see his progress; and watch his life and doctrine closely, for in doing so he will save himself and his hearers."}],"sections":[{"heading":"When Spirituality Despises Creation","paragraphs":["Chapter 4 opens with a warning that the false teaching in Ephesus was not merely human error: “But the Spirit says expressly that in later times some will fall away from the faith, paying attention to seducing spirits and doctrines of demons” (v. 1). ‘Later times’ in the New Testament describes the whole period between Christ's first and second comings, not only the final days; Paul sees the prophecy already being fulfilled in Ephesus. Behind false teachers stand deceiving spirits. Jesus warned, “Beware of false prophets, who come to you in sheep’s clothing, but inwardly are ravening wolves” (Matthew 7:15).","The human agents speak lies with masks of piety, “branded in their own conscience as with a hot iron” (v. 2). And their teaching was a form of super-spirituality: “forbidding marriage and commanding to abstain from foods” (v. 3). It sounds holy to renounce marriage and certain foods, but it rested on the idea that the physical world is inferior or evil. Paul had faced something similar in Colossae, where teachers said, “‘Don’t handle, nor taste, nor touch’” (Colossians 2:21), rules that “aren’t of any value against the indulgence of the flesh” (Colossians 2:23).","Paul's answer goes back to creation: these foods God “created to be received with thanksgiving by those who believe and know the truth. For every creature of God is good, and nothing is to be rejected, if it is received with thanksgiving. For it is sanctified through the word of God and prayer” (vv. 3–5). Jesus Himself declared all foods clean (Mark 7:19), and Peter heard, “What God has cleansed, you must not call unclean” (Acts 10:15). Marriage too is God's good gift: “Let marriage be held in honor among all” (Hebrews 13:4). True holiness is not the rejection of God's gifts but their grateful use."]},{"heading":"Training for Godliness","paragraphs":["Timothy's task is to teach these things, and in doing so he will be “a good servant of Christ Jesus, nourished in the words of the faith, and of the good doctrine which you have followed” (v. 6). A leader feeds others only what he is feeding on himself. So Paul gives two commands: reject myths, and train in godliness. “But refuse profane and old wives’ fables. Exercise yourself toward godliness” (v. 7). The athletic image is deliberate: godliness requires discipline, effort and endurance, empowered by grace.","Paul compares physical and spiritual training: “For bodily exercise has some value, but godliness has value in all things, having the promise of the life which is now, and of that which is to come” (v. 8). He does not despise physical exercise — he often uses athletic pictures, as in “Every man who strives in the games exercises self-control in all things. Now they do it to receive a corruptible crown, but we an incorruptible” (1 Corinthians 9:25). But he puts it in perspective. The body is temporary; godliness pays dividends now and for ever.","This is why Christian ministry involves toil and suffering: “For to this end we both labor and suffer reproach, because we have set our trust in the living God, who is the Savior of all men, especially of those who believe” (v. 10). The phrase ‘Saviour of all … especially of believers’ does not mean everyone will be saved; it means that God is the preserver and would-be Saviour of all people (2:4), and in a special and saving sense the Saviour of those who believe."]},{"heading":"An Example Despite Youth","paragraphs":["Then Paul turns personal: “Let no man despise your youth; but be an example to those who believe, in word, in your way of life, in love, in spirit, in faith, and in purity” (v. 12). Timothy may have felt intimidated by older men in the church and by confident false teachers. Paul's answer is not to demand respect but to earn it by example. The areas are comprehensive: what he says, how he lives, how he loves, his faith and his purity. Paul gave the same instruction to Titus: “in all things showing yourself an example of good works” (Titus 2:7).","Next, priorities: “Until I come, pay attention to reading, to exhortation, and to teaching” (v. 13). The public reading of Scripture, followed by exhortation and teaching, was the heart of the gathering, just as in the synagogue, where “After the reading of the law and the prophets” the leaders asked for “any word of exhortation for the people” (Acts 13:15). And gifts: “Don’t neglect the gift that is in you, which was given to you by prophecy, with the laying on of the hands of the elders” (v. 14). Paul later reminded him to “stir up the gift of God which is in you” (2 Timothy 1:6).","Finally, perseverance: “Be diligent in these things. Give yourself wholly to them, that your progress may be revealed to all. Pay attention to yourself, and to your teaching. Continue in these things, for in doing this you will save both yourself and those who hear you” (vv. 15–16). Life first, then teaching. A leader can teach correct doctrine and still shipwreck his life; or live well while teaching error. Both must be watched. God uses a leader who perseveres in both as an instrument of salvation for others."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Creation, Discipline and Example","paragraphs":["The New Testament repeatedly rejects false asceticism. Jesus said that food “doesn’t go into his heart, but into his stomach” (Mark 7:19), declaring all foods clean. Peter was told, “What God has cleansed, you must not call unclean” (Acts 10:15). Paul taught that both the one who eats and the one who abstains do so to the Lord: “He who eats, eats to the Lord, for he gives God thanks” (Romans 14:6). And Hebrews insists, “Let marriage be held in honor among all, and let the bed be undefiled” (Hebrews 13:4).","Yet the New Testament also calls for real discipline. Paul writes of himself, “but I beat my body and bring it into submission, lest by any means, after I have preached to others, I myself should be rejected” (1 Corinthians 9:27). The difference is the motive: false asceticism tries to earn holiness by rejecting creation; true discipline trains a redeemed person to use God's gifts rightly and grow in godliness.","Timothy's calling and reputation appear in Acts: “The brothers who were at Lystra and Iconium gave a good testimony about him” (Acts 16:2). Paul later urged him to “stir up the gift of God which is in you through the laying on of my hands” (2 Timothy 1:6). And Titus received the same charge to be an example: “in all things showing yourself an example of good works; in your teaching showing integrity, seriousness, incorruptibility” (Titus 2:7)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Sear / brand / cauterise","hebrew":"καυτηριάζω","translit":"kautēriázō","strongs":"G2743","verses":"v. 2","insight":"Strong's: “to brand (‘cauterize’), i.e. (by implication) to render unsensitive (figuratively).” It appears only here in the New Testament. It can picture a conscience so burned that it no longer feels, or a conscience branded with the mark of its master, as slaves were branded. Either way, those who repeatedly silence their conscience can reach a point where lies no longer trouble them. Keep your conscience tender by responding quickly when the Spirit convicts."},{"english":"Creature / created thing","hebrew":"κτίσμα","translit":"ktísma","strongs":"G2938","verses":"v. 4","insight":"Strong's: “an original formation (concretely), i.e. product (created thing).” From κτίζω, to create. Paul says every κτίσμα of God is καλός, good — echoing Genesis 1, where God saw that everything He made was good. False teachers who forbade marriage and foods treated parts of creation as unspiritual. Paul insists that nothing God made is to be rejected when received with thanksgiving. The problem is never the gift; it is how we use it."},{"english":"Exercise / train","hebrew":"γυμνάζω","translit":"gymnázō","strongs":"G1128","verses":"v. 7","insight":"Strong's: “to practise naked (in the games), i.e. train (figuratively).” From γυμνός, naked, because Greek athletes trained unclothed, removing everything that hindered movement. It gives us ‘gymnasium’ and ‘gymnastics’. Hebrews uses it for believers ‘trained’ by God's discipline (Hebrews 12:11). Godliness requires stripping off hindrances and repeated, disciplined practice. Grace does not make effort unnecessary; it makes godly effort possible and fruitful."},{"english":"Godliness","hebrew":"εὐσέβεια","translit":"eusébeia","strongs":"G2150","verses":"v. 7, 8","insight":"Strong's: “piety; specially, the gospel scheme.” The key word of 1 Timothy. In verses 7–8 it is something one trains for, and it has value ‘in all things’, carrying promise for this life and the next. True godliness is not outward religion — the false teachers had plenty of that — but a life of reverent devotion to God shaped by the truth of Christ (3:16). It is worth more than any physical fitness."},{"english":"Example / pattern","hebrew":"τύπος","translit":"týpos","strongs":"G5179","verses":"v. 12","insight":"Strong's: “a die (as struck), i.e. (by implication) a stamp or scar; by analogy, a shape, i.e. a statue, (figuratively) style or resemblance; specially, a sampler (‘type’), i.e. a model (for imitation) or instance (for warning).” Originally the mark left by a blow, like a seal pressed into wax. A leader's life leaves an impression on others. The Thessalonians became a τύπος to all believers (1 Thessalonians 1:7); Paul made himself a τύπος (2 Thessalonians 3:9). Timothy is to be one in word, conduct, love, faith and purity. Your life is shaping someone."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: the tenderness of your conscience, the shape of your spiritual training, and the example you are setting.","items":[{"title":"A Tender Conscience","body":"False teachers had seared consciences. The Spirit wants to keep yours tender. Is there an area where you have grown numb to conviction — a habit you no longer feel bad about, a compromise that once troubled you? Ask Him to restore sensitivity. When He brings conviction, respond quickly. A tender conscience is not weakness; it is spiritual health."},{"title":"Train, Don't Just Try","body":"Godliness grows through training: daily, deliberate, grace-empowered practice. The Spirit may be showing you that your spiritual life has been more wishing than training. What spiritual exercises would strengthen you — regular Scripture reading, prayer, fasting, generosity, confession, service? Ask Him to help you build one new practice this month, as a trainee in His gymnasium."},{"title":"Someone Is Watching","body":"Like Timothy, you may feel too young, too new or too unimportant to influence anyone. But someone is watching your life: a child, a younger believer, a colleague. The Spirit asks: what impression is your life leaving? Ask Him to make you an example in speech, conduct, love, faith and purity, so that others see Christ in you."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Gratitude Check","body":"Do you receive God's ordinary gifts — food, rest, marriage, health — with thanksgiving (vv. 3–5)? Or do you either despise them or take them for granted? Choose to give thanks deliberately before every meal this week."},{"title":"The Training Check","body":"Compare the time you give to physical or entertainment pursuits with the time you give to training in godliness (vv. 7–8). Write down one realistic change to rebalance it."},{"title":"The Example Check","body":"Rate yourself honestly in the five areas of verse 12: speech, conduct, love, faith, purity. Which one most needs growth? Ask God for help in that area and name one practical step."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"A Thirty-Day Godliness Plan","body":"Design a simple training plan for thirty days. Choose three exercises: one for the word (for example, reading one chapter a day), one for prayer (for example, ten minutes each morning), and one for character (for example, a daily act of hidden kindness, or guarding your tongue in one specific situation). Track it like an athlete tracks training. At the end, write down what has changed in you and what you will continue."},{"title":"Read, Exhort, Teach at Home","body":"Apply verse 13 at home or in a small group. Once this week, read a passage of Scripture aloud (reading), share one way it should change how you live (exhortation), and explain one truth it teaches (teaching). Keep it short and simple. Invite others to respond. Make it a weekly habit."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Holy Spirit, You have spoken clearly that some will fall away, following deceiving spirits and teachings of demons. Keep me from every lie, however spiritual it sounds. Keep my conscience tender and never let it be seared. Thank You that everything You created is good, and that nothing is to be rejected when it is received with thanksgiving. Let me receive Your gifts gratefully and sanctify them by Your word and prayer. Make me a good servant of Christ Jesus, nourished on the words of faith and good teaching. Help me refuse empty myths and train myself for godliness, which is valuable for this life and the life to come. I set my hope on You, the living God, the Saviour of all, especially of those who believe. Whatever my age or weakness, make me an example in speech, conduct, love, faith and purity. Let me not neglect the gift You have given me. Help me give myself fully to what You have called me to do, watch my life and my teaching closely, and persevere, so that I and those who hear me may be saved. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Daniel and the King's Food","ref":"Daniel 1:1-21","relatedVerses":"v. 7-8, 12","summary":"As a young exile in Babylon, Daniel was chosen to be trained for the king's service and offered the king's rich food and wine. He resolved not to defile himself and asked to be given vegetables and water instead. After ten days, he and his friends looked healthier than the others, and God gave them knowledge and understanding. Daniel's refusal was not a rejection of God's creation but faithfulness to God's law and refusal to compromise with an idolatrous court. He shows what 1 Timothy 4 calls for: a young person disciplined in godliness, setting an example in conduct and faith, whose progress was evident to all."},{"title":"Peter's Vision of the Sheet","ref":"Acts 10:9-16","relatedVerses":"v. 3-5","summary":"On a rooftop in Joppa, Peter fell into a trance and saw heaven opened and a great sheet let down, full of all kinds of animals, reptiles and birds. A voice told him to kill and eat. Peter refused, saying he had never eaten anything common or unclean. “A voice came to him again the second time, ‘What God has cleansed, you must not call unclean’” (Acts 10:15). This happened three times. The vision prepared Peter to welcome the Gentile Cornelius into the church, and it confirmed that the food laws no longer divided God's people. 1 Timothy 4:4 states the same truth: every creature of God is good, and nothing is to be rejected if received with thanksgiving."},{"title":"Young Samuel Who Listened","ref":"1 Samuel 3:1-21","relatedVerses":"v. 12, 15-16","summary":"In a time when the word of the LORD was rare, the boy Samuel served in the tabernacle under the old priest Eli. One night the LORD called Samuel by name three times, and Samuel thought it was Eli. The fourth time, following Eli's advice, he answered, ‘Speak, for your servant hears.’ God gave him a hard message about Eli's house, and Samuel told it faithfully. The LORD was with Samuel as he grew, and let none of his words fall to the ground; all Israel recognised him as a prophet. Like Timothy, Samuel was young, yet his faithfulness made his progress evident to all, and no one despised his youth."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Timothy","chapter":5,"chapterTitle":"The Church as Family — Widows, Elders and Purity Without Partiality","themeTitle":"“Honor Widows Who Are Widows Indeed”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Don’t rebuke an older man, but exhort him as a father; the younger men as brothers;","understanding":"The church is a family, so speak to its members as family. πρεσβυτέρῳ μὴ ἐπιπλήξῃς, “Don’t rebuke an older man” — ἐπιπλήσσω means to strike at with words, to scold harshly — ἀλλὰ παρακάλει ὡς πατέρα, “but exhort him as a father” — appeal, encourage, with respect. νεωτέρους ὡς ἀδελφούς, “the younger men as brothers” — νεώτερος is the comparative of νέος from the new family: newer, younger in time. A young leader corrects older people with honour and younger people as equals.","words":[{"strongs":"G4245","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","english":"older man"},{"strongs":"G1969","hebrew":"ἐπιπλήσσω","translit":"epiplḗssō","english":"rebuke"},{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"exhort"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"father"},{"strongs":"G3501","hebrew":"νέος","translit":"néos","english":"younger men"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brothers"}]},{"v":2,"text":"the elder women as mothers; the younger as sisters, in all purity.","understanding":"πρεσβυτέρας ὡς μητέρας, “the elder women as mothers”; νεωτέρας ὡς ἀδελφάς, “the younger as sisters,” ἐν πάσῃ ἁγνείᾳ, “in all purity.” The family picture protects relationships: treating young women as sisters guards against any hint of impropriety. A young pastor must be above suspicion.","words":[{"strongs":"G4245","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","english":"elder women"},{"strongs":"G3384","hebrew":"μήτηρ","translit":"mḗtēr","english":"mothers"},{"strongs":"G3501","hebrew":"νέος","translit":"néos","english":"younger"},{"strongs":"G79","hebrew":"ἀδελφή","translit":"adelphḗ","english":"sisters"},{"strongs":"G47","hebrew":"ἁγνεία","translit":"hagneía","english":"purity"}]},{"v":3,"text":"Honor widows who are widows indeed.","understanding":"χήρας τίμα, “Honor widows” — τιμάω means to value, honour, and here clearly includes financial support (as ‘honour’ includes pay in v. 17). But specifically τὰς ὄντως χήρας, “who are widows indeed” — truly without support. In the ancient world a widow without family was among the most vulnerable people. Caring for widows was one of the earliest marks of the church (Acts 6:1).","words":[{"strongs":"G5503","hebrew":"χήρα","translit":"chḗra","english":"widows"},{"strongs":"G5091","hebrew":"τιμάω","translit":"timáō","english":"Honor"},{"strongs":"G3689","hebrew":"ὄντως","translit":"óntōs","english":"indeed"}]},{"v":4,"text":"But if any widow has children or grandchildren, let them learn first to show piety towards their own family, and to repay their parents, for this is acceptable in the sight of God.","understanding":"If a widow has τέκνα ἢ ἔκγονα, “children or grandchildren,” they must μανθανέτωσαν πρῶτον τὸν ἴδιον οἶκον εὐσεβεῖν, “learn first to show piety towards their own family.” εὐσεβέω is the verb of the godliness family: godliness begins at home, in caring for parents. And ἀμοιβὰς ἀποδιδόναι τοῖς προγόνοις, “to repay their parents” — giving back what was received in childhood. “For this is acceptable (ἀπόδεκτον) in the sight of God.”","words":[{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"children"},{"strongs":"G1549","hebrew":"ἔκγονον","translit":"ékgonon","english":"grandchildren"},{"strongs":"G3129","hebrew":"μανθάνω","translit":"manthánō","english":"learn"},{"strongs":"G2151","hebrew":"εὐσεβέω","translit":"eusebéō","english":"show piety"},{"strongs":"G287","hebrew":"ἀμοιβή","translit":"amoibḗ","english":"repay"},{"strongs":"G591","hebrew":"ἀποδίδωμι","translit":"apodídōmi","english":"repay"},{"strongs":"G4269","hebrew":"πρόγονος","translit":"prógonos","english":"parents"},{"strongs":"G587","hebrew":"ἀπόδεκτος","translit":"apódektos","english":"acceptable"}]},{"v":5,"text":"Now she who is a widow indeed, and desolate, has her hope set on God, and continues in petitions and prayers night and day.","understanding":"The real widow: μεμονωμένη, “desolate” — left alone, isolated — ἤλπικεν ἐπὶ θεόν, “has her hope set on God,” and προσμένει ταῖς δεήσεσιν καὶ ταῖς προσευχαῖς, “continues in petitions and prayers” — two words from the pray family, as in 2:1: specific requests and worshipful prayer — νυκτὸς καὶ ἡμέρας, “night and day.” Like Anna in the temple (Luke 2:37), she makes prayer her life's work.","words":[{"strongs":"G3443","hebrew":"μονόω","translit":"monóō","english":"desolate"},{"strongs":"G1679","hebrew":"ἐλπίζω","translit":"elpízō","english":"has her hope set"},{"strongs":"G4357","hebrew":"προσμένω","translit":"prosménō","english":"continues"},{"strongs":"G1162","hebrew":"δέησις","translit":"déēsis","english":"petitions"},{"strongs":"G4335","hebrew":"προσευχή","translit":"proseuchḗ","english":"prayers"}]},{"v":6,"text":"But she who gives herself to pleasure is dead while she lives.","understanding":"ἡ δὲ σπαταλῶσα, “But she who gives herself to pleasure” — σπαταλάω means to live luxuriously, self-indulgently — ζῶσα τέθνηκεν, “is dead while she lives.” A striking contrast with the life family: physically alive (ζάω), spiritually dead (θνήσκω). Self-indulgence is a slow spiritual death.","words":[{"strongs":"G4684","hebrew":"σπαταλάω","translit":"spataláō","english":"gives herself to pleasure"},{"strongs":"G2348","hebrew":"θνήσκω","translit":"thnḗskō","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"lives"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Also command these things, that they may be without reproach.","understanding":"καὶ ταῦτα παράγγελλε, “Also command these things” — the military word again — ἵνα ἀνεπίλημπτοι ὦσιν, “that they may be without reproach” — the same quality required of overseers (3:2). The goal is a church whose care for widows gives outsiders nothing to criticise.","words":[{"strongs":"G3853","hebrew":"παραγγέλλω","translit":"parangéllō","english":"command"},{"strongs":"G423","hebrew":"ἀνεπίληπτος","translit":"anepílēptos","english":"without reproach"}]},{"v":8,"text":"But if anyone doesn’t provide for his own, and especially his own household, he has denied the faith, and is worse than an unbeliever.","understanding":"A strong statement: “But if anyone doesn’t provide for (προνοεῖ — think ahead for) his own, and especially (μάλιστα) his own household (οἰκείων), he has denied (ἤρνηται) the faith, and is worse (χείρων) than an unbeliever (ἀπίστου).” Even pagans cared for their elderly parents. A believer who neglects family has contradicted the faith by his actions, whatever his words.","words":[{"strongs":"G4306","hebrew":"προνοέω","translit":"pronoéō","english":"provide"},{"strongs":"G2398","hebrew":"ἴδιος","translit":"ídios","english":"own"},{"strongs":"G3122","hebrew":"μάλιστα","translit":"málista","english":"especially"},{"strongs":"G3609","hebrew":"οἰκεῖος","translit":"oikeîos","english":"household"},{"strongs":"G720","hebrew":"ἀρνέομαι","translit":"arnéomai","english":"denied"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G5501","hebrew":"χείρων","translit":"cheírōn","english":"worse"},{"strongs":"G571","hebrew":"ἄπιστος","translit":"ápistos","english":"unbeliever"}]},{"v":9,"text":"Let no one be enrolled as a widow under sixty years old, having been the wife of one man,","understanding":"χήρα καταλεγέσθω, “Let … be enrolled as a widow” — καταλέγω, to put on a list. There seems to have been an official list of widows supported by the church and perhaps committed to serve it in prayer and good works. The conditions: μὴ ἔλαττον ἐτῶν ἑξήκοντα, “not under sixty years old,” γεγονυῖα ἑνὸς ἀνδρὸς γυνή, “having been the wife of one man” — a ‘one-man woman’, faithful in marriage, the mirror of the overseer's qualification (3:2).","words":[{"strongs":"G2639","hebrew":"καταλέγω","translit":"katalégō","english":"enrolled"},{"strongs":"G5503","hebrew":"χήρα","translit":"chḗra","english":"widow"},{"strongs":"G2094","hebrew":"ἔτος","translit":"étos","english":"years"},{"strongs":"G1835","hebrew":"ἑξήκοντα","translit":"hexḗkonta","english":"sixty"},{"strongs":"G1520","hebrew":"εἷς","translit":"heîs","english":"one"},{"strongs":"G435","hebrew":"ἀνήρ","translit":"anḗr","english":"man"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"wife"}]},{"v":10,"text":"being approved by good works, if she has brought up children, if she has been hospitable to strangers, if she has washed the saints’ feet, if she has relieved the afflicted, and if she has diligently followed every good work.","understanding":"Her reputation: ἐν ἔργοις καλοῖς μαρτυρουμένη, “being approved by good works” — καλός from the good family: beautiful, noble works. Five examples: ἐτεκνοτρόφησεν, “brought up children”; ἐξενοδόχησεν, “been hospitable to strangers”; ἁγίων πόδας ἔνιψεν, “washed the saints’ feet” — humble service, as Jesus did (John 13); θλιβομένοις ἐπήρκεσεν, “relieved the afflicted”; παντὶ ἔργῳ ἀγαθῷ ἐπηκολούθησεν, “diligently followed every good work.” Both words of the good family in one verse: καλός (noble, admirable) and ἀγαθός (beneficial, doing good).","words":[{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G3140","hebrew":"μαρτυρέω","translit":"martyréō","english":"approved"},{"strongs":"G5044","hebrew":"τεκνοτροφέω","translit":"teknotrophéō","english":"brought up children"},{"strongs":"G3580","hebrew":"ξενοδοχέω","translit":"xenodochéō","english":"been hospitable"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"},{"strongs":"G4228","hebrew":"πούς","translit":"poús","english":"feet"},{"strongs":"G3538","hebrew":"νίπτω","translit":"níptō","english":"washed"},{"strongs":"G2346","hebrew":"θλίβω","translit":"thlíbō","english":"afflicted"},{"strongs":"G1884","hebrew":"ἐπαρκέω","translit":"eparkéō","english":"relieved"},{"strongs":"G18","hebrew":"ἀγαθός","translit":"agathós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G1872","hebrew":"ἐπακολουθέω","translit":"epakolouthéō","english":"diligently followed"}]},{"v":11,"text":"But refuse younger widows, for when they have grown wanton against Christ, they desire to marry;","understanding":"νεωτέρας δὲ χήρας παραιτοῦ, “But refuse younger widows” — do not put them on the list. The reason: ὅταν γὰρ καταστρηνιάσωσιν τοῦ Χριστοῦ, “when they have grown wanton against Christ” — when natural desires draw them away from a pledge of devotion to Christ — γαμεῖν θέλουσιν, “they desire to marry.” Paul is not condemning remarriage (v. 14 commends it); he is warning against a vow made too young and then broken.","words":[{"strongs":"G3501","hebrew":"νέος","translit":"néos","english":"younger"},{"strongs":"G5503","hebrew":"χήρα","translit":"chḗra","english":"widows"},{"strongs":"G3868","hebrew":"παραιτέομαι","translit":"paraitéomai","english":"refuse"},{"strongs":"G2691","hebrew":"καταστρηνιάω","translit":"katastrēniáō","english":"grown wanton against"},{"strongs":"G1060","hebrew":"γαμέω","translit":"gaméō","english":"marry"}]},{"v":12,"text":"having condemnation, because they have rejected their first pledge.","understanding":"ἔχουσαι κρίμα, “having condemnation,” ὅτι τὴν πρώτην πίστιν ἠθέτησαν, “because they have rejected their first pledge.” πίστις here means a pledge, a promise of faithfulness — perhaps a commitment made when enrolled to serve Christ as a widow. ἀθετέω means to set aside, break. Better not to make the pledge than to break it.","words":[{"strongs":"G2917","hebrew":"κρίμα","translit":"kríma","english":"condemnation"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"pledge"},{"strongs":"G114","hebrew":"ἀθετέω","translit":"athetéō","english":"rejected"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Besides, they also learn to be idle, going about from house to house. Not only idle, but also gossips and busybodies, saying things which they ought not.","understanding":"The danger of idleness: ἀργαὶ μανθάνουσιν, “they also learn to be idle” — ἀργός, not working — περιερχόμεναι τὰς οἰκίας, “going about from house to house.” Then φλύαροι, “gossips” — chatterers — καὶ περίεργοι, “and busybodies” — from the work family: ‘working around’, meddling, the same idea as 2 Thessalonians 3:11. λαλοῦσαι τὰ μὴ δέοντα, “saying things which they ought not.” Supported idleness breeds gossip, whether in men or women.","words":[{"strongs":"G692","hebrew":"ἀργός","translit":"argós","english":"idle"},{"strongs":"G3129","hebrew":"μανθάνω","translit":"manthánō","english":"learn"},{"strongs":"G4022","hebrew":"περιέρχομαι","translit":"periérchomai","english":"going about"},{"strongs":"G5397","hebrew":"φλύαρος","translit":"phlýaros","english":"gossips"},{"strongs":"G4021","hebrew":"περίεργος","translit":"períergos","english":"busybodies"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"saying"},{"strongs":"G1163","hebrew":"δεῖ","translit":"deî","english":"ought"}]},{"v":14,"text":"I desire therefore that the younger widows marry, bear children, rule the household, and give no occasion to the adversary for insulting.","understanding":"βούλομαι οὖν νεωτέρας γαμεῖν, “I desire therefore that the younger widows marry,” τεκνογονεῖν, “bear children,” οἰκοδεσποτεῖν, “rule the household” — literally ‘be master of the house’: Paul gives women real authority in managing the home. The aim: μηδεμίαν ἀφορμὴν διδόναι τῷ ἀντικειμένῳ λοιδορίας χάριν, “give no occasion to the adversary for insulting” — no foothold for opponents (human or satanic) to slander the church.","words":[{"strongs":"G1014","hebrew":"βούλομαι","translit":"boúlomai","english":"desire"},{"strongs":"G3501","hebrew":"νέος","translit":"néos","english":"younger"},{"strongs":"G1060","hebrew":"γαμέω","translit":"gaméō","english":"marry"},{"strongs":"G5041","hebrew":"τεκνογονέω","translit":"teknogonéō","english":"bear children"},{"strongs":"G3616","hebrew":"οἰκοδεσποτέω","translit":"oikodespotéō","english":"rule the household"},{"strongs":"G874","hebrew":"ἀφορμή","translit":"aphormḗ","english":"occasion"},{"strongs":"G480","hebrew":"ἀντίκειμαι","translit":"antíkeimai","english":"adversary"},{"strongs":"G3059","hebrew":"λοιδορία","translit":"loidoría","english":"insulting"}]},{"v":15,"text":"For already some have turned away after Satan.","understanding":"“For already some have turned away (ἐξετράπησαν) after Satan.” The same verb as 1:6 (‘turned away to vain talking’). Paul is not speculating: some young widows in Ephesus had already followed the false teachers or abandoned the faith. Pastoral rules are written from real pain.","words":[{"strongs":"G1624","hebrew":"ἐκτρέπω","translit":"ektrépō","english":"turned away"},{"strongs":"G4567","hebrew":"Σατανᾶς","translit":"Satanâs","english":"Satan"}]},{"v":16,"text":"If any man or woman who believes has widows, let them relieve them, and don’t let the assembly be burdened; that it might relieve those who are widows indeed.","understanding":"If any πιστὸς ἢ πιστή, “man or woman who believes,” has widows in the family, let them ἐπαρκείτω, “relieve” them, help them, καὶ μὴ βαρείσθω ἡ ἐκκλησία, “and don’t let the assembly be burdened” — weighed down — so that it can help τὰς ὄντως χήρας, “those who are widows indeed.” Families first; the church supports those who have no one.","words":[{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"believes"},{"strongs":"G5503","hebrew":"χήρα","translit":"chḗra","english":"widows"},{"strongs":"G1884","hebrew":"ἐπαρκέω","translit":"eparkéō","english":"relieve"},{"strongs":"G916","hebrew":"βαρέω","translit":"baréō","english":"burdened"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assembly"},{"strongs":"G3689","hebrew":"ὄντως","translit":"óntōs","english":"indeed"}]},{"v":17,"text":"Let the elders who rule well be counted worthy of double honor, especially those who labor in the word and in teaching.","understanding":"οἱ καλῶς προεστῶτες πρεσβύτεροι, “Let the elders who rule well” — lead well — διπλῆς τιμῆς ἀξιούσθωσαν, “be counted worthy of double honor” — τιμή means honour and also payment (the context of v. 18 shows it includes material support). μάλιστα οἱ κοπιῶντες ἐν λόγῳ καὶ διδασκαλίᾳ, “especially those who labor in the word and in teaching” — κοπιάω from the work family: toil to weariness. Preaching and teaching well is hard work, and it deserves support.","words":[{"strongs":"G2573","hebrew":"καλῶς","translit":"kalōs","english":"well"},{"strongs":"G4291","hebrew":"προΐστημι","translit":"proḯstēmi","english":"rule"},{"strongs":"G4245","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","english":"elders"},{"strongs":"G1362","hebrew":"διπλοῦς","translit":"diploûs","english":"double"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"},{"strongs":"G515","hebrew":"ἀξιόω","translit":"axióō","english":"counted worthy"},{"strongs":"G2872","hebrew":"κοπιάω","translit":"kopiáō","english":"labor"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G1319","hebrew":"διδασκαλία","translit":"didaskalía","english":"teaching"}]},{"v":18,"text":"For the Scripture says, “You shall not muzzle the ox when it treads out the grain.” And, “The laborer is worthy of his wages.”","understanding":"λέγει γὰρ ἡ γραφή, “For the Scripture says.” Two quotations. The first from Deuteronomy 25:4: “You shall not muzzle the ox when it treads out the grain” — let the working animal eat from the grain it threshes (Paul uses the same text in 1 Corinthians 9:9). The second: “The laborer is worthy of his wages” — ὁ ἐργάτης, a work-family word: the labourer, worker. These are Jesus' words recorded in Luke 10:7, and Paul calls them γραφή, Scripture, alongside the Law of Moses — an early witness that the Gospel accounts were already received as Scripture.","words":[{"strongs":"G1124","hebrew":"γραφή","translit":"graphḗ","english":"Scripture"},{"strongs":"G5392","hebrew":"φιμόω","translit":"phimóō","english":"muzzle"},{"strongs":"G1016","hebrew":"βοῦς","translit":"boûs","english":"ox"},{"strongs":"G248","hebrew":"ἀλοάω","translit":"aloáō","english":"treads out"},{"strongs":"G2040","hebrew":"ἐργάτης","translit":"ergátēs","english":"laborer"},{"strongs":"G514","hebrew":"ἄξιος","translit":"áxios","english":"worthy"},{"strongs":"G3408","hebrew":"μισθός","translit":"misthós","english":"wages"}]},{"v":19,"text":"Don’t receive an accusation against an elder, except at the word of two or three witnesses.","understanding":"κατὰ πρεσβυτέρου κατηγορίαν μὴ παραδέχου, “Don’t receive an accusation against an elder” — κατηγορία, a formal charge (the root of ‘category’) — ἐκτὸς εἰ μὴ ἐπὶ δύο ἢ τριῶν μαρτύρων, “except at the word of two or three witnesses” — the Old Testament standard (Deuteronomy 19:15) that Jesus applied to the church (Matthew 18:16). Leaders are easy targets for gossip; they deserve the protection of due process.","words":[{"strongs":"G4245","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","english":"elder"},{"strongs":"G2724","hebrew":"κατηγορία","translit":"katēgoría","english":"accusation"},{"strongs":"G3858","hebrew":"παραδέχομαι","translit":"paradéchomai","english":"receive"},{"strongs":"G3144","hebrew":"μάρτυς","translit":"mártys","english":"witnesses"}]},{"v":20,"text":"Those who sin, reprove in the sight of all, that the rest also may be in fear.","understanding":"But protection is not immunity: τοὺς ἁμαρτάνοντας, “Those who sin” — present participle: those who persist in sin — ἐνώπιον πάντων ἔλεγχε, “reprove in the sight of all” — ἐλέγχω, to expose and convict — ἵνα καὶ οἱ λοιποὶ φόβον ἔχωσιν, “that the rest also may be in fear.” A leader's persistent sin, once established, is dealt with openly, because leadership is public and the warning must be too.","words":[{"strongs":"G264","hebrew":"ἁμαρτάνω","translit":"hamartánō","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G1651","hebrew":"ἐλέγχω","translit":"elénchō","english":"reprove"},{"strongs":"G1799","hebrew":"ἐνώπιον","translit":"enṓpion","english":"in the sight"},{"strongs":"G5401","hebrew":"φόβος","translit":"phóbos","english":"fear"}]},{"v":21,"text":"I command you in the sight of God, and the Lord Jesus Christ, and the chosen angels, that you observe these things without prejudice, doing nothing by partiality.","understanding":"A solemn charge: διαμαρτύρομαι, “I command you,” as one calling witnesses, “in the sight of God, and the Lord Jesus Christ, and the chosen (ἐκλεκτῶν) angels.” The heavenly court is watching. “that you observe (φυλάξῃς) these things χωρὶς προκρίματος, without prejudice” — prejudging before the evidence — μηδὲν ποιῶν κατὰ πρόσκλισιν, “doing nothing by partiality” — leaning toward one side, favouritism. Church discipline must be fair, whoever is involved.","words":[{"strongs":"G1263","hebrew":"διαμαρτύρομαι","translit":"diamartýromai","english":"command"},{"strongs":"G1588","hebrew":"ἐκλεκτός","translit":"eklektós","english":"chosen"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G5442","hebrew":"φυλάσσω","translit":"phylássō","english":"observe"},{"strongs":"G4299","hebrew":"πρόκριμα","translit":"prókrima","english":"prejudice"},{"strongs":"G4346","hebrew":"πρόσκλισις","translit":"prósklisis","english":"partiality"}]},{"v":22,"text":"Lay hands hastily on no one, neither be a participant in other men’s sins. Keep yourself pure.","understanding":"χεῖρας ταχέως μηδενὶ ἐπιτίθει, “Lay hands hastily on no one” — probably appointing elders by laying on hands (4:14), though some see restoring repentant sinners. Hasty appointments lead to later scandals. μηδὲ κοινώνει ἁμαρτίαις ἀλλοτρίαις, “neither be a participant (κοινωνέω, share in) other men’s sins” — appointing an unqualified person makes you share responsibility. σεαυτὸν ἁγνὸν τήρει, “Keep yourself pure” — ἁγνός, clean, chaste.","words":[{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hands"},{"strongs":"G5030","hebrew":"ταχέως","translit":"tachéōs","english":"hastily"},{"strongs":"G2007","hebrew":"ἐπιτίθημι","translit":"epitíthēmi","english":"lay"},{"strongs":"G2841","hebrew":"κοινωνέω","translit":"koinōnéō","english":"be a participant"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G53","hebrew":"ἁγνός","translit":"hagnós","english":"pure"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"keep"}]},{"v":23,"text":"Be no longer a drinker of water only, but use a little wine for your stomach’s sake and your frequent infirmities.","understanding":"A personal, practical note in the middle of serious instructions: μηκέτι ὑδροπότει, “Be no longer a drinker of water only” — ὑδροποτέω, to drink only water — ἀλλὰ οἴνῳ ὀλίγῳ χρῶ, “but use a little wine,” διὰ τὸν στόμαχον καὶ τὰς πυκνάς σου ἀσθενείας, “for your stomach’s sake and your frequent infirmities.” Timothy may have abstained to distance himself from drunkenness or from false teachers' laxity, but water was often unsafe. Paul cares for his body as well as his ministry. Keeping yourself pure (v. 22) does not require harming your health; it is ‘a little’ wine, as medicine.","words":[{"strongs":"G5202","hebrew":"ὑδροποτέω","translit":"hydropotéō","english":"drinker of water"},{"strongs":"G3631","hebrew":"οἶνος","translit":"oînos","english":"wine"},{"strongs":"G4751","hebrew":"στόμαχος","translit":"stómachos","english":"stomach"},{"strongs":"G4437","hebrew":"πυκνός","translit":"pyknós","english":"frequent"},{"strongs":"G769","hebrew":"ἀσθένεια","translit":"asthéneia","english":"infirmities"}]},{"v":24,"text":"Some men’s sins are evident, preceding them to judgment, and some also follow later.","understanding":"Back to judging candidates and accusations wisely: “Some men’s sins (ἁμαρτίαι) are evident (πρόδηλοι — obvious beforehand), preceding (προάγουσαι) them to judgment (κρίσιν), and some also follow later (ἐπακολουθοῦσιν).” Some people's sins are obvious at once; others' come to light only with time. That is why hasty appointment is dangerous (v. 22).","words":[{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G4271","hebrew":"πρόδηλος","translit":"pródēlos","english":"evident"},{"strongs":"G4254","hebrew":"προάγω","translit":"proágō","english":"preceding"},{"strongs":"G2920","hebrew":"κρίσις","translit":"krísis","english":"judgment"},{"strongs":"G1872","hebrew":"ἐπακολουθέω","translit":"epakolouthéō","english":"follow"}]},{"v":25,"text":"In the same way also there are good works that are obvious, and those that are otherwise can’t be hidden.","understanding":"The same is true of good: “In the same way also there are good works (τὰ ἔργα τὰ καλά) that are obvious (πρόδηλα), and those that are otherwise can’t be hidden (κρυβῆναι).” καλός again: noble, beautiful deeds. Some faithful people's goodness is visible at once; others' quiet goodness becomes clear over time. Nothing done for God is ultimately hidden. Patience reveals both sin and goodness.","words":[{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G4271","hebrew":"πρόδηλος","translit":"pródēlos","english":"obvious"},{"strongs":"G2928","hebrew":"κρύπτω","translit":"krýptō","english":"hidden"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Widow Who Prays Night and Day","verseRef":"v. 3-5","image":"Picture an elderly woman in a small room in Ephesus. Her husband died years ago; her children are gone. She has no income and no protector. But every morning before dawn and every evening after dark, she kneels by her bed and prays — for the pastor by name, for the young mothers, for the sick, for the missionaries she has heard about, for the city that mocks her faith. The church brings her bread and oil each week and honours her as a mother. Few people know that much of the church's strength flows from her prayers.","meaning":"Paul writes, “Now she who is a widow indeed, and desolate, has her hope set on God, and continues in petitions and prayers night and day” (v. 5). The church is called to “Honor widows who are widows indeed” (v. 3) — caring for them practically and valuing them deeply. James calls this “Pure religion and undefiled before our God and Father … to visit the fatherless and widows in their affliction” (James 1:27). Who are the forgotten, faithful intercessors in your church? Honour them."},{"title":"Two Kinds of Good","verseRef":"v. 10","image":"Picture a woman's life laid out like a scroll before the church elders. They read it aloud: she raised her children in the faith. She opened her home to travelling believers. She knelt to wash the dusty feet of the saints, as Jesus did. She brought food and comfort to the persecuted and the grieving. She followed every opportunity to do good. As the list is read, people in the room nod and remember her kindness to them. No one needs to argue about her character; her works speak.","meaning":"Paul describes a widow “approved by good works, if she has brought up children, if she has been hospitable to strangers, if she has washed the saints’ feet, if she has relieved the afflicted, and if she has diligently followed every good work” (v. 10). Both words from the good family appear: καλός, works that are beautiful and admirable, and ἀγαθός, works that do people good. A life of quiet, practical service becomes a testimony that the whole church can read."},{"title":"The Ox at the Threshing Floor","verseRef":"v. 17-18","image":"Picture an ox walking in circles on a stone threshing floor, its hooves crushing the grain from the stalks. A cruel farmer ties a muzzle on it so that it cannot eat any of the grain it is working on. A wise farmer leaves its mouth free, so that it can bend down and eat as it works. Now picture a pastor who spends long hours studying the Scriptures, preparing sermons, visiting the sick and counselling the broken. Will the church muzzle him, or let him eat from the field he works?","meaning":"Paul writes, “Let the elders who rule well be counted worthy of double honor, especially those who labor in the word and in teaching. For the Scripture says, ‘You shall not muzzle the ox when it treads out the grain.’ And, ‘The laborer is worthy of his wages’” (vv. 17–18). Faithful leaders deserve both respect and material support. Paul quotes Jesus' words from Luke 10:7 as Scripture. Honouring those who feed you with God's word is part of the church's faithfulness."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Relating as Family (v. 1-2)","body":"Timothy is to appeal to older men as fathers, younger men as brothers, older women as mothers and younger women as sisters, in all purity."},{"label":"Caring for Widows (v. 3-16)","body":"Honour true widows; families must first care for their own widowed relatives, and anyone who does not provide for his household has denied the faith; true widows hope in God and pray continually; a list may enrol widows over sixty known for good works; younger widows should marry rather than make pledges they may break and fall into idleness and gossip; believing families should support their widows so that the church can help those truly alone."},{"label":"Honouring and Disciplining Elders (v. 17-21)","body":"Elders who lead well, especially those who labour in preaching and teaching, deserve double honour, including support; accusations against elders require two or three witnesses; those who persist in sin are to be rebuked publicly; all must be done without prejudice or favouritism, before God, Christ and the elect angels."},{"label":"Wisdom and Personal Care (v. 22-25)","body":"Do not appoint leaders hastily or share in others' sins; keep yourself pure; use a little wine for your stomach and frequent illnesses; some sins and good works are evident immediately, others come to light later."}],"sections":[{"heading":"The Church as a Family","paragraphs":["Chapter 5 begins with how a young leader should relate to different people: “Don’t rebuke an older man, but exhort him as a father; the younger men as brothers; the elder women as mothers; the younger as sisters, in all purity” (vv. 1–2). The church is God's household (3:15), and its relationships are family relationships. Correction is not abandoned, but it is given with the respect, warmth and purity appropriate within a family.","The longest section concerns widows (vv. 3–16). In the ancient world, a widow without family support could fall into extreme poverty. From its earliest days the church cared for them; Acts records that “a complaint arose from the Hellenists against the Hebrews, because their widows were neglected in the daily service” (Acts 6:1). Paul's instructions balance compassion and wisdom: “Honor widows who are widows indeed” (v. 3) — those who are truly alone.","Family comes first: “But if any widow has children or grandchildren, let them learn first to show piety towards their own family, and to repay their parents, for this is acceptable in the sight of God” (v. 4). The word ‘show piety’ is εὐσεβέω, the verb of godliness: godliness begins at home. Paul's warning is sharp: “But if anyone doesn’t provide for his own, and especially his own household, he has denied the faith, and is worse than an unbeliever” (v. 8). Jesus condemned religious leaders who used religious excuses to avoid caring for their parents (Matthew 15:5). Real faith shows itself in care for family."]},{"heading":"Widows Indeed","paragraphs":["The true widow is described with beauty: she “has her hope set on God, and continues in petitions and prayers night and day” (v. 5). Like Anna, who “didn’t depart from the temple, worshiping with fastings and petitions night and day” (Luke 2:37), such women were the praying heart of the church. By contrast, “she who gives herself to pleasure is dead while she lives” (v. 6). Self-indulgence empties a life even while it continues.","Verses 9–10 describe an enrolled list of widows over sixty, known for faithfulness in marriage and good works: “if she has brought up children, if she has been hospitable to strangers, if she has washed the saints’ feet, if she has relieved the afflicted, and if she has diligently followed every good work” (v. 10). This list may have involved both support and a commitment to serve the church in prayer and ministry. Dorcas in Joppa shows what such a life looked like: “All the widows stood by him weeping, and showing the coats and garments which Dorcas had made while she was with them” (Acts 9:39).","Younger widows were not to be enrolled, because they might make a pledge of devotion they would later want to break when they desired to remarry, and because supported idleness could lead to gossip and meddling: “Besides, they also learn to be idle, going about from house to house. Not only idle, but also gossips and busybodies, saying things which they ought not” (v. 13). Paul's counsel is that they remarry, raise families and manage their homes (v. 14). He is not criticising young women but protecting them and the church's witness: “For already some have turned away after Satan” (v. 15)."]},{"heading":"Honour, Discipline and Fairness","paragraphs":["Paul then addresses elders. Those who lead well deserve “double honor” (v. 17) — respect and material support — “especially those who labor in the word and in teaching.” His proof is Scripture: “You shall not muzzle the ox when it treads out the grain” (Deuteronomy 25:4, quoted in v. 18), and “The laborer is worthy of his wages” (v. 18), the words of Jesus in Luke 10:7. Paul calls both ‘Scripture’ — a remarkable sign that Jesus' words, as recorded in the Gospel, were already received with the authority of the Old Testament. Elsewhere he writes, “Even so the Lord ordained that those who proclaim the Good News should live from the Good News” (1 Corinthians 9:14).","Elders also need protection and accountability. “Don’t receive an accusation against an elder, except at the word of two or three witnesses” (v. 19) — the principle Jesus taught: “that at the mouth of two or three witnesses every word may be established” (Matthew 18:16). But proven, persistent sin is not hidden: “Those who sin, reprove in the sight of all, that the rest also may be in fear” (v. 20). And all of this must be done “without prejudice, doing nothing by partiality” (v. 21) — no favouritism for friends or prominent people, under the eyes of God, Christ and the angels.","The chapter ends with practical wisdom. “Lay hands hastily on no one” (v. 22): do not rush to appoint leaders, because character takes time to reveal — “Some men’s sins are evident, preceding them to judgment, and some also follow later” (v. 24), and likewise good works “can’t be hidden” (v. 25). In the middle comes a tender personal note: “Be no longer a drinker of water only, but use a little wine for your stomach’s sake and your frequent infirmities” (v. 23). Paul cared for Timothy's health. Holiness does not require neglecting the body."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Widows, Workers and Witnesses in the New Testament","paragraphs":["Care for widows is a mark of true faith throughout the New Testament. James defines it: “Pure religion and undefiled before our God and Father is this: to visit the fatherless and widows in their affliction, and to keep oneself unstained by the world” (James 1:27). Jesus condemned those “who devour widows’ houses, and for a pretense make long prayers” (Mark 12:40), and honoured the poor widow who gave “two small brass coins” (Luke 21:2).","Paul's teaching on supporting gospel workers rests on Jesus' own words. Sending out the seventy, Jesus said, “Remain in that same house, eating and drinking the things they give, for the laborer is worthy of his wages” (Luke 10:7). Paul argued the same with the Corinthians, quoting the ox text: “For it is written in the law of Moses, ‘You shall not muzzle an ox while it treads out the grain’” (1 Corinthians 9:9). And he urged the Galatians: “But let him who is taught in the word share all good things with him who teaches” (Galatians 6:6).","The principle of two or three witnesses protects the innocent: “But if he doesn’t listen, take one or two more with you, that at the mouth of two or three witnesses every word may be established” (Matthew 18:16). And the call to care for family and community reflects Paul's wider teaching: “So then, as we have opportunity, let’s do what is good toward all men, and especially toward those who are of the household of the faith” (Galatians 6:10)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Widow","hebrew":"χήρα","translit":"chḗra","strongs":"G5503","verses":"v. 3, 4, 5, 9, 11, 16","insight":"Strong's: “a widow (as lacking a husband), literally or figuratively.” In both Testaments widows, orphans and foreigners are the classic picture of the vulnerable whom God defends. The early church took this seriously: the first church administrative structure (Acts 6) arose from caring for widows. 1 Timothy 5 shows that care should be generous but wise — family first, church support for those truly alone, and dignity for those who serve in prayer and good works."},{"english":"Show piety / be godly toward","hebrew":"εὐσεβέω","translit":"eusebéō","strongs":"G2151","verses":"v. 4","insight":"Strong's: “to be pious, i.e. (towards God) to worship, or (towards parents) to respect (support).” It is the verb of εὐσέβεια, the key word of 1 Timothy. Paul applies godliness first to caring for one's own parents and grandparents. Real godliness is not only devotion in worship but faithfulness at home. Someone who sings loudly in church but neglects an ageing parent has misunderstood what godliness is."},{"english":"Provide for / think ahead for","hebrew":"προνοέω","translit":"pronoéō","strongs":"G4306","verses":"v. 8","insight":"Strong's: “to consider in advance, i.e. look out for beforehand (actively, by way of maintenance for others; middle voice by way of circumspection for oneself).” It combines πρό, before, and νοέω, to think: to plan ahead for someone's needs. Paul uses it in Romans 12:17 of planning to do what is honourable. Caring for family is not only reacting to crises but thinking ahead for them. Paul says that neglecting this is a denial of the faith."},{"english":"Double / two-fold","hebrew":"διπλοῦς","translit":"diploûs","strongs":"G1362","verses":"v. 17","insight":"Strong's: “two-fold.” ‘Double honour’ most likely means both respect and remuneration — honour in attitude and honour in material support — as the following verse about wages shows. It may also mean generous honour. Either way, elders who lead well and labour in the word deserve to be valued and supported. A church that receives faithful teaching but neglects its teachers has muzzled the ox."},{"english":"Partiality / favouritism","hebrew":"πρόσκλισις","translit":"prósklisis","strongs":"G4346","verses":"v. 21","insight":"Strong's: “a leaning towards, i.e. (figuratively) proclivity (favoritism).” It appears only here in the New Testament. It pictures leaning toward one side of a scale. In church discipline, partiality might mean protecting a popular leader or a friend, or being harsher with those one dislikes. Paul charges Timothy before God, Christ and the angels to act without prejudice or favouritism. Justice in God's house must reflect the justice of God, who shows no partiality."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: how you care for your own family, how you honour the vulnerable and those who serve, and how fairly you judge.","items":[{"title":"Godliness Begins at Home","body":"Paul says that godliness is shown first by caring for one's own parents and household (vv. 4, 8). The Spirit may be pointing you to someone in your family — an ageing parent, a struggling sibling, a grandparent — whom you have neglected while busy with other things, even church work. Ask Him how you can honour them practically this week. Godliness that skips the family is not godliness."},{"title":"Honour the Hidden Servants","body":"The widow who prays night and day, the woman who washes the saints' feet, the elder who labours in the word — these are often unseen. The Spirit wants you to notice them and honour them. Who has prayed for you, served you or taught you faithfully? Thank them, support them, and learn from them. And ask Him to make you one of these hidden servants."},{"title":"Judge Without Favouritism","body":"Paul charges Timothy to act without prejudice or partiality. The Spirit may be showing you where you judge people unequally — excusing friends, condemning those you dislike, believing accusations too quickly or dismissing them too easily. Ask Him for fairness, patience and wisdom, remembering that both sins and good works become clear in time (vv. 24–25)."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Family-Care Check","body":"Write down the names of the older or vulnerable people in your family. Next to each, write when you last contacted or helped them. Choose one practical act of care for this week (vv. 4, 8)."},{"title":"The Honour Check","body":"Who are the people who labour in the word and in teaching for you (v. 17)? How do you honour and support them? Write one specific way you will encourage or support them this month."},{"title":"The Fairness Check","body":"Is there a situation where you have judged someone hastily, or shown favouritism (vv. 19–22)? Write it down, ask God's forgiveness, and decide what fairness requires."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Adopt a Widow or Widower","body":"Identify an elderly widow or widower in your church or neighbourhood who has little family support. Commit to visiting or calling them regularly for the next three months. Ask about their life, help with practical needs, pray with them, and learn from their faith. Treat them as a mother or father (v. 2). Ask your church leaders whether there is a wider need for widow care that you could help organise."},{"title":"Double Honour Week","body":"This week, practise ‘double honour’ for a leader who teaches you God's word. First, honour them in words: write a specific note of thanks for how their teaching has helped you. Second, honour them practically: give generously to support them, provide a meal, or offer help with a burden they carry. Pray for them daily that week."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, thank You for making Your church a family. Teach me to treat older men as fathers, younger men as brothers, older women as mothers and younger women as sisters, in all purity. Give me a heart for widows and all who are alone. Help me show godliness first at home, caring for my own family and repaying those who cared for me. Keep me from self-indulgence that leaves me dead while I live. Make me like the widow who hopes in You and prays night and day, and like those whose lives are full of good works — hospitable, humble, helpful to the afflicted. Keep me from idleness, gossip and meddling. Help me honour those who labour in Your word, and support them generously. Give our church fairness in discipline, without prejudice or partiality. Keep me pure. Thank You for caring even for our bodies. And thank You that nothing done for You is ultimately hidden. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Anna in the Temple","ref":"Luke 2:36-38","relatedVerses":"v. 5","summary":"When Mary and Joseph brought the baby Jesus to the temple, an elderly prophetess named Anna was there. She had been married only seven years before her husband died, “and she had been a widow for about eighty-four years), who didn’t depart from the temple, worshiping with fastings and petitions night and day” (Luke 2:37). Coming up at that very moment, she gave thanks to God and spoke about the child to all who were waiting for the redemption of Jerusalem. Anna is the picture of 1 Timothy 5:5 — a widow indeed, desolate yet hoping in God, praying night and day — and God gave her the privilege of seeing the Messiah."},{"title":"Dorcas, Full of Good Works","ref":"Acts 9:36-42","relatedVerses":"v. 9-10","summary":"In Joppa lived a disciple named Tabitha, or Dorcas, who was full of good works and acts of charity. She became ill and died, and the believers sent for Peter. “Peter got up and went with them. When he had come, they brought him into the upper room. All the widows stood by him weeping, and showing the coats and garments which Dorcas had made while she was with them” (Acts 9:39). Peter sent everyone out, knelt and prayed, and told her to rise. She opened her eyes and sat up, and many believed in the Lord. Dorcas shows the kind of life described in 1 Timothy 5:10 — practical, generous good works that make a whole community grieve when they are gone."},{"title":"Ruth and Naomi","ref":"Ruth 1:1-22","relatedVerses":"v. 3-8","summary":"Naomi lost her husband and both her sons in the land of Moab, leaving her and her two widowed daughters-in-law with nothing. She urged them to go back to their own families. Orpah returned, but Ruth clung to Naomi and declared that Naomi's people would be her people and Naomi's God her God. Ruth went with her to Bethlehem and worked in the fields to provide for her. In time, God provided a kinsman-redeemer, Boaz, and through Ruth's line came King David and ultimately Jesus. Ruth's faithful care for her widowed mother-in-law is a living picture of 1 Timothy 5:4 and 8: showing godliness first to one's own family."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Timothy","chapter":6,"chapterTitle":"Godliness With Contentment — The Love of Money, the Good Fight and the Only Sovereign","themeTitle":"“But Godliness With Contentment Is Great Gain”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Let as many as are bondservants under the yoke count their own masters worthy of all honor, that the name of God and the doctrine not be blasphemed.","understanding":"ὅσοι εἰσὶν ὑπὸ ζυγὸν δοῦλοι, “as many as are bondservants under the yoke” — the servant family's δοῦλος, a slave who belongs to a master; ζυγός, a yoke, the burden of slavery. Many early believers were slaves. They are to count their δεσπότας, “masters” (the word gives ‘despot’), πάσης τιμῆς ἀξίους, “worthy of all honor,” so that “the name of God and the doctrine not be blasphemed.” Paul addresses believers living inside an existing system; he does not endorse it — he has just condemned ‘slave-traders’ (1:10), and he urges slaves to take freedom if they can (1 Corinthians 7:21). His concern here is the gospel's witness.","words":[{"strongs":"G2218","hebrew":"ζυγός","translit":"zygós","english":"yoke"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"bondservants"},{"strongs":"G1203","hebrew":"δεσπότης","translit":"despótēs","english":"masters"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"},{"strongs":"G514","hebrew":"ἄξιος","translit":"áxios","english":"worthy"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G1319","hebrew":"διδασκαλία","translit":"didaskalía","english":"doctrine"},{"strongs":"G987","hebrew":"βλασφημέω","translit":"blasphēméō","english":"blasphemed"}]},{"v":2,"text":"Those who have believing masters, let them not despise them, because they are brothers, but rather let them serve them, because those who partake of the benefit are believing and beloved. Teach and exhort these things.","understanding":"Slaves with πιστοὺς δεσπότας, “believing masters,” must not καταφρονείτωσαν, “despise them,” because they are ἀδελφοί, “brothers.” Equality in Christ could tempt a believing slave to show less respect. Instead, μᾶλλον δουλευέτωσαν, “rather let them serve them” better, because those who receive τῆς εὐεργεσίας, “the benefit” of their good service, are πιστοὶ καὶ ἀγαπητοί, “believing and beloved.” Brotherhood transforms the relationship from within. “Teach and exhort these things.”","words":[{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"believing"},{"strongs":"G1203","hebrew":"δεσπότης","translit":"despótēs","english":"masters"},{"strongs":"G2706","hebrew":"καταφρονέω","translit":"kataphronéō","english":"despise"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brothers"},{"strongs":"G1398","hebrew":"δουλεύω","translit":"douleúō","english":"serve"},{"strongs":"G2108","hebrew":"εὐεργεσία","translit":"euergesía","english":"benefit"},{"strongs":"G482","hebrew":"ἀντιλαμβάνομαι","translit":"antilambánomai","english":"partake"},{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"beloved"},{"strongs":"G1321","hebrew":"διδάσκω","translit":"didáskō","english":"Teach"},{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"exhort"}]},{"v":3,"text":"If anyone teaches a different doctrine, and doesn’t consent to sound words, the words of our Lord Jesus Christ, and to the doctrine which is according to godliness,","understanding":"The false teachers again. εἴ τις ἑτεροδιδασκαλεῖ, “If anyone teaches a different doctrine” — ἕτερος from the ‘another’ family: different in kind (1:3) — καὶ μὴ προσέρχεται ὑγιαίνουσιν λόγοις, “and doesn’t consent to sound words” — healthy words — “the words of our Lord Jesus Christ, and to the doctrine which is according to godliness (εὐσέβειαν).” True teaching agrees with Jesus' words and produces godliness.","words":[{"strongs":"G2085","hebrew":"ἑτεροδιδασκαλέω","translit":"heterodidaskaléō","english":"teaches a different doctrine"},{"strongs":"G4334","hebrew":"προσέρχομαι","translit":"prosérchomai","english":"consent"},{"strongs":"G5198","hebrew":"ὑγιαίνω","translit":"hygiaínō","english":"sound"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"words"},{"strongs":"G1319","hebrew":"διδασκαλία","translit":"didaskalía","english":"doctrine"},{"strongs":"G2150","hebrew":"εὐσέβεια","translit":"eusébeia","english":"godliness"}]},{"v":4,"text":"he is conceited, knowing nothing, but obsessed with arguments, disputes, and word battles, from which come envy, strife, insulting, evil suspicions,","understanding":"Paul's diagnosis: τετύφωται, “he is conceited” — wrapped in smoke, puffed up (3:6) — μηδὲν ἐπιστάμενος, “knowing nothing,” ἀλλὰ νοσῶν, “but obsessed” — literally ‘sick’, the opposite of the ‘sound’ (healthy) words of verse 3 — περὶ ζητήσεις καὶ λογομαχίας, “with arguments, disputes, and word battles” — λογομαχία is ‘word-fighting’, quarrels over terms. The fruit: φθόνος, “envy”; ἔρις, “strife”; βλασφημίαι, “insulting”; ὑπόνοιαι πονηραί, “evil suspicions” — πονηρός from the evil family: actively harmful.","words":[{"strongs":"G5187","hebrew":"τυφόω","translit":"typhóō","english":"conceited"},{"strongs":"G1987","hebrew":"ἐπίσταμαι","translit":"epístamai","english":"knowing"},{"strongs":"G3552","hebrew":"νοσέω","translit":"noséō","english":"obsessed"},{"strongs":"G2214","hebrew":"ζήτησις","translit":"zḗtēsis","english":"arguments"},{"strongs":"G3055","hebrew":"λογομαχία","translit":"logomachía","english":"word battles"},{"strongs":"G5355","hebrew":"φθόνος","translit":"phthónos","english":"envy"},{"strongs":"G2054","hebrew":"ἔρις","translit":"éris","english":"strife"},{"strongs":"G988","hebrew":"βλασφημία","translit":"blasphēmía","english":"insulting"},{"strongs":"G4190","hebrew":"πονηρός","translit":"ponērós","english":"evil"},{"strongs":"G5283","hebrew":"ὑπόνοια","translit":"hypónoia","english":"suspicions"}]},{"v":5,"text":"constant friction of people of corrupt minds and destitute of the truth, who suppose that godliness is a means of gain. Withdraw yourself from such.","understanding":"παραδιατριβαί, “constant friction” — endless wrangling — of people διεφθαρμένων τὸν νοῦν, “of corrupt minds” — rotted through — καὶ ἀπεστερημένων τῆς ἀληθείας, “and destitute of the truth” — robbed of it. Their motive is exposed: νομιζόντων πορισμὸν εἶναι τὴν εὐσέβειαν, “who suppose that godliness is a means of gain” — a way to make money. Religion as a business. “Withdraw yourself from such.”","words":[{"strongs":"G3859","hebrew":"παραδιατριβή","translit":"paradiatribḗ","english":"constant friction"},{"strongs":"G1311","hebrew":"διαφθείρω","translit":"diaphtheírō","english":"corrupt"},{"strongs":"G3563","hebrew":"νοῦς","translit":"noûs","english":"minds"},{"strongs":"G650","hebrew":"ἀποστερέω","translit":"aposteréō","english":"destitute"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"},{"strongs":"G3543","hebrew":"νομίζω","translit":"nomízō","english":"suppose"},{"strongs":"G4200","hebrew":"πορισμός","translit":"porismós","english":"means of gain"},{"strongs":"G2150","hebrew":"εὐσέβεια","translit":"eusébeia","english":"godliness"},{"strongs":"G868","hebrew":"ἀφίστημι","translit":"aphístēmi","english":"withdraw"}]},{"v":6,"text":"But godliness with contentment is great gain.","understanding":"Paul turns their slogan around: “But godliness (εὐσέβεια) with contentment (αὐταρκείας) is great (μέγας) gain (πορισμός).” Godliness is indeed great gain — not as a way to get money, but because, combined with contentment, it makes a person truly rich. αὐτάρκεια means self-sufficiency, being satisfied with what one has; the Stoics prized it as independence, but Paul means sufficiency in God (Philippians 4:11).","words":[{"strongs":"G2150","hebrew":"εὐσέβεια","translit":"eusébeia","english":"godliness"},{"strongs":"G841","hebrew":"αὐτάρκεια","translit":"autárkeia","english":"contentment"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G4200","hebrew":"πορισμός","translit":"porismós","english":"gain"}]},{"v":7,"text":"For we brought nothing into the world, and we certainly can’t carry anything out.","understanding":"The reason: “For we brought (εἰσηνέγκαμεν) nothing into the world (κόσμον), and we certainly can’t carry (ἐξενεγκεῖν) anything out.” The proverbial truth of Job 1:21 and Ecclesiastes 5:15. Every possession is temporary, held between two empty hands. Shrouds have no pockets.","words":[{"strongs":"G1533","hebrew":"εἰσφέρω","translit":"eisphérō","english":"brought"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G1627","hebrew":"ἐκφέρω","translit":"ekphérō","english":"carry out"}]},{"v":8,"text":"But having food and clothing, we will be content with that.","understanding":"ἔχοντες δὲ διατροφὰς καὶ σκεπάσματα, “But having food and clothing” — σκέπασμα is covering, which can include shelter — τούτοις ἀρκεσθησόμεθα, “we will be content with that.” ἀρκέω, to be enough. Contentment is defined by needs met, not desires satisfied. This is not a ban on owning more, but a heart that does not require more to be at peace.","words":[{"strongs":"G1305","hebrew":"διατροφή","translit":"diatrophḗ","english":"food"},{"strongs":"G4629","hebrew":"σκέπασμα","translit":"sképasma","english":"clothing"},{"strongs":"G714","hebrew":"ἀρκέω","translit":"arkéō","english":"content"}]},{"v":9,"text":"But those who are determined to be rich fall into a temptation and a snare and many foolish and harmful lusts, such as drown men in ruin and destruction.","understanding":"οἱ δὲ βουλόμενοι πλουτεῖν, “But those who are determined to be rich” — the problem is the will, the settled desire, not wealth itself — ἐμπίπτουσιν εἰς πειρασμόν, “fall into a temptation,” καὶ παγίδα, “and a snare” — a trap — καὶ ἐπιθυμίας πολλὰς ἀνοήτους καὶ βλαβεράς, “and many foolish and harmful lusts,” αἵτινες βυθίζουσιν, “such as drown men” — sink them like a ship going under — εἰς ὄλεθρον καὶ ἀπώλειαν, “in ruin and destruction.”","words":[{"strongs":"G1014","hebrew":"βούλομαι","translit":"boúlomai","english":"are determined"},{"strongs":"G4147","hebrew":"πλουτέω","translit":"ploutéō","english":"be rich"},{"strongs":"G3986","hebrew":"πειρασμός","translit":"peirasmós","english":"temptation"},{"strongs":"G3803","hebrew":"παγίς","translit":"pagís","english":"snare"},{"strongs":"G453","hebrew":"ἀνόητος","translit":"anóētos","english":"foolish"},{"strongs":"G983","hebrew":"βλαβερός","translit":"blaberós","english":"harmful"},{"strongs":"G1939","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμία","translit":"epithymía","english":"lusts"},{"strongs":"G1036","hebrew":"βυθίζω","translit":"bythízō","english":"drown"},{"strongs":"G3639","hebrew":"ὄλεθρος","translit":"ólethros","english":"ruin"},{"strongs":"G684","hebrew":"ἀπώλεια","translit":"apṓleia","english":"destruction"}]},{"v":10,"text":"For the love of money is a root of all kinds of evil. Some have been led astray from the faith in their greed, and have pierced themselves through with many sorrows.","understanding":"ῥίζα γὰρ πάντων τῶν κακῶν ἐστιν ἡ φιλαργυρία, “For the love of money is a root of all kinds of evil.” Note carefully: not money but φιλαργυρία, ‘love of silver’; and ‘a root’ of ‘all kinds of’ evils — κακός from the evil family, bad and harmful things — not the only root of every evil. Some, ὀρεγόμενοι, “in their greed” — reaching out for it, stretching toward it — ἀπεπλανήθησαν ἀπὸ τῆς πίστεως, “have been led astray from the faith,” καὶ ἑαυτοὺς περιέπειραν ὀδύναις πολλαῖς, “and have pierced themselves through with many sorrows” — impaled themselves on their own spears.","words":[{"strongs":"G4491","hebrew":"ῥίζα","translit":"rhíza","english":"root"},{"strongs":"G2556","hebrew":"κακός","translit":"kakós","english":"evil"},{"strongs":"G5365","hebrew":"φιλαργυρία","translit":"philargyría","english":"love of money"},{"strongs":"G3713","hebrew":"ὀρέγομαι","translit":"orégomai","english":"greed"},{"strongs":"G635","hebrew":"ἀποπλανάω","translit":"apoplanáō","english":"led astray"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G4044","hebrew":"περιπείρω","translit":"peripeírō","english":"pierced through"},{"strongs":"G3601","hebrew":"ὀδύνη","translit":"odýnē","english":"sorrows"}]},{"v":11,"text":"But you, man of God, flee these things, and follow after righteousness, godliness, faith, love, perseverance, and gentleness.","understanding":"σὺ δέ, ὦ ἄνθρωπε θεοῦ, “But you, man of God” — the Old Testament title of Moses, Elijah and the prophets. Two commands: φεῦγε, “flee” — run away from — these things (love of money, quarrels, pride), and δίωκε, “follow after,” pursue, chase: δικαιοσύνην, “righteousness”; εὐσέβειαν, “godliness”; πίστιν, “faith”; ἀγάπην, “love” (the love family's ἀγάπη); ὑπομονήν, “perseverance”; πρᾳότητα, “gentleness.” The Christian life is not only running from sin but running toward Christlikeness.","words":[{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"man"},{"strongs":"G5343","hebrew":"φεύγω","translit":"pheúgō","english":"flee"},{"strongs":"G1377","hebrew":"διώκω","translit":"diṓkō","english":"follow after"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G2150","hebrew":"εὐσέβεια","translit":"eusébeia","english":"godliness"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G5281","hebrew":"ὑπομονή","translit":"hypomonḗ","english":"perseverance"},{"strongs":"G4236","hebrew":"πρᾳότης","translit":"praiótēs","english":"gentleness"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Fight the good fight of faith. Take hold of the eternal life to which you were called, and you confessed the good confession in the sight of many witnesses.","understanding":"ἀγωνίζου τὸν καλὸν ἀγῶνα τῆς πίστεως, “Fight the good fight of faith” — ἀγωνίζομαι and ἀγών, the language of the athletic contest (the root of ‘agony’); καλός from the good family: the noble contest. ἐπιλαβοῦ τῆς αἰωνίου ζωῆς, “Take hold of the eternal life” — grip it, seize it — εἰς ἣν ἐκλήθης, “to which you were called,” καὶ ὡμολόγησας τὴν καλὴν ὁμολογίαν, “and you confessed the good confession,” probably at his baptism or ordination, ἐνώπιον πολλῶν μαρτύρων, “in the sight of many witnesses.”","words":[{"strongs":"G75","hebrew":"ἀγωνίζομαι","translit":"agōnízomai","english":"Fight"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G73","hebrew":"ἀγών","translit":"agṓn","english":"fight"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G1949","hebrew":"ἐπιλαμβάνομαι","translit":"epilambánomai","english":"Take hold"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G3670","hebrew":"ὁμολογέω","translit":"homologéō","english":"confessed"},{"strongs":"G3671","hebrew":"ὁμολογία","translit":"homología","english":"confession"},{"strongs":"G3144","hebrew":"μάρτυς","translit":"mártys","english":"witnesses"}]},{"v":13,"text":"I command you before God, who gives life to all things, and before Christ Jesus, who before Pontius Pilate testified the good confession,","understanding":"παραγγέλλω σοι, “I command you” — the strongest charge in the letter — before God τοῦ ζῳοποιοῦντος τὰ πάντα, “who gives life to all things” (the WEB follows manuscripts with ζῳοποιέω), and before Christ Jesus, τοῦ μαρτυρήσαντος ἐπὶ Ποντίου Πιλάτου τὴν καλὴν ὁμολογίαν, “who before Pontius Pilate testified the good confession” — when He stood on trial and declared Himself a King whose kingdom is not of this world (John 18:37). Timothy's confession follows his Lord's.","words":[{"strongs":"G3853","hebrew":"παραγγέλλω","translit":"parangéllō","english":"command"},{"strongs":"G2227","hebrew":"ζωοποιέω","translit":"zōopoiéō","english":"gives life"},{"strongs":"G4194","hebrew":"Πόντιος","translit":"Póntios","english":"Pontius"},{"strongs":"G4091","hebrew":"Πιλᾶτος","translit":"Pilâtos","english":"Pilate"},{"strongs":"G3140","hebrew":"μαρτυρέω","translit":"martyréō","english":"testified"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G3671","hebrew":"ὁμολογία","translit":"homología","english":"confession"}]},{"v":14,"text":"that you keep the commandment without spot, blameless, until the appearing of our Lord Jesus Christ;","understanding":"The charge: τηρῆσαί σε τὴν ἐντολὴν ἄσπιλον ἀνεπίλημπτον, “that you keep the commandment without spot, blameless” — ἄσπιλος, unstained; ἀνεπίλημπτος, giving no grip to an accuser (3:2) — μέχρι τῆς ἐπιφανείας τοῦ κυρίου ἡμῶν Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ, “until the appearing (ἐπιφάνεια, the shining forth) of our Lord Jesus Christ.” The horizon of faithful ministry is Christ's return.","words":[{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"keep"},{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandment"},{"strongs":"G784","hebrew":"ἄσπιλος","translit":"áspilos","english":"without spot"},{"strongs":"G423","hebrew":"ἀνεπίληπτος","translit":"anepílēptos","english":"blameless"},{"strongs":"G2015","hebrew":"ἐπιφάνεια","translit":"epipháneia","english":"appearing"}]},{"v":15,"text":"which in its own times he will show, who is the blessed and only Ruler, the King of kings, and Lord of lords;","understanding":"“which in its own times (καιροῖς ἰδίοις)” — the time family's καιρός: at God's appointed moment, not ours — “he will show (δείξει)” — God will display Christ's appearing. Then a doxology, perhaps from early worship: ὁ μακάριος καὶ μόνος δυνάστης, “the blessed and only Ruler” — δυνάστης, from δύναμις in the power family: the one Sovereign who holds all power. In a world of emperors claiming divinity, God alone is βασιλεὺς τῶν βασιλευόντων, “King of kings,” καὶ κύριος τῶν κυριευόντων, “and Lord of lords.”","words":[{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"times"},{"strongs":"G1166","hebrew":"δεικνύω","translit":"deiknýō","english":"show"},{"strongs":"G3107","hebrew":"μακάριος","translit":"makários","english":"blessed"},{"strongs":"G3441","hebrew":"μόνος","translit":"mónos","english":"only"},{"strongs":"G1413","hebrew":"δυνάστης","translit":"dynástēs","english":"Ruler"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"King"},{"strongs":"G936","hebrew":"βασιλεύω","translit":"basileúō","english":"kings"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G2961","hebrew":"κυριεύω","translit":"kyrieúō","english":"lords"}]},{"v":16,"text":"who alone has immortality, dwelling in unapproachable light; whom no man has seen, nor can see: to whom be honor and eternal power. Amen.","understanding":"ὁ μόνος ἔχων ἀθανασίαν, “who alone has immortality” — deathlessness belongs to God by nature; creatures receive it only as a gift. φῶς οἰκῶν ἀπρόσιτον, “dwelling in unapproachable light” — light no one can come near (compare Moses, who could see only God's back). “whom no man has seen, nor can see” — God in His essential being is invisible (John 1:18). To Him be τιμὴ καὶ κράτος αἰώνιον, “honor and eternal power” — κράτος from the power family: dominion-strength that prevails. Amen.","words":[{"strongs":"G3441","hebrew":"μόνος","translit":"mónos","english":"alone"},{"strongs":"G110","hebrew":"ἀθανασία","translit":"athanasía","english":"immortality"},{"strongs":"G3611","hebrew":"οἰκέω","translit":"oikéō","english":"dwelling"},{"strongs":"G5457","hebrew":"φῶς","translit":"phōs","english":"light"},{"strongs":"G676","hebrew":"ἀπρόσιτος","translit":"aprósitos","english":"unapproachable"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"seen"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"},{"strongs":"G2904","hebrew":"κράτος","translit":"krátos","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"}]},{"v":17,"text":"Charge those who are rich in this present world that they not be haughty, nor have their hope set on the uncertainty of riches, but on the living God, who richly provides us with everything to enjoy;","understanding":"τοῖς πλουσίοις ἐν τῷ νῦν αἰῶνι, “those who are rich in this present world” — αἰών, from the world and time families: ‘this present age’, which is passing. Their riches belong to an age that is ending. παράγγελλε, “Charge” them μὴ ὑψηλοφρονεῖν, “not be haughty” — high-minded, proud — μηδὲ ἠλπικέναι ἐπὶ πλούτου ἀδηλότητι, “nor have their hope set on the uncertainty of riches,” ἀλλ᾽ ἐπὶ θεῷ τῷ ζῶντι, “but on the living God, who richly (πλουσίως) provides us with everything to enjoy (ἀπόλαυσιν).” God is not stingy; He gives richly for enjoyment. The problem is trusting the gifts instead of the Giver.","words":[{"strongs":"G4145","hebrew":"πλούσιος","translit":"ploúsios","english":"rich"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G3853","hebrew":"παραγγέλλω","translit":"parangéllō","english":"Charge"},{"strongs":"G5309","hebrew":"ὑψηλοφρονέω","translit":"hypsēlophronéō","english":"haughty"},{"strongs":"G1679","hebrew":"ἐλπίζω","translit":"elpízō","english":"have their hope set"},{"strongs":"G83","hebrew":"ἀδηλότης","translit":"adēlótēs","english":"uncertainty"},{"strongs":"G4149","hebrew":"πλοῦτος","translit":"ploûtos","english":"riches"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"living"},{"strongs":"G3930","hebrew":"παρέχω","translit":"paréchō","english":"provides"},{"strongs":"G4146","hebrew":"πλουσίως","translit":"plousíōs","english":"richly"},{"strongs":"G619","hebrew":"ἀπόλαυσις","translit":"apólausis","english":"enjoy"}]},{"v":18,"text":"that they do good, that they be rich in good works, that they be ready to distribute, willing to share;","understanding":"Four commands for the rich: ἀγαθοεργεῖν, “that they do good” — ἀγαθός put into action; πλουτεῖν ἐν ἔργοις καλοῖς, “that they be rich in good works” — καλός: beautiful, noble deeds (both good-family words again, as in 5:10); εὐμεταδότους εἶναι, “ready to distribute” — generous; κοινωνικούς, “willing to share” — fellowship expressed in giving. True wealth is measured by generosity.","words":[{"strongs":"G14","hebrew":"ἀγαθοεργέω","translit":"agathoergéō","english":"do good"},{"strongs":"G4147","hebrew":"πλουτέω","translit":"ploutéō","english":"be rich"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G2130","hebrew":"εὐμετάδοτος","translit":"eumetádotos","english":"ready to distribute"},{"strongs":"G2843","hebrew":"κοινωνικός","translit":"koinōnikós","english":"willing to share"}]},{"v":19,"text":"laying up in store for themselves a good foundation against the time to come, that they may lay hold of eternal life.","understanding":"The result: ἀποθησαυρίζοντας ἑαυτοῖς θεμέλιον καλὸν εἰς τὸ μέλλον, “laying up in store for themselves a good foundation against the time to come” — echoing Jesus' ‘lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven’ (Matthew 6:20). ἵνα ἐπιλάβωνται τῆς αἰωνίου ζωῆς, “that they may lay hold of eternal life” (some manuscripts read ‘the life that is truly life’). Generosity is not a way to earn salvation but evidence of having grasped real life.","words":[{"strongs":"G597","hebrew":"ἀποθησαυρίζω","translit":"apothēsaurízō","english":"laying up in store"},{"strongs":"G2310","hebrew":"θεμέλιος","translit":"themélios","english":"foundation"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G3195","hebrew":"μέλλω","translit":"méllō","english":"to come"},{"strongs":"G1949","hebrew":"ἐπιλαμβάνομαι","translit":"epilambánomai","english":"lay hold"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"}]},{"v":20,"text":"Timothy, guard that which is committed to you, turning away from the empty chatter and oppositions of what is falsely called knowledge;","understanding":"ὦ Τιμόθεε, τὴν παραθήκην φύλαξον, “Timothy, guard that which is committed to you” — παραθήκη is a deposit entrusted to someone's safekeeping, like valuables left with a banker. The gospel is a deposit to be guarded, not altered. ἐκτρεπόμενος τὰς βεβήλους κενοφωνίας, “turning away from the empty chatter” — κενοφωνία, empty sounds — καὶ ἀντιθέσεις τῆς ψευδωνύμου γνώσεως, “and oppositions of what is falsely called knowledge” — γνῶσις from the know family, ‘knowledge’ falsely so called. Some later teachers called themselves ‘Gnostics’ from this word.","words":[{"strongs":"G5442","hebrew":"φυλάσσω","translit":"phylássō","english":"guard"},{"strongs":"G3866","hebrew":"παραθήκη","translit":"parathḗkē","english":"committed"},{"strongs":"G1624","hebrew":"ἐκτρέπω","translit":"ektrépō","english":"turning away"},{"strongs":"G952","hebrew":"βέβηλος","translit":"bébēlos","english":"profane"},{"strongs":"G2757","hebrew":"κενοφωνία","translit":"kenophōnía","english":"empty chatter"},{"strongs":"G477","hebrew":"ἀντίθεσις","translit":"antíthesis","english":"oppositions"},{"strongs":"G5581","hebrew":"ψευδώνυμος","translit":"pseudṓnymos","english":"falsely called"},{"strongs":"G1108","hebrew":"γνῶσις","translit":"gnōsis","english":"knowledge"}]},{"v":21,"text":"which some profess, and thus have wandered from the faith. Grace be with you. Amen.","understanding":"ἥν τινες ἐπαγγελλόμενοι, “which some profess” — claiming this ‘knowledge’ — περὶ τὴν πίστιν ἠστόχησαν, “and thus have wandered from the faith” — ἀστοχέω, missed the mark, the archer's word from 1:6. The letter ends where it began: some have missed the target. Then: ἡ χάρις μεθ᾽ ὑμῶν, “Grace be with you” — plural, ‘you all’: the letter was meant for the whole church.","words":[{"strongs":"G1861","hebrew":"ἐπαγγέλλω","translit":"epangéllō","english":"profess"},{"strongs":"G795","hebrew":"ἀστοχέω","translit":"astochéō","english":"wandered"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"Grace"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Shrouds Have No Pockets","verseRef":"v. 6-8","image":"Picture a newborn baby in a midwife's arms: naked, empty-handed, owning nothing. Now picture an old man on his deathbed in Ephesus. He spent his life accumulating land, silver and slaves. His relatives are already arguing in the next room about who will inherit what. He dies, and they wrap his body in a linen shroud. It has no pockets. Everything he gathered stays behind. Then picture a poor believer who has food, clothing and a heart at rest in God. She dies too — but she goes into eternity rich.","meaning":"Paul writes, “But godliness with contentment is great gain. For we brought nothing into the world, and we certainly can’t carry anything out. But having food and clothing, we will be content with that” (vv. 6–8). Contentment is not resignation; it is the settled peace of knowing God is enough. Jesus warned, “a man’s life doesn’t consist of the abundance of the things which he possesses” (Luke 12:15). The question is not how much you have, but whether you can be at rest with what God provides."},{"title":"The Self-Inflicted Wounds","verseRef":"v. 9-10","image":"Picture a man reaching desperately for a bag of gold hanging just out of reach on a wall of spikes. He stretches, strains and lunges. Each time he grabs at it, he is cut by the spikes around it. His arms bleed, his clothes tear, but he keeps reaching. Finally he gets the bag — and finds himself pierced in a dozen places, alone, his family gone, his faith abandoned, his peace destroyed. He did not mean to wound himself. He only wanted more.","meaning":"“But those who are determined to be rich fall into a temptation and a snare and many foolish and harmful lusts, such as drown men in ruin and destruction. For the love of money is a root of all kinds of evil. Some have been led astray from the faith in their greed, and have pierced themselves through with many sorrows” (vv. 9–10). Money itself is not evil; the love of it is a root from which many kinds of evil grow. Watch for the reaching heart, the restless stretching toward more."},{"title":"Flee, Pursue, Fight","verseRef":"v. 11-12","image":"Picture a young soldier on a battlefield. Behind him, enemies he must not engage are trying to lure him into an ambush: greed, quarrels, pride. He runs from them. Ahead of him is a prize, and he runs toward it with all his strength: righteousness, godliness, faith, love, endurance, gentleness. Then the real battle begins, and he plants his feet and fights — not against people, but for the faith entrusted to him, gripping eternal life with both hands. Behind him stand many witnesses who heard him confess Christ, and above him the King who once made the same good confession before Pilate.","meaning":"“But you, man of God, flee these things, and follow after righteousness, godliness, faith, love, perseverance, and gentleness. Fight the good fight of faith. Take hold of the eternal life to which you were called” (vv. 11–12). The Christian life has three verbs: flee, pursue, fight. Some things you must run from; some you must chase; and the faith itself you must defend with everything you have. Paul did it to the end: “I have fought the good fight. I have finished the course. I have kept the faith” (2 Timothy 4:7)."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Slaves and Masters (v. 1-2)","body":"Believing slaves should honour their masters so that God's name and the teaching are not slandered, and serve believing masters all the better because they are beloved brothers; Timothy must teach and urge these things."},{"label":"False Teachers and the Love of Money (v. 3-10)","body":"Anyone teaching a different doctrine is conceited and diseased with controversies that produce envy, strife and suspicion, imagining godliness is a means of gain; but godliness with contentment is great gain, since we bring nothing in and take nothing out; those who want to be rich fall into temptation and ruin, for the love of money is a root of all kinds of evil."},{"label":"The Charge to the Man of God (v. 11-16)","body":"Timothy must flee these things and pursue righteousness, godliness, faith, love, endurance and gentleness; fight the good fight of faith and take hold of eternal life; and keep the commandment unstained until the appearing of Christ, whom God will display in His time — the blessed and only Sovereign, King of kings and Lord of lords, immortal and dwelling in unapproachable light."},{"label":"A Charge to the Rich (v. 17-19)","body":"The rich must not be proud or trust uncertain riches but God, who richly provides everything to enjoy; they must do good, be rich in good works, generous and ready to share, storing up a good foundation for the future so that they take hold of real life."},{"label":"Guard the Deposit (v. 20-21)","body":"Timothy must guard what has been entrusted to him, avoiding godless chatter and the contradictions of what is falsely called knowledge, by which some have swerved from the faith. Grace be with you all."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Godliness Is Not a Business","paragraphs":["The final chapter begins with instructions to believing slaves (vv. 1–2). Slavery was woven into the Roman economy, and many early Christians were slaves. Paul tells them to honour their masters “that the name of God and the doctrine not be blasphemed” (v. 1). This must be read honestly: Paul is addressing how believers live faithfully within a system they could not change, not endorsing that system. In the same letter he lists ‘slave-traders’ among the lawless (1:10), and elsewhere he writes, “if you get an opportunity to become free, use it” (1 Corinthians 7:21), and tells masters to give their servants “that which is just and equal” (Colossians 4:1). The gospel's principle that “there is neither slave nor free man” (Galatians 3:28) would eventually undermine the institution itself.","Then Paul returns to the false teachers and exposes their roots. They do not agree with “sound words, the words of our Lord Jesus Christ, and to the doctrine which is according to godliness” (v. 3). Instead they are “conceited, knowing nothing, but obsessed with arguments, disputes, and word battles” (v. 4). The fruit of their teaching is ugly: envy, strife, insults, evil suspicions and constant friction. And their motive is money: they “suppose that godliness is a means of gain” (v. 5). Religion had become a business.","Paul takes their slogan and turns it inside out: “But godliness with contentment is great gain” (v. 6). Godliness is gain — but not financial gain. Combined with contentment, it makes a person truly rich. The reason is simple: “For we brought nothing into the world, and we certainly can’t carry anything out. But having food and clothing, we will be content with that” (vv. 7–8). Paul had learned this personally: “I have learned in whatever state I am, to be content in it” (Philippians 4:11). Hebrews adds the foundation of contentment: “Be free from the love of money, content with such things as you have, for he has said, ‘I will in no way leave you, neither will I in any way forsake you’” (Hebrews 13:5)."]},{"heading":"A Root of All Kinds of Evil","paragraphs":["Paul warns those who are not content: “But those who are determined to be rich fall into a temptation and a snare and many foolish and harmful lusts, such as drown men in ruin and destruction” (v. 9). The issue is the determination, the settled desire, to be rich. That desire becomes a trap. Then the famous verse: “For the love of money is a root of all kinds of evil. Some have been led astray from the faith in their greed, and have pierced themselves through with many sorrows” (v. 10). Notice what Paul does not say: money is not evil, and the love of money is not the only cause of every sin. But it is a root from which many kinds of evil grow.","Jesus taught the same: “No one can serve two masters … You can’t serve both God and Mammon” (Matthew 6:24), and “Beware! Keep yourselves from covetousness, for a man’s life doesn’t consist of the abundance of the things which he possesses” (Luke 12:15). The rich fool built bigger barns, but God said, “You foolish one, tonight your soul is required of you” (Luke 12:20). Greed does not only harm others; it pierces the greedy person with many sorrows.","Against this, Paul charges Timothy: “But you, man of God, flee these things, and follow after righteousness, godliness, faith, love, perseverance, and gentleness. Fight the good fight of faith. Take hold of the eternal life to which you were called” (vv. 11–12). Flee, pursue, fight. The strength for this comes from looking at Christ, “who before Pontius Pilate testified the good confession” (v. 13), and at His coming appearing. Paul bursts into worship of God, “the blessed and only Ruler, the King of kings, and Lord of lords; who alone has immortality, dwelling in unapproachable light” (vv. 15–16). Next to that King, money looks very small."]},{"heading":"Rich Toward God","paragraphs":["Not all the Ephesian believers were poor; some were wealthy. Paul does not tell them to give everything away, but he charges them: “Charge those who are rich in this present world that they not be haughty, nor have their hope set on the uncertainty of riches, but on the living God, who richly provides us with everything to enjoy” (v. 17). Two dangers attend wealth: pride and false security. And there is a beautiful truth here: God “richly provides us with everything to enjoy.” He is not a miser. The problem is not enjoying His gifts but trusting them instead of Him.","Then the positive command: “that they do good, that they be rich in good works, that they be ready to distribute, willing to share; laying up in store for themselves a good foundation against the time to come, that they may lay hold of eternal life” (vv. 18–19). The rich are to become rich in a different currency — good works and generosity. This echoes Jesus: “but lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust consume” (Matthew 6:20), and His warning about the one who “lays up treasure for himself, and is not rich toward God” (Luke 12:21).","The letter closes with a final charge: “Timothy, guard that which is committed to you, turning away from the empty chatter and oppositions of what is falsely called knowledge; which some profess, and thus have wandered from the faith” (vv. 20–21). The gospel is a deposit entrusted to Timothy, to be guarded, not improved. The false teachers claimed special knowledge; Paul calls it ‘falsely called knowledge’. Some ‘missed the mark’ — the same archer's word as 1:6. The letter ends where it began: guard the truth, aim at love and godliness. And the final word is grace: “Grace be with you.”"]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Money, Contentment and the King of Kings","paragraphs":["Jesus' teaching on money underlies this chapter. “Don’t lay up treasures for yourselves on the earth, where moth and rust consume, and where thieves break through and steal” (Matthew 6:19). “How difficult it is for those who have riches to enter into God’s Kingdom!” (Mark 10:23). And Paul himself reminded the Ephesian elders of Jesus' words: “It is more blessed to give than to receive” (Acts 20:35). Contentment is possible because God has promised, “I will in no way leave you, neither will I in any way forsake you” (Hebrews 13:5).","The ‘good confession’ of Jesus before Pilate appears in the Gospels: “Pilate therefore said to him, ‘Are you a king then?’ Jesus answered, ‘You say that I am a king. For this reason I have been born, and for this reason I have come into the world, that I should testify to the truth’” (John 18:37). Timothy's confession of faith follows the pattern of his Lord's.","The doxology of verses 15–16 resonates with Revelation, where the Lamb “is Lord of lords, and King of kings” (Revelation 17:14) and bears the name “KING OF KINGS, AND LORD OF LORDS” (Revelation 19:16). And the invisibility of God is explained in John: “No one has seen God at any time. The one and only Son, who is in the bosom of the Father, he has declared him” (John 1:18). The God who dwells in unapproachable light has made Himself known in Christ."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Contentment / sufficiency","hebrew":"αὐτάρκεια","translit":"autárkeia","strongs":"G841","verses":"v. 6","insight":"Strong's: “self-satisfaction, i.e. (abstractly) contentedness, or (concretely) a competence.” The Stoic philosophers used it for self-sufficiency, needing nothing from outside. Paul transforms it: Christian contentment is not self-sufficiency but God-sufficiency. The related adjective appears in Philippians 4:11: ‘I have learned in whatever state I am, to be content in it.’ Paul uses the noun in 2 Corinthians 9:8 of God giving all sufficiency in all things. Contentment is a learned trust that God is enough."},{"english":"Love of money","hebrew":"φιλαργυρία","translit":"philargyría","strongs":"G5365","verses":"v. 10","insight":"Strong's: “avarice.” Literally ‘love of silver’ (φίλος + ἄργυρος). It appears only here in the New Testament; the related adjective describes the Pharisees as ‘lovers of money’ (Luke 16:14). Paul does not say money is evil, but that the love of it is a root of all kinds of evils. Money is a good servant and a terrible master. The test is not how much you have, but what you love and trust."},{"english":"Fight / strive / contend","hebrew":"ἀγωνίζομαι","translit":"agōnízomai","strongs":"G75","verses":"v. 12","insight":"Strong's: “to struggle, literally (to compete for a prize), figuratively (to contend with an adversary), or genitive case (to endeavor to accomplish something).” It gives us ‘agonise’. Paul uses it of Epaphras wrestling in prayer (Colossians 4:12) and of his own ministry (Colossians 1:29). ‘Fight the good fight of faith’ pictures an athlete or soldier giving full effort. The Christian life is not passive drifting; it is an all-out contest for faith, with eternal life as the prize already given and to be grasped."},{"english":"Ruler / sovereign","hebrew":"δυνάστης","translit":"dynástēs","strongs":"G1413","verses":"v. 15","insight":"Strong's: “a ruler or officer.” From δύναμαι, to be able, related to δύναμις in the power family: one who holds power. It is used of the Ethiopian court official (Acts 8:27) and of rulers brought down by God (Luke 1:52). Here God is ‘the blessed and only Ruler’: every other ruler holds power temporarily and on loan. In a city where the emperor claimed divine honours, Paul confesses that there is only one true Sovereign."},{"english":"Deposit / trust committed","hebrew":"παραθήκη","translit":"parathḗkē","strongs":"G3866","verses":"v. 20","insight":"Strong's: “a deposit, i.e. (figuratively) trust.” It describes valuables entrusted to someone for safekeeping, to be returned intact. The gospel is not Timothy's to change; it is a deposit from God to be guarded faithfully. Paul uses the same word twice in 2 Timothy (1:12, 14). Every generation of believers receives the same deposit and must hand it on unchanged. Guarding it means both holding to it and living it."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: your relationship with money, the direction you are running, and the treasure you are guarding.","items":[{"title":"What Are You Reaching For?","body":"The love of money often hides behind good reasons: security, family, opportunity. The Spirit asks: what are you reaching for? Is there a restless desire for more that is pulling you away from faith, peace or generosity? Ask Him to expose any love of money in your heart and to teach you contentment — the settled trust that, with food and clothing and God's presence, you have enough."},{"title":"Flee, Pursue, Fight","body":"Paul gives Timothy three verbs. The Spirit may be showing you something to flee — a temptation you keep flirting with. He may be calling you to pursue a virtue you have neglected — gentleness, endurance, love. And He may be calling you to fight for your faith in a season of pressure or doubt. Ask Him which verb is most urgent for you this week, and act on it."},{"title":"Rich Toward God","body":"If you have more than you need, the Spirit is not condemning you, but He is calling you: do good, be rich in good works, be generous and ready to share. God richly provides everything for your enjoyment, and also so that you can bless others. Ask Him where your generosity should flow this month, and lay up treasure that lasts."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Contentment Check","body":"Finish this sentence honestly: ‘I would be content if I had …’ If the answer is more than food, clothing and God (v. 8), ask God to show you where your heart is reaching too far."},{"title":"The Root Check","body":"Look at your spending, saving and giving over the last month. Do they reveal love of money, or trust in God (vv. 9–10, 17)? Name one change you will make."},{"title":"The Deposit Check","body":"What has God entrusted to you — the gospel, a family, a ministry, gifts, resources (v. 20)? Are you guarding these faithfully? Write down one way you will guard or use one of them better."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"A Generosity Experiment","body":"For one month, practise verses 18–19. Set aside a specific amount — larger than feels comfortable — to give away. Look for needs: a struggling family, a missionary, a widow, a ministry. Give quietly. Also give time: do one practical good work each week. At the end of the month, write down what God taught you about contentment, trust and joy through giving."},{"title":"Learn the Doxology","body":"Memorise verses 15–16: “the blessed and only Ruler, the King of kings, and Lord of lords; who alone has immortality, dwelling in unapproachable light; whom no man has seen, nor can see: to whom be honor and eternal power. Amen.” Speak it aloud each morning for a week, especially when you feel anxious about money or the future. Let worship of the true Sovereign shrink the power of wealth over your heart."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Blessed and only Sovereign, King of kings and Lord of lords, You alone have immortality and dwell in unapproachable light. Let my life honour You and not bring blasphemy on Your name. Keep me from conceit, quarrels over words, envy, strife and evil suspicion. Keep me from ever treating godliness as a means of gain. Teach me that godliness with contentment is great gain. I brought nothing into the world and I will take nothing out; with food and clothing and You, I have enough. Uproot every love of money in me, and keep me from the snares and sorrows of greed. Make me flee these things and pursue righteousness, godliness, faith, love, endurance and gentleness. Help me fight the good fight of faith and take hold of eternal life. Keep me faithful, unstained and above reproach until the appearing of my Lord Jesus Christ. Where You have given me more than I need, make me humble, hopeful in You alone, rich in good works, generous and ready to share. Help me guard the gospel You have entrusted to me. Grace be with me and all Your people. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Rich Fool","ref":"Luke 12:13-21","relatedVerses":"v. 7-10, 17","summary":"A man asked Jesus to make his brother share an inheritance. Jesus refused and warned, “Beware! Keep yourselves from covetousness, for a man’s life doesn’t consist of the abundance of the things which he possesses” (Luke 12:15). Then He told of a rich man whose land produced a huge harvest. He decided to tear down his barns and build bigger ones, and said to himself that he could take his ease, eat, drink and be merry for many years. “But God said to him, ‘You foolish one, tonight your soul is required of you. The things which you have prepared—whose will they be?’” (Luke 12:20). Jesus concluded: “So is he who lays up treasure for himself, and is not rich toward God” (Luke 12:21). 1 Timothy 6 gives the same warning and the same remedy."},{"title":"Jesus' Good Confession Before Pilate","ref":"John 18:33-38","relatedVerses":"v. 13","summary":"Pilate summoned Jesus and asked if He was the King of the Jews. Jesus answered that His kingdom was not of this world; if it were, His servants would fight. “Pilate therefore said to him, ‘Are you a king then?’ Jesus answered, ‘You say that I am a king. For this reason I have been born, and for this reason I have come into the world, that I should testify to the truth. Everyone who is of the truth listens to my voice’” (John 18:37). Pilate asked, ‘What is truth?’ and went out. Facing death, Jesus confessed the truth calmly and faithfully. Paul points Timothy to this ‘good confession’ as the model for his own unwavering witness."},{"title":"Zacchaeus Gives Half His Goods","ref":"Luke 19:1-10","relatedVerses":"v. 17-19","summary":"Zacchaeus was a chief tax collector in Jericho, rich and despised. When Jesus came through, he climbed a sycamore tree to see Him, and Jesus called him by name and invited Himself to his house. People grumbled that Jesus was going to be the guest of a sinner. But Zacchaeus stood and said he would give half his goods to the poor, and if he had cheated anyone, he would repay four times as much. Jesus declared that salvation had come to his house, because the Son of Man came to seek and to save the lost. Zacchaeus shows what happens when a rich man stops hoping in uncertain riches and puts his hope in God: he becomes rich in good works, ready to share."}]}],"review":{"type":"finalReview","book":"1 Timothy","titleEmojis":"🏛️🛡️","bigPicture":{"subtitle":"Order in God's House, Godliness in God's People","thesis":"If we had to summarize the Book of 1 Timothy in one overarching thought, it is this: because Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners, His church — the household of the living God and the pillar and foundation of the truth — must guard that truth, pray for all people, be led by people of tested character, care for its vulnerable, and pursue godliness with contentment rather than gain, so that the gospel is displayed in a watching world.","paragraphs":["Start with the man, because Paul did. Timothy was young for his task, perhaps timid, often unwell, and left in charge of a troubled church in a proud, wealthy, religious city. False teachers had arisen from within the church itself, spinning myths and genealogies, arguing about words, forbidding marriage and certain foods, and treating godliness as a way to make money. Paul writes to strengthen his ‘true child in faith’ (1:2) for the battle: to silence false teaching, set the church in order and keep himself and his teaching faithful.","But the letter never loses sight of the gospel. In the first chapter Paul declares, “The saying is faithful and worthy of all acceptance, that Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners; of whom I am chief” (1:15). In the second, “For there is one God, and one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus” (2:5). In the third, “the mystery of godliness is great: God was revealed in the flesh” (3:16). The instructions about prayer, leaders, widows and money are not a rulebook; they are what the gospel looks like when it shapes a community.","The purpose of the letter is stated at its centre: “that you may know how men ought to behave themselves in God’s house, which is the assembly of the living God, the pillar and ground of the truth” (3:15). God's household needs order: prayer for all people, worship free of anger and display, overseers and deacons of proven character, a young leader who is an example, wise care for widows, honour and accountability for elders, right attitudes toward masters and money. Each of these protects and displays the truth.","And the key word is godliness. The false teachers had religion without godliness — controversy, pride and greed. Paul calls Timothy to train himself for the real thing: “For bodily exercise has some value, but godliness has value in all things, having the promise of the life which is now, and of that which is to come” (4:8). The letter ends with a warning and a charge: “For the love of money is a root of all kinds of evil” (6:10), so “Fight the good fight of faith. Take hold of the eternal life to which you were called” (6:12), and “guard that which is committed to you” (6:20)."]},"map":{"intro":"1 Timothy is the fifteenth book of the New Testament and the first of Paul's three ‘pastoral letters’, written probably in the early to mid 60s AD after his first Roman imprisonment, to Timothy, whom he had left in Ephesus. It is followed by 2 Timothy and Titus, which share its concerns for sound teaching, godly leadership and church order.","sections":[{"title":"From Letters to Churches to Letters to Leaders","body":"Romans through 2 Thessalonians were written to churches. 1 Timothy begins the letters written to individual leaders (with 2 Timothy, Titus and Philemon). The voice changes from ‘you all’ to ‘you’ — a spiritual father writing to his son. Yet the letter was meant to be read aloud (‘Grace be with you’ is plural in 6:21). The church overhears what Paul expects of its leaders, and leaders learn what the church needs from them."},{"title":"The Household of God","body":"No New Testament letter develops the picture of the church as a household more fully. The church is God's οἶκος (3:15), with the living God as Father. Leaders must manage their own households well (3:4–5, 12). Members relate as fathers, mothers, brothers and sisters (5:1–2). Widows are cared for first by their own families, then by the church family (5:4, 16). Slaves and masters who believe are brothers (6:2). The structure of 1 Timothy is family order shaped by the gospel."},{"title":"Words That Need Care","body":"1 Timothy contains passages that believers interpret differently: women learning and teaching (2:11–15), the qualifications ‘husband of one wife’ (3:2) and ‘the women’ (3:11), the list of widows (5:3–16), and instructions to slaves (6:1–2). This study has tried to give the strongest textual reading — noting, for example, that Paul grounds 2:12–14 in creation and the fall, and that he condemns slave-traders even while instructing enslaved believers — and to say honestly where uncertainty remains. Read these passages humbly, in the light of the whole letter's gospel heart."}],"roadAhead":{"intro":"The Road Ahead: 1 Timothy ended with a charge to “guard that which is committed to you” (6:20). The next two books continue the pastoral letters: 2 Timothy, Paul's final letter, hands the deposit to Timothy one last time before Paul's death; Titus applies the same principles to a young church on the island of Crete.","books":[{"book":"2 Timothy","connection":"Some years later Paul writes to Timothy again — this time from a cold Roman prison, knowing his death is near: “For I am already being offered, and the time of my departure has come” (2 Timothy 4:6). The themes of 1 Timothy return with deeper urgency. The ‘deposit’ must be guarded: “That good thing which was committed to you, guard through the Holy Spirit who dwells in us” (2 Timothy 1:14), and passed on: “The things which you have heard from me among many witnesses, commit the same to faithful men, who will be able to teach others also” (2 Timothy 2:2). The young man told not to let anyone despise his youth is now told not to be ashamed: “Therefore don’t be ashamed of the testimony of our Lord, nor of me his prisoner; but endure hardship for the Good News according to the power of God” (2 Timothy 1:8). And the ‘good fight’ of 1 Timothy 6:12 becomes Paul's own epitaph: “I have fought the good fight. I have finished the course. I have kept the faith” (2 Timothy 4:7). Watch for how a dying apostle hands on the faith."},{"book":"Titus","connection":"Titus was another of Paul's trusted co-workers, left on the island of Crete: “I left you in Crete for this reason, that you would set in order the things that were lacking, and appoint elders in every city, as I directed you” (Titus 1:5). His task parallels Timothy's in Ephesus: appoint elders of proven character, silence false teachers and teach sound doctrine. Like 1 Timothy, Titus links truth with godliness: Paul writes of “the knowledge of the truth which is according to godliness” (Titus 1:1). And it contains one of the most beautiful summaries of grace: “For the grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation to all men, instructing us to the intent that, denying ungodliness and worldly lusts, we would live soberly, righteously, and godly in this present age” (Titus 2:11–12). Watch for how grace itself trains believers in godliness."}]}},"themesMastered":[{"title":"The Goal Is Love","anchorRef":"1 Timothy 1:5","body":"“but the goal of this command is love, out of a pure heart and a good conscience and sincere faith” (1:5). All sound teaching aims at love. The false teachers ‘missed the mark’ and turned to empty talk; their speculation produced arguments, not love. The test of what you learn is what it produces in you: love flowing from a clean heart, an honest conscience and faith without pretence."},{"title":"Mercy for the Chief of Sinners","anchorRef":"1 Timothy 1:15","body":"“The saying is faithful and worthy of all acceptance, that Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners; of whom I am chief” (1:15). Paul never stopped marvelling that a blasphemer and persecutor had received mercy. His life became an example of Christ's perfect patience. Sound doctrine begins here: no one is too bad for grace, and no one is too good to need it."},{"title":"One God, One Mediator, Prayer for All","anchorRef":"1 Timothy 2:5","body":"“For there is one God, and one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus” (2:5), who gave Himself as a ransom for all. Because God desires all people to be saved, the church prays for all people — petitions, prayers, intercessions and thanksgiving — including rulers and opponents. No other go-between is needed or possible."},{"title":"The Household of God, Pillar of the Truth","anchorRef":"1 Timothy 3:15-16","body":"The church is “God’s house, which is the assembly of the living God, the pillar and ground of the truth” (3:15). It holds up the mystery of godliness: Christ revealed in the flesh and taken up in glory (3:16). That is why its leaders must be tested in character, its members must relate as family, its widows must be honoured, its elders supported and held accountable, and its teaching guarded."},{"title":"Godliness With Contentment","anchorRef":"1 Timothy 6:6","body":"“But godliness with contentment is great gain” (6:6). Godliness (εὐσέβεια), the key word of the letter, is reverent devotion to God lived out in daily life, and it must be trained for like an athlete (4:7–8). Its great enemy in Ephesus was the love of money, “a root of all kinds of evil” (6:10). The man of God flees greed, pursues righteousness and godliness, fights the good fight of faith, and if rich, becomes rich in good works."}],"experientialLesson":{"intro":"How do you walk out the Book of 1 Timothy practically moving forward? Here is your experiential blueprint:","steps":[{"step":1,"title":"Aim Your Faith at Love","lesson":"The Lesson: “but the goal of this command is love, out of a pure heart and a good conscience and sincere faith” (1:5). Knowledge that does not produce love has missed the target.","practice":"The Practice: For the next month, after each time you study Scripture or hear teaching, write one sentence answering: ‘How should this make me more loving, and toward whom?’ Then do one concrete act of love based on it within twenty-four hours. Track it in a notebook. At the end of the month, read back through and notice how your learning has been turning into love."},{"step":2,"title":"Pray for All, Starting With Leaders","lesson":"The Lesson: “I exhort therefore, first of all, that petitions, prayers, intercessions, and givings of thanks, be made for all men” (2:1), because God desires all to be saved and there is one Mediator for all.","practice":"The Practice: Make a weekly prayer list with seven categories, one for each day: national leaders, local leaders, church leaders, family, neighbours, people who oppose you, and people who do not yet know Christ. Under each, write two or three names. Pray through the list each week for three months, using all four kinds of prayer. Note any answers and any change in your attitude toward those people."},{"step":3,"title":"Train for Godliness and Practise Contentment","lesson":"The Lesson: “Exercise yourself toward godliness” (4:7), because “godliness with contentment is great gain” (6:6), while the love of money pierces people with many sorrows.","practice":"The Practice: Set up a simple godliness training plan with one daily practice (Scripture and prayer), one weekly practice (an act of service or hospitality) and one monthly practice (a deliberate act of generosity beyond your comfort). At the same time, practise contentment: for thirty days, before every purchase that is not a need, pause and ask, ‘Am I content without this?’ Give thanks for what you have. Write down what changes in your heart."}]},"finalDeclaration":"“Father, I close this letter to a young pastor in a troubled city, and I declare: the goal of all I learn is love, out of a pure heart, a good conscience and sincere faith. I refuse myths, endless speculation and different doctrine. Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners, and I am one of them; I have received mercy, and His patience is displayed in me. To the King eternal, immortal, invisible, the only God, be honour and glory for ever. I will pray for all people — for rulers and for those who oppose me — because You desire all to be saved and to come to the knowledge of the truth. There is one God and one Mediator between God and humanity, the man Christ Jesus, who gave Himself as a ransom for all. I belong to Your household, the church of the living God, the pillar and foundation of the truth. Great is the mystery of godliness: Christ was revealed in the flesh, vindicated by the Spirit, seen by angels, proclaimed among the nations, believed on in the world, and taken up in glory. Everything You created is good, and I receive it with thanksgiving. I will train myself for godliness, which holds promise for this life and the life to come. Whatever my age, I will be an example in speech, conduct, love, faith and purity. I will honour my family, care for the vulnerable, respect those who labour in the word, and judge without favouritism. Godliness with contentment is great gain; I brought nothing into the world and will take nothing out. I flee the love of money, and I pursue righteousness, godliness, faith, love, endurance and gentleness. I will fight the good fight of faith and take hold of eternal life. I will be rich in good works, generous and ready to share. I will guard the gospel You have entrusted to me until the appearing of my Lord Jesus Christ, the blessed and only Sovereign, the King of kings and Lord of lords. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”"}});
